2 CONT     * Magnus Lagabøtes Landslov og Bylov utgitt i Norges gamle Love bind 2
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus VI of Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus Lagabøte (old Norse Magnús lagabœtir, English Magnus the law-mender) or Magnus Håkonsson (old Norse Magnús Hákonarson) (1238 - 9 May 1280), was king of Norway from 1263 until 1280.
2 CONT
2 CONT Knut Helle (1974). Norge blir en stat, 1130-1319, pp. 134-146. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus Lagabøte (old Norse Magnús lagabœtir, English Magnus the law-mender) or Magnus Håkonsson (old Norse Magnús Hákonarson) (1238 - 9 May 1280), was king of Norway from 1263 until 1280.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the youngest son of king Håkon Håkonsson and his wife Margaret Skuladotter. He was born in Tunsberg and was baptised in May 1238. He spent most of his upbringing in Bergen. In 1257 his older brother Håkon Håkonsson the Youn
2 CONC g died, leaving Magnus the heir-apparent to the kingdom. His father gave him the title of king the same year. On 11 September 1261, he married the Danish princess Ingeborg, the daughter of the late Danish king Erik Plogpenning, af
2 CONC ter she was practically abducted by king Håkon's men from the monastery she was living in. The struggle to claim Ingeborg's inheritance from her murdered father later involved Norway in intermittent conflicts with Denmark for deca
2 CONC des to come. Magnus and Ingeborg were crowned directly after their marriage, and Magnus was given Ryfylke for his personal upkeep. On 16 December 1263 king Håkon died while fighting the Scottish king over the Hebrides, and Magnus 
2 CONC became the ruler of Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus' rule brought about a change from the somewhat aggressive foreign policy of his father. In 1266 he gave up the Hebrides and the Isle of Man to Scotland, in return for a large sum of silver and a yearly payment, under the Tr
2 CONC eaty of Perth, by which the Scots at the same time recognised Norwegian rule in the Orkney Islands. In 1269 the Treaty of Winchester cemented good relations with the English king Henry III. Magnus also seems to have had good relat
2 CONC ions with the Swedish king Valdemar Birgersson, and in the 1260s, the border with Sweden was officially defined for the first time. When Valdemar was deposed by his two brothers and fled to Norway in 1275, this stirred Magnus into
2 CONC  gathering a leidang-fleet for the first and only time in his reign. With a large fleet, he met with the new Swedish king Magnus Ladulås to try to bring about a settlement between the two brothers, but without success, Magnus of S
2 CONC weden would not give in to pressure and the Magnus of Norway retreated without engaging in hostile actions.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Internal policies
2 CONT
2 CONT In internal politics, Magnus carried out a great effort to modernise the law-code, which gave him his epithet law-mender. In 1274 he promulgated the new national law, a unified code of laws to apply for the whole country, includin
2 CONC g the Faroe islands and Shetland. This replaced the different regional laws which had existed before. It was supplemented by a new municipal law (a law for the cities) in 1276, and a slightly modified version was also drawn up for
2 CONC  Iceland. A unified code of laws for a whole country was at this time something quite new, which had until then only been introduced in Sicily and Castile. His code introduced the concept that crime is an offense against the state
2 CONC  rather than against the individual and thus narrowed the possibilities of personal vengeance. It increased the formal power of the king, making the throne the source of justice. The municipal law gave the cities increased freedom
2 CONC  from rural control. A specific section fixed the law of succession to the throne, in accordance with the arrangements laid down by king Håkon Håkonsson in 1260. The royal succession was an important and prickly matter, the last o
2 CONC f the civil wars, fought for decades over disputed successions to the throne, having finally ended only in 1240. In 1273 Magnus gave his eldest son, five-year-old Eirik, the name of king, and his younger brother Håkon the title of
2 CONC  duke, thus making unequivocally clear what the royal succession would be.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Magnus was by all accounts a personally very pious king, his work with the law-codes brought him into conflict with the archbishop, who resisted temporal authority over the church, and sought to preserve the churches infl
2 CONC uence over the kingdom. In 1277, the Settlement of Tønsberg settled the conflict, with both sides compromising. The church preserved considerable independence in judicial matters, but gave up its old claim that the Norwegian kingd
2 CONC om was a fief under the ultimate authority of the Catholic church.
2 CONT
2 CONT In cultural terms Magnus continued his father's policy of introducing European courtly culture to Norway. In 1277 he replaced the old Norse titles lendmann and skutilsvein with the European titles baron and riddar (knight), at the
2 CONC  same time giving them certain extra privileges and the right to be addressed as lord (herra). Magnus is probably also the first Norwegian king to have named himself using an ordinal number - he called himself Magnus IV. Immediate
2 CONC ly after his father's death, he commissioned the Icelander Sturla Þórðarson to write his father's saga, or biography. In 1278, he commissioned the same man to write his own saga. The saga of Magnus the lawmender thus became the la
2 CONC st of the medieval Norwegian kings' sagas, unfortunately only a short fragment of it has been preserved.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Death and aftermath
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1280, Magnus fell ill in Bergen, and died 9 May. He had already planned to have his son Eirik crowned at midsummer as co-ruler, instead Eirik now took over as sole king at the age of 12. Real power fell to a circl
2 CONC e of advisors, prominent among them Magnus' queen Ingeborg. Magnus was remembered as a good ruler, who ruled by law rather than by the sword. Some modern historians have considered him a weak king, for giving up the Hebrides and g
2 CONC iving in to demands of the church, but others consider these wise policies, sparing the kingdom unnecessary and unfruitful wars abroad, while preserving stability at home. Magnus was buried in the church of the Franciscan monaster
2 CONC y in Bergen, which is since the 16th century the cathedral of Bergen.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnus_VI_of_Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus VI Håkonsson Lagaböte af Norge (1238 - 1280)  Äktenskap med Ingegorg Eriksdotter af Danmark(ca. 1244-1287) år 1261 i Bergen, Norge (Källa: Capppelen´s Norges Historie) Hon var dotter till Konung Erik IV Valdemarsson Plogpen
2 CONC ning av Danmark (1241-1250 och Drottning Jutta av Sacksen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans anfader var Harald Hårfager av Ynglingaätten. Han är anfader till många svenska, danska och sachsiska kungar och där finns även nutida ättlingar. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus Lagabøte var Norges konge fra 1263, til sin død i 1280.
2 CONT
2 CONT Historisk ble han kalt Magnus VI Lagabøte av Norge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus (Håkonsson) Lagaböter, Magnús Lagabœtir, född 1 maj 1238, död 9 maj 1280, kung av Norge från december 1263 till sin död. Son till Håkon Håkonsson. Gift med Ingeborg Eriksdotter 1261. Barn [redigera]
2 CONT
