2 CONT Bild: Folkungaättens vapensköld
2 CONT
2 CONT Bengt Snivil eller Snivel, svensk högreståndsperson som ska ha levt på 1100-talet, men endast känd från 1400-talsskrifter. Enligt Saxo var han son till Folke den tjocke och prinsessan Ingegärd Knutsdotter av Danmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barn:
2 CONT
2 CONT Birger Bengtsson (känd som Birger Brosa) med Sigrid Lakman Magnus Bengtsson (Magnus Minnisköld) med Sigrid Lakman Karl Bengtsson (känd som Karl Döve)  Ulfhild Björnsdotter
2 CONT
2 CONT Folkungaätten är den etablerade benämningen på en medeltida svensk kungasläkt som också ibland i litteraturen kallas för Bjälboätten. Till Folkungaätten räknas en del av de personer som agnatiskt härstammade från Bengt Snivil. Han
2 CONC  hade tre kända söner, Birger, Magnus och Karl.
2 CONT
2 CONT Släkten brukar indelas i ett antal grenar och med Folkungaätten avses i allmänhet den gren som utgick från Birger Jarl och i ett antal generationer från och med Birgers son Valdemar Birgersson kom att inneha kungamakten. Gällande 
2 CONC de andra grenarna, se länkar nedan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Namnet Folkungaätten menas ibland vara efter en Folke Filbyter, vilken emellertid inte är en historisk person. Beteckningen infördes 1616 av Johannes Messenius som hämtade uppgifterna från en i senare avskrift bevarad 1400-talskäl
2 CONC la. Enligt denna källa härstammade både Birger Jarl och de av hans politiska motståndare som kallades folkungar från personer med namnet Folke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Den ofta förekommande uppgiften att "folkungar" närmast skulle vara en "partibeteckning" framfördes först av Rolf Pipping som hans personliga uppfattning och senare gav Erik Lönnroth Pipping sitt stöd. Uppfattningen bygger på jämf
2 CONC örelser med norska förhållanden och de där förekommande beteckningarna "kuvlungar" och "ribbungar" m fl.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beteckningen Bjälboätten efter ättens stamgods Bjälbo i Östergötland kunde eventuellt vara en mer adekvat benämning för släkten eftersom Bjälbo är det tidigaste kända godset i dess ägo; dock är Folkungaätten sedan länge etablerat 
2 CONC i historievetenskaplig, särskilt medeltidsgenealogisk, litteratur. Valet av beteckning är en historievetenskaplig konvention och som sådan en stipulativ definition. Den kan alltså varken vara rätt eller fel, bara mer eller mindre 
2 CONC adekvat. Det som tvingar fram en dylik konvention är att släkten i fråga själv inte kallade sig någonting alls.
2 CONT
2 CONT Folkungaätten dog ut i slutet av 1300-talet med Erik Valdemarsson (d.y.).
2 CONT
2 CONT  Källor  [redigera] Svenskt biografiskt lexikon  Äldre svenska frälsesläkter 
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: Europeische Stammtafeln (Vol. 1-16) Author: Schwennicke, Detlev Publication: Marburg, Germany: Verlag von J. A. Stargardt, 1980- Page: 3:162 
2 CONT
2 CONT House of Bjelbo (also known as House of Bjälbo, Bjälboätten in Swedish) was an Ostrogothian Swedish family that provided for several medieval Swedish bishops, jarls and kings. The house was later erroneously also referred to as th
2 CONC e House of Folkung (Folkungaätten).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide] 1 Origins  2 Jarls and bishops  3 Rise to royalty  4 Unclarities in genealogy  5 See also  6 References 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Origins The dynastic name "Bjelbo" comes from the family's oldest known manor, which was located in Östergötland, outside of Skänninge in the present-day commune of Mjölby.[2] The name "Bjelbo" was given to the family in la
2 CONC ter times, as family names were not used in the Middle Ages. Family's legendary forefather was Folke Filbyter.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Jarls and bishops
2 CONT
2 CONT Coat of arms of the House of Bjelbo.House of Bjelbo produced most of the jarls in Sweden in the 11th, 12th and 13th centuries until the title was abolished in 1266. Different branches of the family were often rivals for the office
2 CONC  of jarl. Most of the kings during that time were also from Östergötland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1100, Folke the Fat became the first known Bjelbo jarl, and probably the first jarl of all Sweden, under king Inge I of Sweden. He was married to a daughter of king Canute IV of Denmark. According to legends, he was the fir
2 CONC st of his family so elevated. Nothing is known of any of Folke's collateral relatives, though it is well-established that several of his sons' descendants were important lords.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other notable jarls from the family were Birger Brosa, Charles the Deaf, Ulf Fase, and Birger jarl. In the early 13th century, some members of the family moved to Norway, and held the office of jarl there. Noteworthy is that regar
2 CONC dless of the ruling royal family, Bjelbos continued to hold the position of the jarl in the kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several members of the family were also Bishops of Linköping at least in the 13th century. Diocese covered the area of Östergötland. Bishop of Linköping was often involved in the eastern activities.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Rise to royalty Valdemar, a son of Birger jarl, was elected as the King of Sweden in 1250. House of Bjelbo had gradually married to all rival royal dynasties in Sweden eventually producing an heir related to them all. When 
2 CONC the previous king Eric had died without an heir apparent, his sister's son, and also Birger's son, was the most suitable option to hold the royal office.
2 CONT
2 CONT The male line reigned in Sweden until 1364. During 1320-1387, the members of the family were also kings in Norway. Almost all subsequent monarchs of Sweden, Norway and Denmark descend from the House of Bjelbo through female lines.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Unclarities in genealogy Particularly, several persons conventionally assigned as sons and grandsons to Benedict Snivel, son of Folke the Fat, may actually be related to him in a different way. Conventional assignations str
2 CONC etch two generations to cover a hundred years, which is probably not realistic, and three generations over 150 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Bengt Snivels FOLKESON av Flandern (16830). Han var tydligvis oppkalt etter sin mors farbor, den prins Bengt som falt ved kong Knuts side i 1086. He was married to Ulvhilde BJØRNSDTR av Danmark. (16831
