2 CONC galand to be fostered at Bjarkey by Vidkun Jonson"[421].  Morkinskinna names “Magnus” as the son of King Sigurd and his concubine “Borghildr, the daughter of Óláfr of Dalr”, adding that he was “raised in his youth north on Bjarkey
2 CONC  with Vidkunnr Jónsson”[422].  '''He succeeded his father in 1130 as MAGNUS "Blinde/the Blind" King of Norway, deposed 1135.'''  Snorre records that Magnus was proclaimed king on the death of his father, commenting that he was "a 
2 CONC great drinker, greedy of money, hard and obstinate", but that he was opposed by Harald "Gille" although an agreement was reached 3 Oct 1130 to divide the country between them[423].  Snorre records that King Magnus was taken prison
2 CONC er by King Harald's forces, who later blinded him, cut off one of his feet and castrated him[424].  In a later passage, he recounts that Magnus "went north to Nidaros…went into the cloister on the holm and assumed the monk's dress
2 CONC "[425].  Snorre records that Sigurd "Slembidjakn", after murdering King Harald, took ex-king Magnus from his monastery "to make his cause appear better" but that, after his forces were defeated, Magnus fled "eastward to Gautland a
2 CONC nd then to Denmark" where he was well received by King Erik "Emun" who agreed to invade Norway[426].  Snorre describes how ex-king Magnus was killed at Hvalar, near Holm the Grey, the day after Martinmas[427].  '''married (1133, r
2 CONC epudiated 1133) KIRSTIN Knudsdatter of Denmark, daughter of KNUT "Lavard" Duke of South Jutland/Sønderjylland [Schleswig] and King of the Obotriten & his wife Ingeborg Mstislavna of Kiev ([1118]-).'''  Snorre refers to the wife of
2 CONC  King Magnus as "a daughter of Knut Lavard…sister of the Danish King Valdemar" and records that her husband sent her back to Denmark[428].  Morkinskinna records that King Magnus married “Kirstín, the daughter of Knút lávardr and t
2 CONC he sister of the Danish king Váldimarr” but adds that he sent her back to Denmark[429]. 
