1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Ragnhild Skoftesdotter Giske, Ragnhildr Skoptisdóttir==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Father	Skofte 
2 CONT * Ragnhild Skoftesdatter, Born	12th century (Julian )
2 CONT * Death	12th century
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Spouse==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: Dag Eilivsson,  Ragnhild was married to Dag Eilivsson, Lendmann from Viken. They were in the garden Bratsberg in Telemark. The sagas announce that Dag had 6 Born: two Gutar, Vatnorm and Gregorius, and four girls – Bauge
2 CONC id, Borghild, Sigrid and Gyrid. Ragnhild was probably not the mother of all Dag's born. From the age of the eldest guten, Vatnorm, there are doubts as to whether Ragnhild can have his mother. Gregorius, however, was a common child
2 CONC  of Ragnhild and Dag, and it is precisely this zone and his political role as the castar light on the position of Ragnhild. - https://nbl.snl.no/Ragnhild_Skoftesdotter
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Children==
2 CONT
2 CONT *  Gregorius Dagsson, Birth: circa 1106 in Bratsberg, Gjerpen, Skien, Telemark, Norway, Death: January 07, 1161  Ranrike (no KUNGÄLV, Sweden) (Killed when he tried to cross the ice)Place of Burial: Gimsøy nonnekloster (the nunnery
2 CONC  at Gimsøy), Skien, Telemark, Norway
2 CONT * Baugeid Dagsdotter, was in the church and was the abbot of Gimsøy monastery.
2 CONT * Gyrid Dagsdotter; married Gyrd Åmundesson, foster brother of King Inge.
2 CONT * Sigrid Dagsdotter; married Halldor Brynjulvsson paa Vettaland
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source''' - Annaler for nordisk oldkyndighed og historie, page 215 - https://books.google.com/books?id=V3EJAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA215&lpg=PA215&dq=Jon+Hallkellson&source=bl&ots=_YH_GCoakF&sig=ACfU3U0uVcOPfuwu-8akzYNI69EarYn0TA&hl=en&sa=
2 CONC X&ved=2ahUKEwjw6pzp9tjiAhUEvZ4KHTzGAbAQ6AEwB3oECAoQAQ#v=onepage&q=Jon%20Hallkellson&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT ==History==
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnhild Skoftesdotter, Born before 1103; exact birth year and birthplace are not known; Death year and city is not known. Høvdinghustru. Parents: Lendmann Skofte Ogmundsson (approx. 1040–1103); Mother's name is not known. Married
2 CONC  to the husband Dag Eilivsson (see NBL1, bd. 3). Sonones daughter of Torberg Arnesson (dead about 1050); mother of Gregorius Dagsson (dead 1161).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnhild Skoftesdotter is one of the many important but nurturing "invisible" women in the political landscape in the 12th century. Like so many other women in the fairytale literature, Ragnhild is primarily referred to as daughte
2 CONC r, spouse and mother of several influential noblemen, but her story also highlights women's power and influence on political life in the Middle Ages.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnhild listened to the mighty Giske danger at Sunnmøre; his father was a lover at Giske in the late 1000s. He was married to Gudrun Tordsdotter, but it is uncertain whether Gudrun was the mother of Ragnhild. Ragnhild is not ment
2 CONC ioned between these common children, and so can thus have been born outside of marriage. This claim is strengthened by information that Ragnhild had a sister, Gyda. This sister is never connected to Skofte or Guessing in any kind 
2 CONC of way, other than as Ragnhild's sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ragnhild was married to Dag Eilivsson, the lover from Viken. Dei was a tranquil busette at the garden Bratsberg in Telemark. The sagas state that Dag had 6 children: two boys, Vatnorm and Gregorius, and four girls - Baugeid, Borgh
2 CONC ild, Sigrid and Gyrid. Ragnhild was probably not the mother of all Dag's children. From the age of the oldest gut, Vatnorm, it is doubtful whether Ragnhild can have our mother. Gregory, on the other hand, was a common child of Rag
2 CONC nhild and Dag, and it is precisely this zone and his political role that cast light on Ragnhild's position.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abstract and Morkinskin contain a story, or "toe", which, among other things, Ragnhild and her sister, Gyda, are important actors. History is based on a conflict between Gyda and her neighbor, Geirstein. After a long period of hos
2 CONC tility, Geirstein was killed by Gyda's workmen, and he sent him from the garden in a hurry to protect him against the two sons of Geirstein. Dei was hirdmen with King Sigurd Munn, and the daughter of Geirstein was the king was fre
2 CONC e. Against such powerful fathers, Gyda turns to sister and to the sister-in-law Gregorius with prayer for help. Gregory did not want to do anything about it at first, claiming that he was not a guy to fight against King Sigurd. Bu
2 CONC t Ragnhild pressed on and demanded action - to repudiate the injustice against the kinsmen deira, and for Gregory to show what he was capable of. The only thing to get it as he wanted.
2 CONT
2 CONT History of Gyda, Ragnhild and Gregorius, who can otherwise seem loose and trivial, has a very clear function in the saga. It is a preoccupation with, and an explanation for, the enmity between King Harald Gilles sons, who broke ou
2 CONC t in the 1150s, and on Gregory's loyalty to King Inge. Like other "inciting" women in the sagas, Ragnhild is made to convey norms and ideals - social expectations to Gregory. At the same time, history illustrates a political room 
2 CONC for maneuver for women who are often ignored in the literature. Politics did not rarely lead the world, in an arena where Ragnhild and other women could have a big impact on the decisions.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was not Vatnorm and Gregorius in Ragnhild's family who excelled among the elite in the 12th century. Of the daughters Borghild was married to Kåre at Austrått, Sigrid with Halldor at Vetteland in Ranrike and Gyrid with Gyrd Åmu
2 CONC ndesson, foster brother to King Inge. Baugeid went to church saying the best and our abbess for Gimsøy monastery.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources and literature==
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Fagrskinna, published by Finnur Jónsson, Copenhagen 1932
2 CONT
2 CONT     * HKr. by F. Hødnebø and H. Magerøy, Norway's kingdoms, bd. 3, 1979
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Morkinskinna, published by Finnur Jónsson, Copenhagen 1902–03
2 CONT
2 CONT     * NFH, bd. 6, 1857/1943
2 CONT
2 CONT     * S. Bagge: "Man and woman in Heimskringla", in B. Sellevold (ed.): Focus on women in medieval sources, Skara 1992
2 CONT
2 CONT     * C. M. Nordal: Women and politics during the Civil War, h.oppg. UiB, Bergen 1998
2 CONT
2 CONT     * B. Opheim: With support from friends and friends. Relationship and science as party-forming factors in the Norwegian internal conflict 1130–1208, h.oppg. UiB, Bergen 1998
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Links==
