1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Sigurd 'Digri' Hlodvisson Orkneyjarl=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Parents: Hlodve Orkneyjarl and his wife
2 CONT * Spouse: Olith ingen Mael Coluim meic Cinaeda or Donada [Med Lands' explanation for the 'daughter of Malcolm not likely to have been being taken from her first husband, Findlaech before his death in 1020 is not a strong argument 
2 CONC in my opinion, given that Findlaech and  Sigurd were old adversaries and  in1005 Malcolm became king, perhaps making his daughter a greater prize than the beaten enemy of Sigurd warranted. Secondary sources put Thorfinn's parents 
2 CONC as Donada & Sigurd - Sharon]
2 CONT
2 CONT = Sigurd Hlodvisson=
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd Hlodvisson (circa 960–23 April 1014), popularly known as Sigurd the Stout, was Earl of Orkney. The main source for his life is the Orkneyinga Saga, written some two centuries after his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Orkneyinga Saga reports that Sigurd was the son of Hlodvir, one of the five sons of Thorfinn Skull-Splitter, and Eithne, said to be a daughter of Kjarvalr, King of Ireland—Cerball mac Dúnlainge, King of Osraige, who died in 88
2 CONC 8. Hlodvir died in his bed and was succeeded as Earl by Sigurd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd's uncle Ljot had been killed in war against the Scots, and Sigurd soon faced trouble from his southern neighbours. An "Earl Finnleik" led an army against him which outnumbered Sigurd's men by seven to one. The Saga famously
2 CONC  records Sigurd's mother's reply when he went to her for advice:
2 CONT
2 CONT I would long have fostered thee in my wool-basket, if I had known that thou wouldst live for ever, and fortune decides as to a man's life, and not circumstances. It is better to die with honour than to live with dishonour. Receive
2 CONC  a standard which I have made with my whole knowledge, and I expect it will be victorious to him before whom it is carried, but the bane of him who bears it.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Raven banner worked as just Sigurd's mother said: he was victorious but the standard-bearer was killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the 13th century Njal's Saga, Gormflaith prompted her son, Sigtrygg Silkbeard, into getting Sigurd to fight against her former husband, Brian Ború:
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>"...she sent him to Earl Sigurd to ask for support... Sigtrygg sailed back to Ireland and told his mother that the jarl had joined him."
2 CONT
2 CONT The 12th century Irish source, the Cogadh Gaedhil re Gallaibh, records the events of the Battle of Clontarf in 1014. The "foreigners and Leinstermen" were led by Brodir of the Isle of Man and Sigurd, and the battle lasted all day.
2 CONC  Though Brian was killed in the battle, the Irishmen ultimately drove back their enemies into the sea, and Sigurd himself was killed. Sigurd left four sons: Brusi, Sumarlidi, Einar and Thorfinn, each of whom would also bear the ti
2 CONC tle Earl of Orkney.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#SigurdDigridied1014A Medieval Lands, SCOTTISH NOBILITY]
2 CONT
2 CONT >c)          [http://www.geni.com/people/Torf-Einarr-R%C3%B6gnvaldarson/6000000001045369865   EINAR] "Turf-Einar” .  Orkneyinga Saga names “[http://www.geni.com/people/Hallad-Orkneyjarl/6000000001065274497?through=6000000001045491
2 CONC 041   Hallad], [http://www.geni.com/people/Hrollaug-Eyjafj%C3%B6r%C3%B0ur/6000000001065449177?through=6000000001045491041   Hrollaug] and the youngest [http://www.geni.com/people/Torf-Einarr-R%C3%B6gnvaldarson/6000000001045369865 
2 CONC  Einar]” 797].  Snorre names "Hallad, the second Einar, the third Hrollaug" as the three sons of "Earl Ragnvald" by concubines, adding that they were all "grown men" when their legitimate brothers were still children[798].  Jarl [
2 CONC of Orkney].  Orkneyinga Saga records that Ragnvald sent his “youngest son Einar” to “the islands” after his brother Hallad returned to Norway and that [http://www.geni.com/people/Harald-I-Halvdansson-%C2%ABFairhair%C2%BB-H%C3%A5rf
2 CONC agre/3950516705030063754  Harald I "Hårfagre" King of Norway] gave “the title of earl”, adding that his mother was “slave-born on each side of her family”, and stating that he killed “Thorir Tree-Beard and Kalf Scurvy” and succeed
2 CONC ed in imposing his authority in Orkney where “he was the first man to dig peat for fuel…at Tarbat Ness in Scotland”, and that he “was tall and ugly…and…one-eyed”[799].]  
2 CONT >Orkneyinga Saga records that “[http://www.geni.com/people/Halvdan-Haraldsson-%C2%ABLonglegs%C2%BB-H%C3%A5legg/6000000005049052265?through=3950516705030063754  Halfdan Long-Leg] and [http://www.geni.com/people/Gudr%C3%B8d-Haraldss
2 CONC on-%C2%ABGleam%C2%BB-Ljome-Konge-av-Hadeland/6000000003645734861?through=3950516705030063754  Gudrod Gleam], King Harald´s sons by Snæfrid” attacked “Earl Rognvald of More, killed him and assumed his authority”, that Halfdan left 
2 CONC for Orkney and “conquered the islands and set himself up as king over them”, Jarl Einar “fled the islands over to Scotland” but returned “later in the year…[and] came out as victor”, and that Halfdan´s body was found in the sea an
2 CONC d mutilated (including graphic details of the mutilation)[810].  Orkneyinga Saga records that the ensuing dispute with King Harald was settled by payment of a tax of “sixty gold marks” to the king, which Einar paid “out of his own
2 CONC  pocket on condition that he should hold all the estates [in Orkney] in fee”[811].  It should be noted that there are considerable chronological difficulties with the career of Turf-Einar and his sons, as reported in Orkneyinga Sa
2 CONC ga.  The starting point for the analysis of these problems is the date of the battle in Northumbria in which Turf-Einar´s two oldest sons were killed, which can with reasonable accuracy be placed in the early 950s.  If these two s
2 CONC ons were at least in their late thirties or forties when they died (a difficult assumption to make considering that they had no reported direct heirs), their births could not be placed before [905/15] at the earliest.  The likelih
2 CONC ood would then be that their father, Turf-Einar, was not born much earlier than [975/95].  The difficulty is that this date is completely incompatible with (a) the fact that the illegitimate sons of Ragnvald were reported as “grow
2 CONC n men” when their legitimate half-brothers were children, and (b) that the date when Ivar, the oldest legitimate son, was killed is estimated to [874]
2 CONT >- (see Cawley’s NORWEGIAN NOBILITY for further details concerning the reported events in the lives of Turf-Einar´s ancestors).  [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#_Toc268450047 (Cawley's Medlands)]
2 CONT
2 CONT >m ---.  The name of Einar´s wife is not known.  
2 CONT
2 CONT >Einar [& his wife] had [three] children:  
2 CONT
2 CONT >>1.         [http://www.geni.com/people/Arnkell/6000000001169221716   ARNKEL] (-killed Stainmore, Westmoreland 954).  Orkneyinga Saga names “[http://www.geni.com/people/Arnkell/6000000001169221716   Arnkel] …[http://www.geni.com/
2 CONC people/Erlend/6000000001169221723?through=6000000001045369865  Erlend] and…[http://www.geni.com/people/Thorfinn-I-Hausakljufer-Einarsson/6000000000160866853  Thorfinn] Skull-Splitter” as the three sons of Einar[812].  Snorre names
2 CONC  "Earls Arnkel and Erlend, the sons of Earl Torfeinar" when recording that they accompanied King [http://www.geni.com/people/Eirik-I-Haraldsson-%C2%ABBlood-axe%C2%BB-Blod%C3%B8ks-c-885/6000000003094359287  Eirik "Blodøks"] from Or
2 CONC kney to plunder the Hebrides and Ireland, but were defeated and killed with King Eirik[813].  Joint Jarl of Orkney with his brothers.  Snorre records that "Arnkel, Erlend and Thorfin Hausakljufer" succeeded their father but that t
2 CONC he first two were killed "in a war expedition"[814].  Orkneyinga Saga records that “Earls Arnkel and Erlend, the sons of Turf-Einar” joined ex-King Eirik after he was expelled from Northumbria by [http://www.geni.com/people/Edmund
2 CONC -I-The-Magnificent-King-of-the-English/6000000006294594631   Edmund] King of England and that they sailed to the Hebrides, looted in Ireland and Strathclyde, until they were killed in battle against [http://www.geni.com/people/Ola
2 CONC f-Cuaran-King-of-Northumbria/6000000011858807667   Olaf King of Northumbria] [815].]  [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#_Toc268450047 (Cawley's Medlands)]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>2.         [http://www.geni.com/people/Erlend/6000000001169221723?through=6000000001045369865   ERLEND] (-killed Stainmore, Westmoreland 954).  Orkneyinga Saga names “[http://www.geni.com/people/Arnkell/6000000001169221716   Arn
2 CONC kel] …[http://www.geni.com/people/Erlend/6000000001169221723?through=6000000001045369865  Erlend] and…[http://www.geni.com/people/Thorfinn-I-Hausakljufer-Einarsson/6000000000160866853  Thorfinn] Skull-Splitter”  as the three sons 
2 CONC of Einar[816].  Snorre names "Earls Arnkel and Erlend, the sons of Earl Torfeinar" when recording that they accompanied King [http://www.geni.com/people/Eirik-I-Haraldsson-%C2%ABBlood-axe%C2%BB-Blod%C3%B8ks-c-885/60000000030943592
2 CONC 87  Eirik "Blodøks"] from Orkney to plunder the Hebrides and Ireland, but were defeated and killed with King Eirik[817].  Joint Jarl of Orkney with his brothers.  Snorre records that "Arnkel, Erlend and Thorfin Hausakljufer" succe
2 CONC eded their father but that the first two were killed "in a war expedition"[818].  Orkneyinga Saga records that “Earls Arnkel and Erlend, the sons of Turf-Einar” joined ex-King Eirik after he was expelled from Northumbria by [http:
2 CONC //www.geni.com/people/Edmund-I-The-Magnificent-King-of-the-English/6000000006294594631   Edmund] King of England and that they sailed to the Hebrides, looted in Ireland and Strathclyde, until they were killed in battle against [ht
2 CONC tp://www.geni.com/people/Olaf-Cuaran-King-of-Northumbria/6000000011858807667   Olaf King of Northumbria] [815].]  [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTTISH%20NOBILITY.htm#_Toc268450047 (Cawley's Medlands)]
2 CONT
2 CONT >>3.         [http://www.geni.com/people/Thorfinn-I-Hausakljufer-Einarsson/6000000000160866853 THORFINN] "Hausakliffer/Skullcleaver" ([910/20]-[soon after 977], bur Hoxa, North Ronaldsway).  Orkneyinga Saga names “[http://www.geni
2 CONC .com/people/Arnkell/6000000001169221716   Arnkel] …[http://www.geni.com/people/Erlend/6000000001169221723?through=6000000001045369865  Erlend] and…[http://www.geni.com/people/Thorfinn-I-Hausakljufer-Einarsson/6000000000160866853  
2 CONC Thorfinn] Skull-Splitter”  as the three sons of Einar[820].  Snorre names "Thorfin Hausakljufer, a son of Torfeinar" when recording that he was Jarl in Orkney when the islands were invaded by the sons of King [http://www.geni.com/
2 CONC people/Eirik-I-Haraldsson-%C2%ABBlood-axe%C2%BB-Blod%C3%B8ks-c-885/6000000003094359287  Eirik "Blodøks"] [821].  Joint Jarl of Orkney with his brothers.  Sole Jarl of Orkney 954, and 955-976.  Orkneyinga Saga records that “Earl Th
2 CONC orfinn Skull-Splitter…died in his bed” and was “laid in a burial mound at Hoxa in North Ronaldsway”[822].  The Complete Peerage estimates Thorfinn´s death to “soon after 977” which appears broadly consistent with the events in whi
2 CONC ch his children are reported as involved[823].  
2 CONT
