1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Gudrun Nevsteinsdatter, av Rein=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Nevstein Haldorsson Stallare and Ingerid Sigurdsdatter af Vestfold 
2 CONT * Gudrun Nevsteinsdatter (born about 1052 ) was the daughter of Nevstein Stallare and Ingerid Sigurdsdatter . Gudrun married Skule Tostesson , also known as Kongsfostre, on the farm Rein in Rissa in Sør-Trøndelag.
2 CONT * Snorre Sturlason writes in Harald Hardrådes saga that “King Olav married him to his uncle, Gudrun Nevstein's daughter; her mother was Ingerid, daughter of King Sigurd Syr and Asta; she was the sister of King Olav the Holy and Ki
2 CONC ng Harald ” 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORWEGIAN%20NOBILITY.htm#SkuliTostesson Project MedLands Norway Nobility]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''SKULI Tostesson "Kongsfostre",''' son of [TOSTIG Godwinsson & his wife Judith de Flandre] .  Snorre names "Skule, a son of Earl Toste, who since has been called the king's foster-son, and his brother Ketil Krok…of high family i
2 CONC n England" when recording that they accompanied Olav King of Norway after their father's were both killed at the battle of Stamford Bridge[222], the context suggesting that he and his brother were adults at the time.  If this is c
2 CONC orrect, they must have been their father's illegitimate sons and not the children of Tostig who were “unweaned” when their father died, referred to in the Vita Ædwardi Regis[223].  Morkinskinna records that “Skúli, the son of Jarl
2 CONC  Tostig Godwinson, and Ketill krókr from Hálogaland came to Norway with King Óláfr” after the failed invasion of England in 1066[224].  Snorre records that King Olav granted lands "eastward at Konungahella, Oslo, Tunsberg, Sarpsbo
2 CONC rg, Bergen, and north at Nidaros" to Skule[225].  Morkinskinna records that “not long after King Haraldr´s fall Skúli went west to England to ask for the return of King Haraldr´s body” which was “readily granted to him”[226]. 
2 CONT '''married GUDRUN Nefsteinsdatter,''' daughter of NEFSTEIN --- & his wife Ingerid Sigurdsdatter.  Snorre records that King Olav gave Skule "his female relative, Gudrun, the daughter of Nefstein…and Ingerid" in marriage[227].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Skuli & his wife GUDRUN Nefsteinsdatter had two children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1.         '''ASULF Skulesson of Rein''' Snorre names "Asolf of Reine" as the son of Skule & his wife[228].  Morkinskinna names “Ásólfr, the father of Guthormr at Rein” as the son of “Skúli, the son of Jarl Tostig Godwinson”[229
2 CONC ]. '''married THORA Skoptisdotter,''' daughter of SKOPTI Ogmundsson of Giske & his wife ---.  Snorre names "Thora, a daughter of Skopte Ogmundson" as the wife of "Asolf of Reine"[230]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''ASULF Skulesson of Rein & THORA Skoptisdotter his wife had two children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>* a)         '''GUTTORM Asulfsson of Rein''' Snorre names "Guthorm of Reine" as the son of "Asolf of Reine" & his wife[231].  Morkinskinna names “Ásólfr, the father of Guthormr at Rein” as the son of “Skúli, the son of Jarl To
2 CONC stig Godwinson”[232]. 
2 CONT >>>>* b)         '''SIGRID married HALLKEL Huk.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''HALLKEL Huk & his wife SIGRID Asulfsdotter had two children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>* i)          '''JON Halkelsson married  ([1155]) MARGARETA Haraldsdatter, illegitimate daughter of HARALD "Gille" King of Norway & his mistress'''.  Snorre names "Harald Gille's third daughter…Margaret who was married to Jo
2 CONC n Halkelson, a brother of Simon"[233]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jon Halkelsson & his wife MARGARETA Haraldsdatter had one child'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>>* (a)       '''HALLKEL Jonsson''' (-killed in battle Florevåg near Bergen 3 Apr 1194).  From Blindheim Jarl in Norway. married (after 19 Jun 1179) as her second husband, RAGNHILD Erlingsdatter,''' widow of JON Thorbergsson,
2 CONC  daughter of ERLING Ormsen Skakke & his wife Kristin of Norway.'''  The Saga of King Sverre records the marriage of "Ragnhild daughter of Earl Erling and Kristin Kings-daughter" and "Halkel son of Jon Hallkelsson"[234].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Halkel Jonsson & his wife RAGNHILD Erlingsdatter had one child'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>>>* (1)       '''RAGNVALD Hallkelsson''' (-murdered Husvik near Drøbak 1217, bur Oslo).  Stormann of the Bagler in Norway. 
2 CONT >>>>>>* ii)         '''SIMON Skalp''' (-killed in battle 1161).  Snorre names "Simon Skalp, a son of Halkel Huk" when recording his marriage[235].  married  ([1155]) MARIA Haraldsdatter, illegitimate daughter of HARALD "Gille" Kin
2 CONC g of Norway & his mistress ---.  Snorre names "Harald Gille's second daughter…Maria who was married to Simon Skalp, a son of Halkel Huk"[236].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Simon Skalp & his wife MARIA Haraldsdatter had one child'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>>(a)       '''NIKOLAS''' Snorre names Nikolas as son of "Simon Skalp, a son of Halkel Huk" & his wife[237].  Snorre records that "Simon Skalp's son, the son of a daughter of King Harald Gille" was proposed as king in 1161[23
2 CONC 8]. 
2 CONT * 2.         '''RAGNHILD Skulisdatter'''  married ORM Svendsson "Krypinge-Orm, son of SVEN Svensson & his wife Ragna Ormsdatter (-[1140]).'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORWEGIAN%20NOBILITY.htm#NefsteinMIngeridSigudsB Project MedLands Norway Nobility]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''NEFSTEIN, son of -- married INGERID Sigurdsdatter, daughter of SIGURD Syr King of Ringeringe & his wife Asta Gudbrandsdatter.'''  Snorre names "Ingerid, a daughter of Sigurd Syr and Asta" as the mother of Gudrun, daughter of Ne
2 CONC fstein[220]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Nefstein & his wife Ingerid Sigurdsdatter had three children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1.         '''SIGURD'''  married ---.  The name of Sigurd´s wife is not known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sigurd & his wife had one child'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>a)         '''SIGURD Sigurdsson''' (-1135).
2 CONT * 2.         '''TORE Ingridsson'''
2 CONT * 3.         '''GUDRUN Nefsteinsdatter''' Snorre records that King Olav gave Skule "his female relative, Gudrun, the daughter of Nefstein…and Ingerid" in marriage[221].  married SKULI Tostesson,''' son of [TOSTIG Godwinsson & his 
2 CONC wife Judith de Flandre]. '''
