1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Ivar Sigtryggsson=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * His father, Sigtrygg of Närke, was a wealthy and influential magnate. According to Snorri Sturluson’s famous Norse saga compilation, the Heimskringla, Sigtrygg famously took in and housed the exiled Norwegian King Olaf Haraldsso
2 CONC n (later known as St. Olaf) and his family during the winter of 1030 as they journeyed east toward Garðaríki (Kievan Rus')
2 CONT * Ivar Sigtryggsson (often recorded in Norse sagas as Ivar, son of Sigtrygg of Nerick) was an 11th-century Swedish nobleman and chieftain. He belonged to a powerful regional dynasty from the historical province of Närke (formerly 
2 CONC Nerike), Sweden.
2 CONT * The Descendants: Ivar’s son, Sune Ivarsson, continued the family's regional dominance. Through this lineage, Ivar is considered an early ancestor of the Algotssöner family, an influential noble house that later produced several 
2 CONC lawspeakers (lagmän) in the province of Västergötland
2 CONT * Hiberno-Norse Rulers: Elements of the Uí Ímair (Dynasty of Ivar) who ruled Dublin, York, and the Irish Sea. This includes Harald Sigtryggsson (King of Limerick) or Olaf Sigtryggsson, both sons of the Viking king Sigtrygg Cáech
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://theutenberg.org/vastergotlands-lagman-algotssoner-och-folkungar/Västergötland's Lawmen – Algotssöner and Folkungar]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Earl of West Göta, Karl Sunesson, was the great-grandson of the great man "Sigtrygg in Närke".'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''As can be seen from the table of the lawmen of Västergötland, the name of the 15th lawman was Sigtrygg Algotsson . He was the son of the 12th lawman Algot Karl(e)sson and the grandson of the 11th lawman Karl(e) of Edsvära or Ka
2 CONC rl Sunesson''' . As mentioned above, his father was Sune Ivarsson , who was the son and grandson of the great men Ivar Sigtryggsson and “Sigtrygg in Närke”, i.e. the “lord Sigtrygg” who at the beginning of the 11th century receive
2 CONC d the Norwegian king Olaf the fat (the holy) and his family on the journey to Gårdarike. It should be mentioned that the name Sigge is a short form or so-called hypocorism for names that begin with Sig-, for example Sigmund , Sigv
2 CONC ard , Sigsten and Sigtrygg. A grandfather or great-grandfather with, for example, the name Sigtrygg may well have been reflected in a grandson or granddaughter's name Sigge, which later name is therefore a short form of the name S
2 CONC igtrygg. When naming children, it was the "principle of invocation" that was the most prominent principle during this time. The meaning is that the names of older ancestors were "inherited" in the family and therefore recur in lin
2 CONC e after line within the family or family group. The "inherited" names can be derived on both the agnatic and cognatic side. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding the name Sigtrygg and its etymology, we can descend into the very oldest mythology, for Saxo tells in the Chronicle of the Kingdom of Denmark – in the first book of 'The Tale of Gram' – that the Swedish king Sigtrygg had
2 CONC  a daughter named Gro and that the earl of the Goths Svarin aspired to King Sigtrygg's Swedish kingdom. In the Poetic Edda – in the ' Second poem about Helge Hundingsbane' –  there is a story about a 'Svarin's mound' , where a chi
2 CONC eftain Svarin was supposed to lie buried. It is in connection with the name Sigryn and the place Arasten that 'Svarin's mound' is mentioned. Starkodder, who according to the Hervarar saga fought a battle at the falls in Stora Ed o
2 CONC r Trollhättan , was, according to the Poetic Edda, at home at 'Svarin's mound' , where he apparently lived. Snorri's Ynglingasaga also tells of Vendsyssel's king Sigtrygg. Vendsyssel was the northernmost part of Jutland, which, th
2 CONC rough archaeological finds, including the so-called king's mound in Hullsjö in Gärdhem parish, can be proven to have had early connections with the area around Hunneberg in Väne county. Just next to Hullsjö, the aforementioned gol
2 CONC d finds in Vittene were made a few years ago. In the family of the Norwegian founder of the kingdom, Harald Hårfager we find his daughter Álof (Olof) and his son Sigtrygg. Obviously the name Sigtrygg is a name that finds its roots
2 CONC  not only in the old Danish and Norwegian royal families but far down in the purely mythological contexts. Wasn't the name Sigtrygg a transliteration of Allfather himself, i.e. Odin? Just as the name Gaut (Al-gaut) was also a tran
2 CONC sliteration of Allfather himself.19
2 CONT
2 CONT In Harald Hårfager's saga in Heimskringla it is told how Harald Hårfager's sons, among them his son Sigtrygg , received Vingulmark, Raumarike, Vestfold and Telemark. It is also interesting to note that already in a society founded
2 CONC  by Norwegian Vikings in Ireland during the period 989 – 1029 there is found King Sigtrygg Silkskegg , who was the son of the king of Ireland 940 – 980 Olof Kurran and the woman Kormland. In an attack on Ireland, Njal's saga tells
2 CONC  us , King Sigtrygg and his mother Kormland had to ask for help from Earl Sigurd in the Orkney Islands. Since the Swedish savior Erengisle Suneson (Sune Jonsson's family; boat) , who is known from 1334 – 1392, calls himself Earl o
2 CONC f the Orkney Islands, it may be of interest to mention this.20  
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Sigtrygg is also found in the Danish regency list. A Danish king Sigtrygg was contemporary with the ruler of East Franconia, i.e. Germany, who goes by the name Louis the Fat (899 – 911). Louis the Fat was the son of Louis
2 CONC  the German and thus the great-grandson of Emperor Charlemagne . Among the last Carolingians one encounters names such as Charles the Bald , Charles the Fat , Charles the Fool , Louis the Blind, etc. It should be noted that empero
2 CONC rs, kings, rulers and other great men at this time were given epithets such as 'the bald', 'the fat', 'the blind', 'the pious', 'the foolish', etc. The same type of epithet is also connected with the ancestor of the Folkunga famil
2 CONC y, Folke the Fat , who for chronological reasons must be placed at the end of the 11th century and the first part of the 12th century. According to the genealogy given above, Folke the Fat (Folke Grossus) was the son of Ingevald a
2 CONC nd grandson of Folke Filbyter . When it comes to the question of “Folke Filbyter and his three Christian sons Ingemund, Halsten and Ingevald” – according to the genealogy given above – the question is whether we are still in the r
2 CONC eal historical layers or in the area of ​​mythology. However, the names Ingemund , Halsten and Ingevald are highly historical realities, as these names were borne by the very oldest saved. For example, there is the interesting lil
2 CONC y- bearing knight Halsten Petersson , mentioned in Njudung and in Flundre härad by the Göta älv 1329 – 1357, King Magnus Eriksson’s confidant.21 
2 CONT
2 CONT If one assumes that the genealogy of the Folkunga family presented above – in Olaus Petri's version – reflects a historical reality, then chronologically the three sons Ingemund , Hallsten and Ingevald would be in the time span of
2 CONC  the middle of the 11th century and onwards. The pagan Folke Filbyter could then, hypothetically, be thought to have lived during the beginning of the 11th century. The chronology is important in this context. King Knut Svensson t
2 CONC he Holy was killed in 1086, whereupon his presumably young daughters Ingegerd and Cecilia were taken to safety to Sweden, where they both then married, Ingegerd with Folke the Fat and Cecilia with Earl Erik of Göta. Based on this,
2 CONC  Ingegerd Knutsdotter and Folke the Fat should be placed in the time span 1075 –1150 and their son Bengt Snivel to around 1110 – 1170. If these time spans are reasonably accurate, Folke the Fat's father Ingevald should be placed i
2 CONC n the time span 1030 – 1080 and his supposed father Folke Filbyter in the time span late 9th – early 11th century. In and of itself, this is chronologically consistent with the 1029 mentioned great man 'Sigtrygg i Närke', who was 
2 CONC the great-grandfather of Earl Karl Sunesson/lawman Karl(e) of Edsvära, and he is probably related to both Sigtrygg Bengtsson's lily- bearing ancestors and the ancestors of the Algotssöns. 
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id= 6000000225645530833&size=large}} 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Image 7.''' According to the Vidhemsprästen, Folke den tjocke and Ingegärd Knutsdotter's grandson Magnus Minnesköld lived at Bjälbo in Göstrings district in Östergötland. If you read Snorri's Heimskringla, you might conclude th
2 CONC at his grandson, Earl Birger Brosa , Magnus Minnesköld's older brother, also lived at Bjälbo. The farm is assumed to have been an old chieftain's farm with a castle hall and defensive tower. The stone church in Bjälbo dates from t
2 CONC he second half of the 12th century, i.e. from Birgers Brosa's time. The tower has a slightly later date. However, there is no evidence anywhere that the brothers' grandfather Folke den tjocke and his wife Ingegärd Knutsdotter (dau
2 CONC ghter of King Knut the Holy) can be linked to Bjälbo. Photo: the author. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The designations “Folkungar” and “Folkungaätten” are ambiguous. The Folkungarna were a “political interest group” or a “political party”, usually in opposition to the monarchy. But the designations can also refer to a family, a fa
2 CONC mily clan or a dynasty, where it is the ancestor Folke who forms the root of the designation; thus “Folke’s descendants” ! Whether the mythical Folke Filbyter should be included in this context is uncertain, but Folke den tjöke ca
2 CONC n definitely be counted as the ancestor of the family or clan. As mentioned above, he lived during the first half of the 12th century.22 
2 CONT
2 CONT But did Folke and his wife Ingegärd Knutsdotter live in Bjälbo in Östergötland? Nothing is known about this. No sources tell us where Folke and Ingegärd lived. We do know that their grandson Magnus Minnesköld owned Bjälbo. If you 
2 CONC read Snorri Sturluson's Heimskringla , you might conclude that it may have been the family estate of Bjälbo that was also inhabited by his grandson Earl Birger Brosa – Magnus Minnesköld's older brother – during the second half of 
2 CONC the 12th century. Bjälbo can be assumed to have been an old chieftain's estate with a castle hall and defensive structures. The stone church in Bjälbo dates from the second half of the 12th century, i.e. from the time of Earl Birg
2 CONC er Brosa. The tower has a slightly later date. It is a strongly built tower with good defensive capabilities and with many rooms for living during the battles that must have raged around Bjälbo. The designation Bjälboätten is some
2 CONC times used synonymously with the designation Folkungaätten. The term Bjälboätten can very well be used if one refers to Magnus Minnesköld and his descendants. However, the term should hardly be used for the generations before Magn
2 CONC us Minnesköld, this is because it is not known where the earliest “folkunga generations” were settled and owned. As noted, an old and substantial landholding can be attested to the older ranks of the Folkungas in Västergötland, an
2 CONC d there are also indications of abundant landholding around Nydala Monastery by Lake Rusken in Njudung. The traditional terms “Folkungar” and “Folkungaätten” can still very well be used as designations for the descendants of Folke
2 CONC  and Ingegärd.23 
2 CONT
