1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ingamoder
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Ingamoder Emundsdotter''' alt Maria Anundsdotter
2 CONT
2 CONT I de flesta uppslagsböcker framgår att hustrun till Stenkil var Emund den gamles dotter, vid namn Inga Emundsdotter (1043-1090). Alternativt hade Stenkils hustru varit Emund den gamles dotter skulle Stenkil gift sig med sin egen h
2 CONC alvsyster. Därför hävdas av vissa att det bör vara Emund den gamles halvbror Anund Jakob, som är far till Stenkils hustru och hennes namn var Maria Anundsdotter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Osäkerheten om släktrelationen kvarstår dock.
2 CONT
2 CONT Källor
2 CONT  1) 	Gunnar Hedin - Sveriges kungar och drottningar under 1000 år
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingamoder Emundsdotter den heliga av Sverige, född 1043, död 1090, var en svensk prinsessa, drottning av Sverige och ett katolskt helgon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hennes verkliga namn är okänt. "Ingamoder" betyder "Inges moder"; hon var mor till kung Inge den äldre. Ingamoder är inget av katolska kyrkan sanktionerat och erkänt helgon. "Ingmoders källa" i Dala mellan Falköping och Tidaholm s
2 CONC ägs vara grundad till hennes minne; det är möjligt att detta var en äldre, hednisk källa, som sedan omvigdes till en kristen källa. Här offrades mynt för hälsans skull. Man gjorde vallfärder dit fram till 1800-talet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingamoder föddes som barn till kung Emund den gamle av Sverige och hans norska drottning, Astrid Nialsdotter, och gifte sig med Stenkil Ragnvaldssson, som efterträdde hennes far på tronen; äktenskapet med henne kan mycket väl ha g
2 CONC jort att han ansågs mer legitim som tronarvinge. Hennes bröder, Emund och Ingvar, hade båda dött före fadern.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingamoder var drottning av Sverige mellan 1060/1061 och 1066.
2 CONT
2 CONT Familj  
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Barn ==
2 CONT
2 CONT Erik Stenkilsson, kung av Sverige
2 CONT
2 CONT Halsten, kung av Sverige.
2 CONT
2 CONT Inge den äldre , kung av Sverige.
2 CONT
2 CONT Externa länkar  
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.fredrikahlander.com/genealogy/pf9e0fc0b.html
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.sollidensbil.se/sol_sv.kungar2.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT Åke Ohlmarks "Alla Sveriges drottningar"
2 CONT
2 CONT Her name is not known, she is called Ingamoder Emundsdotter = Mother of Inge Daughter of Emund.
2 CONT She was the mother of three Swedish Kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingamoder Emundsdotter of Sweden, (1043–1090), was a Swedish princess and probably queen consort, and allegedly a local Saint, daughter of King Emund the Old, married to King Stenkil of Sweden and mother of King Inge I of Sweden o
2 CONC f Sweden. Her real name is not known; the name "Ingamoder" was allegedly her name as a Saint, which meant "Mother of Inge".
2 CONT
2 CONT This woman was born a child of King Emund the Old of Sweden and his queen consort Astrid Njalsdotter from Norway. Her brother, Emund, as well as her alleged brother Ingvar, died before her father. She married Stenkil Rangvaldsson 
2 CONC in about 1058. As her mother allegedly had been previously married to the father of her spouse, the step-father of this princess, the marriage would have broken the laws of the Christian church. This has been suggested as a reason
2 CONC  why her actual name is not mentioned in any documents. Her spouse replaced her father on the throne in 1061; it has been assumed that the marriage made her husband more acceptable for the throne. Born Princess of Sweden, she is t
2 CONC o be be considered a Swedish queen if she was married to her spouse when he had become King (that is unknown), and thereby she would have held the rare distinction of being a daughter of a king to become a queen consort of her own
2 CONC  birth country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingamoder has been depicted as a local Swedish saint. An old holy well ascribed to a Saint Ingamoder, "The Well of Ingamoder", may originally have been a holy Pagan well, allegedly located since her times in Dala between Falköping
2 CONC  and Tidaholm and the goal of pilgrimages, where people offered coins for health until the 19th century. This princess or queen, however, is not an officially sanctioned and recognized saint by the Catholic church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her parentage, at least on her father's side (Edmund), and the fact that she was the mother of at least two future kings, has been considered probable by several authorities. The rest of her story is entirely unsubstantiated and m
2 CONC ust be considered legend.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://images.google.no/imgres?imgurl=http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Fredrik_I,_king_of_Sweden.jpg/100px-Fredrik_I,_king_of_Sweden.jpg&imgrefurl=http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Swedish_monarchs&usg=_
