2 CONT
2 CONT Sadly for Vratislaus, his last years were occupied by dynastic quarrelling. When his brother Otto died in 1086, he gave Olomouc to his son Boleslaus, which was seen to be an act against the interests of Conrad. Vratislaus raised a
2 CONC n army against Conrad and sent it out under his other son Bretislaus. Instead, this son turned on him. Vratislaus, in keeping with Bohemian custom, designated an heir: Conrad. Thus reconciled with his surviving brother, the two de
2 CONC molished Bretislaus, who fled to Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vratislaus died of a hunting wound on January 14, 1092, after a reign of thirty years. He was buried in St. Peter and Paul′s Church, Vyšehrad.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family Vratislaus was married three times. His first wife Maria died during premature childbirth. He married the second time in 1057 to Adelaide, daughter of Andrew I of Hungary, who died in 1061. They had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Vratislaus (-1061) Judith (1056/58-1086), married to Ladislaus I Herman, son of Casimir I of Poland Ludmila(-after 1100) Bretislaus II of Bohemia (c. 1060–December 22, 1100), Duke of Bohemia In 1062, Vratislaus married a third tim
2 CONC e to Świętosława of Poland, a daughter of Casimir I of Poland. They had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Boleslaus (-1091) Borivoj II of Bohemia (c. 1064-February 2, 1124), Duke of Bohemia Vladislaus I of Bohemia (-April 12, 1125), Duke of Bohemia Sobeslav I of Bohemia (-February 14, 1140), Duke of Bohemia Judith (c. 1066-9 December 
2 CONC 1108), married to Wiprecht de Groitzsch Boleslaus II of Bohemia   Emma of Melnik         Berthold, Margrave of the Nordmark   Eilika of Walbeck   Otto    
2 CONT
2 CONT     Oldrich of Bohemia           Božena (Křesinova)           Henry of Schweinfurt           Gerberga     
2 CONT
2 CONT             Bretislaus I of Bohemia                           Judith of Schweinfurt             
2 CONT
2 CONT                             Vratislaus II of Bohemia                             
