2 CONT
2 CONT   The Duke of Normandy has won
2 CONT
2 CONT     The battle, to our bane.
2 CONT
2 CONT   On the field of Hastings, where he fought,
2 CONT
2 CONT     The king is lying slain.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Arise and come with us; we seek
2 CONT
2 CONT     His body among the dead.
2 CONT
2 CONT   To Waltham Abbey it shall be borne.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Twas thus our Abbot said.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The woman arose and girded her gown,
2 CONT
2 CONT     And silently went behind
2 CONT
2 CONT   The hurrying monks. Her grizzly hair
2 CONT
2 CONT     Streamed wildly on the wind.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Barefoot through bog and bush and briar
2 CONT
2 CONT     She followed and did not stay,
2 CONT
2 CONT   Till Hastings and the cliffs of chalk
2 CONT
2 CONT     They saw at dawn of day.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The mist, that like a sheet of white
2 CONT
2 CONT     The field of battle cloaked,
2 CONT
2 CONT   Melted anon; with hideous din
2 CONT
2 CONT     The daws flew up and croaked.
2 CONT
2 CONT   In thousands on the bloody plain
2 CONT
2 CONT     Lay strewn the piteous corpses,
2 CONT
2 CONT   Wounded and torn and maimed and stripped,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Among the fallen horses.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The woman stopped not for the blood;
2 CONT
2 CONT     She waded barefoot through,
2 CONT
2 CONT   And from her fixed and staring eyes
2 CONT
2 CONT     The arrowy glances flew.
2 CONT
2 CONT   Long, with the panting monks behind,
2 CONT
2 CONT     And pausing but to scare
2 CONT
2 CONT   The greedy ravens from their food,
2 CONT
2 CONT     She searched with eager care.
2 CONT
2 CONT   She searched and toiled the livelong day,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Until the night was nigh;
2 CONT
2 CONT   Then sudden from her breast there burst
2 CONT
2 CONT     A shrill and awful cry.
2 CONT
2 CONT   For on the battle-field at last
2 CONT
2 CONT     His body she had found.
2 CONT
2 CONT   She kissed, without a tear or word,
2 CONT
2 CONT     The wan face on the ground.
2 CONT
2 CONT   She kissed his brow, she kissed his mouth,
2 CONT
2 CONT     She clasped him close, and pressed
2 CONT
2 CONT   Her poor lips to the bloody wounds
2 CONT
2 CONT     That gaped upon his breast.
2 CONT
2 CONT   His shoulder stark she kisses too,
2 CONT
2 CONT     When, searching, she discovers
2 CONT
2 CONT   Three little scars her teeth had made
2 CONT
2 CONT     When they were happy lovers.
2 CONT
2 CONT   The monks had been and gotten boughs,
2 CONT
2 CONT     And of these boughs they made
2 CONT
2 CONT   A simple bier, whereon the corse
2 CONT
2 CONT     Of the fallen king was laid.
2 CONT
2 CONT   To Waltham Abbey to his tomb
2 CONT
2 CONT     The king was thus removed;
2 CONT
2 CONT   And Edith of the Swan's Neck walked
2 CONT
2 CONT     By the body that she loved.
2 CONT
2 CONT   She chanted litanies for his soul
2 CONT
2 CONT     With a childish, weird lament
2 CONT
2 CONT   That shuddered through the night. The monks
2 CONT
2 CONT     Prayed softly as they went.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT A History of Britain: At the Edge of the World, 3500 BC - 1603 AD by Simon Schama, BBC/Miramax, 2000 ISBN 0-7868-6675-6
2 CONT
2 CONT The German Classics of the Nineteenth and Twentieth Centuries, Volume 06: Masterpieces of German Literature Translated into English in Twenty Volumes by Kuno Francke http://www.gutenberg.org/etext/12473
2 CONT
2 CONT Great Tales from English History: The Truth About King Arthur, Lady Godiva, Richard the Lionheart, and More by Robert Lacey, 2004 ISBN-10: 031610910X
2 CONT
2 CONT House of Godwine: The History of Dynasty by Emma Mason, 2004 ISBN-10: 1852853891
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Lines: 176-2, 176A-4, 177-1
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ealdgyth Swan-neck, also known as Edith the Fair, Edith, Aldgyth and most commonly Edith Swan-Neck {1025-1086(?)} is best known as the mistress or common-law wife of King Harold II of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT She bore him several children and was his common law wife (according to Danish law, by a civil "handfast" marriage) for over 20 years. Though she was not considered Harold's wife by the Church, there is no indication that the chil
2 CONC dren she bore by Harold were treated as illegitimate by the culture at the time. In fact, one of Harold Godwinesson and Edith Swan-Neck's daughters, Gyda Haraldsdatter, (also known as Gytha of Wessex), was addressed as "princess" 
2 CONC and was married to the Grand Duke Of Kiev, Vladimir Monomakh. It is through this union that the Godwinesson line would remain in European nobility and would, ironically (given that this line was ended due to the Norman invasion of
2 CONC  1066), reenter English royalty, making the current ruler of England, Queen Elizabeth II the 29th great-granddaughter of King Harold II and Edith Swan-Neck.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though King Harold II would "legally" marry Edith (Ealdgyth) of Mercia, the widow of the Welsh ruler Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, (who he defeated in battle), in 1064, this is seen by most modern scholars as a marriage of political means
2 CONC . Since at the time Mercia and Wales were allied against England, the political marriage would give the English claim in two very troublesome regions, as well as give Harold Godwinesson a marriage deemed "legitimate" by the clergy
2 CONC  of the Church, something his longtime common law wife, Edith Swan-Neck unfortunately could not provide.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ealdgyth Swan-neck, also known as Edith the Fair, Edith, Aldgyth and most commonly Edith Swan-Neck {1025-1086(?)} is best known as the mistress or common-law wife of King Harold II of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT She bore him several children and was his common law wife (according to Danish law, by a civil "handfast" marriage) for over 20 years. Though she was not considered Harold's wife by the Church, there is no indication that the chil
2 CONC dren she bore by Harold were treated as illegitimate by the culture at the time. In fact, one of Harold Godwinesson and Edith Swan-Neck's daughters, Gyda Haraldsdatter, (also known as Gytha of Wessex), was addressed as "princess" 
2 CONC and was married to the Grand Duke Of Kiev, Vladimir Monomakh. It is through this union that the Godwinesson line would remain in European nobility and would, ironically (given that this line was ended due to the Norman invasion of
2 CONC  1066), reenter English royalty, making the current ruler of England, Queen Elizabeth II the 29th great-granddaughter of King Harold II and Edith Swan-Neck.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though King Harold II would "legally" marry Edith (Ealdgyth) of Wessex, the widow of the Welsh ruler Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, (who he defeated in battle), in 1064, this is seen by most modern scholars as a marriage of political means
2 CONC . Since at the time Mercia and Wales were allied against England, the political marriage would give the English claim in two very troublesome regions, as well as give Harold Godwinesson a marriage deemed "legitimate" by the clergy
2 CONC  of the Church, something his longtime common law wife, Edith Swan-Neck unfortunately could not provide.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith Swan-Neck would be remembered in history and folklore for one very important thing: it was she who identified Harold after his defeat at The Battle of Hastings. Harold's body was horrifically mutilated after the battle by th
2 CONC e Norman army of William the Conquerer and despite the pleas by Harold's own mother for William to surrender Harold's body for burial, the Norman army refused even though Harold's mother offered William Harold's weight in gold. It
2 CONC  was then that Edith Swan-Neck walked through the carnage of battle so that she may identify Harold by markings on his chest known only to her. It was because of Edith Swan-Neck's identification of Harold's body that Harold was gi
2 CONC ven a Christian burial by the monks at Waltham. This legend was recounted in the well-known poem by Heinrich Heine, "The Battlefield of Hastings" (1855), which features Edith Swan-neck as the main character and claims that the 'ma
2 CONC rks known only to her' were in fact love bites.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ealdgyth,_daughter_of_Earl_%C3%86lfgar
