2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod I Yaroslavich (Ukrainian and Russian: Всеволод I Ярославич), (1030 – 13 April 1093) ruled as Grand Prince of Kiev from 1078 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Russian History in Portrait page on Vsevolod Yaroslavich (Grand Prince):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.rulex.ru/01030958.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT
2 CONT From Darryl Lundy's Peerage page (Forrás / Source):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p22074.htm#i220736
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod I, Grand Duke of Kiev 
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #220736, d. 1093
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=22 March 2021
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod I, Grand Duke of Kiev was the son of Jarislaus I, Grand Duke of Kiev and Ingegarde of Sweden.  He married Irene (?), daughter of Constantine IX Monomachus, Emperor of Constantinople. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1093. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod I, Grand Duke of Kiev succeeded to the title of Grand Duke Vsevolod I of Kiev in 1076.  He was deposed as Grand Duke of Kiev in 1077. He succeeded to the title of Grand Duke Vsevolod I of Kiev in 1078. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Vsevolod I, Grand Duke of Kiev and Anastasia Irene Maria Monomachos of Byzantium.
2 CONT
2 CONT -1. Vladimir II Monomakh, Prince of Novgorod and Kiev+1 d. 19 May 1125
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Storfyrste av Kiev, Vsevolod I av Novgorod (1030-1093).
2 CONT
2 CONT Storfyrste Vsevolod I av Novgorod. Født 1030. Død 13.04.1093. Han var sønn av Storfyrste av Kiev, Jaroslav I den Vise av Novgorod og Ingegjerd Olavsdatter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod giftet seg omkring 1046 med Prinsesse Theodora Monomachus av Bysants. De fikk sønnen:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Storfyrste Vladimir II Monomakh av Novgorod. Født 1053. Død 19.05.1125.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod var fyrste av Perejaslavl i 1054. Storfyrste av Kiev 1078 - 1093. Vsevolod var 2. ganger gift med Oda av Tyskland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1). N. de Baumgarten: Généalogie et Mariage occidenteaux des Rurikides Russes du Xe au XIII Siècle. Mogens Bugge: Våre forfedre, nr. 553. Bent og Vidar Billing Hansen: Rosensverdslektens forfedre, side 90.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Fyrste av Pereyaslavl fra 1054.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fyrste av Chernigiv 1077-78.
2 CONT
2 CONT Storfyrste av Kijev [Kiev] i 1077 og 1078 - 1093.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod var 2. gang gift med Oda av Tyskland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod støttet sin bror Izyaslev i Kijev mot den tyrkiske folkegruppen Polovsti (kumaner, Polouzianer/Kipcakser] og troneraneren Vseslav I Bryacheslav. Han holdt seg utenfor da deres bror, Svyatoslav, tok tronen i Kijev (1073-76
2 CONC ), men støttet igjen sin bror, Izyaslav, når denne returnerte i 1077. Han flyttet så til Chernigiv. Men deres nevø Oleg Svyatoslavish, en sønn til den tidligere herskeren i Chernigiv, var ikke villig til å kompromisse. Han brakte 
2 CONC inn en polovtsi-arme for å angripe Vsyevolod i 1078. Vsyevolod flyktet til Kijev hvor hans bror Izyaslav raskt organiserte en redningsarme. Når så Izyaslav døde i slaget mot Oleg utenfor Chernigiv, flyttet Vsyevolod til Kijev. I e
2 CONC t familiekompromiss overtok Oleg Chernigiv.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tekst: Tore Nygaard
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilder:
2 CONT
2 CONT N. de Baumgarten: Généalogie et Mariage occidenteaux des Rurikides Russes du Xe au XIII Siècle. Mogens Bugge: Våre forfedre, nr. 553. Bent og Vidar Billing Hansen: Rosensverdslektens forfedre, side 90.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation for Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#VsevolodIaroslavichdied1093B
2 CONT
2 CONT VSEVOLOD Iaroslavich, son of IAROSLAV I "Mudriy/the Wise" Grand Prince of Kiev & his second wife Ingigerd Olafsdottir of Sweden (1030-13 Apr 1093, bur Kiev, Church of St Sophia).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Vsevolod "a fourth son" to Vladimir in 1030.  Snorre names "Valdemar, Vissivald and Holte the Bold" as the children of "King Jarisleif" & his wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT In accordance with the terms of his father's testament, he succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Pereyaslavl, the territory including Rostov, the whole Volga, Oka and Klazma river basin and the region of Bielooziero.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was defeated [1062] by the Kuman [Cumans], in their first attack against  Rus.  He was forced out of Pereyaslavl and retreated to Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was appointed to succeed his brother Sviatoslav as Prince of Chernigov in 1073.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his brother in 1076 as VSEVOLOD I Grand Prince of Kiev, but was deposed in 1077 by his older brother Iziaslav.
2 CONT
2 CONT His nephew Oleg Sviatoslavich claimed Chernigov after the death of his father Sviatoslav, but was expelled by Vsevolod in Apr 1078.  Oleg returned with support from the Kuman [Cumans] to take Chernigov by force, defeating Vsevolod
2 CONC  on the river Sozh, east of Pereyaslavl, 25 Aug 1078[339].  Prince Vsevolod thereupon sought support from his brother Iziaslav, and they defeated Oleg 3 Oct 1078 at Nezhatin Meadow near Chernigov, where Prince Iziaslav was killed[
2 CONC 340].
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod was restored once more as Grand Prince of Kiev in 1078 after Prince Iziaslav's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death 13 Apr 1093 of Vsevolod son of Yaroslav and his place of burial[341].
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (1046) [MARIA] [Irina], daughter of [--- Monomachos] & his wife --- ([1030/35]-1067).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle refers to the wife of Vsevolod as "the Greek princess" but does not name her or give her origin[342].  The primary source which states her name has not yet been identified.  She and her marriage are not refer
2 CONC red to in Greek sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT No doubt her belonging to the Monomachos family has been assumed, firstly because her son is generally known as "Vladimir Monomakh" and secondly because Emperor Konstantinos IX Monomachos ruled in Byzantium at the date of her marr
2 CONC iage, but no information has been found to corroborate this relationship.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is unlikely that she was the daughter of Emperor Konstantinos himself as he is not recorded in Greek sources as having had children by any of his wives or mistresses, although her birth date range (estimated from the birth of h
2 CONC er son in 1053) would be consistent with her having been the daughter of his second marriage.  In particular, Psellos mentions no children in his detailed review of the events of the emperor´s reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her birth date is estimated from her having given birth to her son in 1053.  The primary source which corroborates the date of her marriage has not so far been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly --- Kuman princess, daughter of --- Khan of the Kumans (-1111).  Baumgarten records this marriage and cites a primary source in support[343].  She adopted the name ANNA after her marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Vsevolod I & his first wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Vladimir Vsevolodich "Monomach" (b. 1053, d. 19 May 1125, Grand Prince of Kiev)
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Vsevolod I & his second wife had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Rostislav Vsevolodich (b. 1070, d. 26 May 1093, drowend in the Stugna River while retreating, buried St. Sophia Cathedral, Kiev, Prince of Pereyaslavl)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Yevpraxia/Adelheid Vsevolodich (b. 1071, d. 10 July 1109, buried Pecherskiy Caves, Kiev, married firstly Heinrich I der Lange, Graf von Stade, Markgraf der Nordmark, and secondly Emperor Heinrich IV King of Germany, divorcing h
2 CONC im after he imprisons and abuses her)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Yekaterina Vsevolodovna (d. 11 August 1108, nun)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Unknown Vsevolodovna (d. 1089)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Yanka (Anna) Vsevolodovna (d. 3 November 1112, Abbess of Janczyn)
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wikipedia page on Vsevolod I of Kiev:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vsevolod_I,_Prince_of_Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod I Yaroslavich (Ukrainian and Russian: Всеволод I Ярославич), (1030 – 13 April 1093, died at Vyshhorod near Kiev) ruled as Grand Prince of Kiev from 1078 until his death. (Russian Wikipedia notes that he was the first to h
2 CONC ave printed the title "Prince of All Russia")
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the fourth and favourite son of Yaroslav I the Wise by Ingigerd Olafsdottir.
2 CONT
2 CONT To back up an armistice signed with the Byzantine Empire in 1046, his father married him to a daughter of Emperor Constantine IX Monomachos, Anastasia, a princess, d. 1067. The couple had a son, the future Vladimir Monomakh.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his father's death in 1054, he received in appanage the towns of Pereyaslav, Rostov, Suzdal, and the township of Beloozero which would remain in possession of his descendants until the end of Middle Ages. Together with his el
2 CONC der brothers Iziaslav and Sviatoslav he formed a sort of princely triumvirate which jointly waged war on the steppe nomads, polovtsy, and compiled the first East Slavic law code. (Russian Wikipedia notes that the Pereyaslavskaya a
2 CONC nd Chernihiv dioceses increased during this period before becoming archdioceses.)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1067 Vsevolod's Greek wife died and he soon married a Kypchak princess, Anna. She brought him another son, who drowned after the Battle of the Stugna River, and two daughters, one becoming a nun and another, Eupraxia of Kiev, m
2 CONC arrying Emperor Henry IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT (The breakup of the Triumvirate is noted in Russian Wikipedia as taking place in the 1070s. Svyatoslav took Kiev in 1073, and Vsevolod received several domains that were redistributed to him.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon Sviatoslav's sudden death in December 1076, Vsevolod inherited the Kievan throne, but ceded it to the banished Iziaslav in return for his patrimony of Chernigov. But Iziaslav died two years later (October 3, 1078 in the Battl
2 CONC e of Nezhatina Meadows against exiles Oleg Svyatoslavich and Boris Vyacheslavich), and Vsevolod took the Kievan throne yet again.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Russian Wikipedia notes: In 1079, Oleg and his brother Roman moved back from Tmutarakan near present Kerch to Kiev, but Vsevolod paid the Polovechens to kill the two. Tmutarakan was restored to Kiev's control.)
2 CONT
2 CONT (Russia Wikipedia notes that his reign was marred by attacks by the Polovtsy and constant internecine wars between nephews and cousins among Vsevolod's grandchildren. This was the result of the imperfect law of inheritance establi
2 CONC shed by Yaroslav the Wise. During his reign, Rostislav's sons were particularly active - these were the grandsons of the eldest son of Yaroslav the Wise, Vladimir. They were exiled and prohibited from receiving principalities, so 
2 CONC they were constantly trying to take by force one city or another. Vsevolod behaved as a weak leader, failing to put down much of the strife amidst all the younger warriors. Nonetheless, compared to what was coming up in the 1090s,
2 CONC  his reign was considered relatively stable.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod was versed in Greek learning and spoke five languages (Swedish as his mother's language, Greek from his wife, possibly English, Russian, and Polovtsy). Since he lost most of his battles, his eldest son, Vladimir Monomakh,
2 CONC  a grand and famous warrior, did most of the fighting for his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Last years of his reign were clouded by grave illness, and Vladimir Monomakh presided over the government.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Russian Wikipedia notes that foreign policy under Vsevolod was marked by intensified relations with the Holy Roman Empire. Emperor Henry IV married his daughter Yevpraxia/Adelheid. The transition of Russia into an enemy of the Em
2 CONC pire was probably associated with the scandalous conflict between Yevpraxia and Henry: Vsevolod's daughter fled from Germany to Verona, and appearing before Pope Urban II, she accused her husband of abusing her, hosting orgies, an
2 CONC d participation in Satanic rituals.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod and his first wife Anastasia (daughter of Constantine IX Monomachos - Ben notes that FMG found that this was unlikely, but she may still have been somehow related, she died in 1067) had only one known son:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Vladimir II Monomakh (1053 - 19 May 1125).
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod and his second wife (a Polovtsian princess who died in 1111) had five known children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Rostislav Vsevolodovich (1070 - 26 May 1093). Drowned while retreating from the Battle of the Stugna River.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Eupraxia of Kiev (1071 - 20 July 1109). Married Henry IV, Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Catherine Vsevolovna (d. 11 August 1108). A nun. Her date of death is recorded in the Primary Chronicle.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Maria Vsevolodovna (d. 1089).
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Anna Vsevolodovna (d. 3 November 1112). Abbess. Visited Constantinople in 1089.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Records at St. Sophia Cathedral in Kiev note that Dmytro, a militiaman of Vsevolod, wrote that a "good Russian prince" died on Wednesday before dinner and was buried the next day, Maundy Thursday, April 14, 1093.)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vsevolod_of_Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod I of Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Vsevolod of Kiev)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod I
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince of Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign 	1078-1093
2 CONT
2 CONT Full name 	Vsevolod Yaroslavovich (Andrei)
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles 	Prince of Pereyaslav (1054-1073) and
2 CONT
2 CONT Chernigov (1073-1078)
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	~1030
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	April 13, 1093 [aged ~63]
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor 	Izyaslav I
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor 	Sviatopolk II
2 CONT
2 CONT Wives 	Anastasia (?-1067)
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna, a daughter of the Cuman Khan (?-1111)
2 CONT
2 CONT Offspring
2 CONT
2 CONT with Anastasia: Vladimir, Ionna
2 CONT
2 CONT with Anna: Eupraxia, Rostislav, Catherine, Maria
2 CONT
2 CONT Dynasty 	Rurik Dynasty
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Yaroslav the Wise
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Irene (a daughter of Olof)
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod I Yaroslavich (Ukrainian and Russian: Всеволод I Ярославич), (1030 – 13 April 1093) ruled as Grand Prince of Kiev from 1078 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the fourth and favourite son of Yaroslav I the Wise by Ingigerd Olafsdottir.
2 CONT
2 CONT To back up an armistice signed with the Byzantine Empire in 1046, his father married him to a daughter of Emperor Constantine IX Monomachos, Anastasia, a princess, d. 1067. The couple had a son, the future Vladimir Monomakh.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his father's death in 1054, he received in appanage the towns of Pereyaslav, Rostov, Suzdal, and the township of Beloozero which would remain in possession of his descendants until the end of Middle Ages. Together with his el
2 CONC der brothers Iziaslav and Sviatoslav he formed a sort of princely triumvirate which jointly waged war on the steppe nomads, polovtsy, and compiled the first East Slavic law code. In 1067 Vsevolod's Greek wife died and he soon marr
2 CONC ied a Kypchak princess, Anna. She brought him another son, who drowned after the Battle of the Stugna River, and two daughters, one becoming a nun and another, Eupraxia of Kiev, marrying Emperor Henry IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kievan court in the times of Vsevolod I
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Reign
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon Sviatoslav's death in 1076, Vsevolod inherited the Kievan throne, but ceded it to the banished Iziaslav in return for his patrimony of Chernigov. But Iziaslav died two years later, and Vsevolod took the Kievan throne yet agai
2 CONC n. Vsevolod was versed in Greek learning and spoke five languages. Since he lost most of his battles, his eldest son, Vladimir Monomakh, a grand and famous warrior, did most of the fighting for his father. Last years of his reign 
2 CONC were clouded by grave illness, and Vladimir Monomakh presided over the government.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod and his first wife Anastasia (daughter of Constantine IX Monomachos) had only one known son:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Vladimir II Monomakh (1053 - 19 May 1125).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anna Vsevolodovna (d. 3 November 1112). Abbess. Visited Constantinople in 1089.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vsevolod and his second wife had five known children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Rostislav Vsevolodovich (1070 - 26 May 1093). Drowned while retreating from the Battle of the Stugna River.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Eupraxia of Kiev (1071 - 20 July 1109). Married Henry IV, Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Catherine Vsevolovna (d. 11 August 1108). A nun. Her date of death is recorded in the Primary Chronicle.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Maria Vsevolodovna (d. 1089).
2 CONT
