2 CONT
2 CONT Ben M. Angel's summary:
2 CONT
2 CONT Relationships:
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Ярослав Владимирович "Мудрий" (Yaroslav I "The Wise"), Grand Prince of Kiev (1019-1054)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Ингегерда (Ingegerd Olofsdotter), Grand Princess of Kiev (1019-1050)
2 CONT
2 CONT Half-Sibling:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Ilya Yaroslavich, Prince of Novgorod (1015-1020)
2 CONT
2 CONT Siblings:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Vladimir Yaroslavich (b.1020, d. 1052), possibly married to Oda von Stade (famously unverified)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Anastasiya Yaroslavna (b. c1023, d. 1074/1096), wife of King Andras I of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Yeliziveta Yaroslavna (d. 1066), wife of King Harald III "Hardråde" of Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Svyatoslav Yaroslavich (b. 1027, d. 1076), Prince of Chernigov after 1054, successor to Izyaslav as Grand Prince of Kiev (1073-1076)
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Vsevolod Yaroslavich (b. 1030, d. 1093), Prince of Pereyaslavl after 1054, successor to Svyatoslav as Grand Prince of Kiev (1076-1093)
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Vyacheslav Yaroslavich (b. 1033/1036, d. 1055/1057), Prince of Smolensk (1054-1055/1057)
2 CONT
2 CONT 9. Anna Yaroslavna (b. 1036, d. 1075/1078), wife of King Henri I of France
2 CONT
2 CONT 10. Igor Yaroslavich (b. 1036 d. 1060), Prince of Volynia (1054-1060), Prince of Smolensk (1057-1060)
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertruda of Poland (Гертруда Польская), (b. 1025, d. 1108)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Mstislav Izyaslavich (d.1069), Prince of Novgorod (1066/1067), Prince of Polotsk (1069)
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Yaropolk Pyotr Izyaslavich (d. 1086), Prince of Volynia and maybe Turov (1078-1086), husband of Kunigunde von Weimar
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Svyatopolk II Mikhail Izyaslavich (b. 1050, d. 1113) Prince of Polotsk (1069-1093), Grand Prince of Kiev (1093-1113)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Eudokhia Izyaslavna (d. 1089), wife of Mieszko of Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Basic information and justifications:
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 1024 - Russian Wikipedia favors Novgorod.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 3 October 1078 - Battle of Nezhatin Niva, outside of Chernigov (died in battle - pierced in the shoulder by a spear)
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Десятинная Церковь, Church of the Tithes or Dormition of the Virgin (Desyatina Tserkva) on Starokiev Hill (Старокиевськой горе) in Kiev
2 CONT
2 CONT Wedding: c.1043 - location unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation: Prince of Turov to 1052, Prince of Novgorod (1052-1054), Grand Prince of Kiev (20 February 1054-1073, 27 December 1076-3 October 1078)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation of Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid (covering his birth family):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#IziaslavIaroslavichdied1078A
2 CONT
2 CONT IAROSLAV I 1019-1054
2 CONT
2 CONT IAROSLAV Vladimirovich, son of VLADIMIR I "Velikiy/the Great" Grand Prince of Kiev & his first wife Rognoda of Polotsk ([978]-Vyshgorod 20 Feb 1054, bur Kiev Church of St Sophia).
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Rostov 988-1010.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Novgorod 1013-1015.
2 CONT
2 CONT He rebelled against his father and refused to pay tribute from Novgorod in 1014.  Challenged by his cousin Sviatopolk I Grand Prince of Kiev, following the latter's accession in Kiev, he defeated Sviatopolk at Liubech [1016] and f
2 CONC orced him to flee to Poland, succeeding as IAROSLAV I "Mudriy/the Wise" Grand Prince of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatopolk returned in Jul 1018 with an army led by his father-in-law, Bolesław I King of Poland, and defeated Iaroslav at the Western Bug and forced him to retire to Novgorod.  Sviatopolk entered Kiev again 14 Aug 1018 and resume
2 CONC d his rule.  After King Bolesław returned to Poland, Iaroslav advanced once more on Kiev.  Sviatopolk fled south to raise another force with the Pechenegs but was finally defeated by the river Alta in 1019, when Iaroslav resumed p
2 CONC ower in Kiev[140].
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1024, his half-brother Mstislav Prince of Tmutorokan moved his headquarters north to Chernigov to challenge Iaroslav, whom he defeated at Listven.  The half-brothers agreed a division of territories, Iaroslav taking the land on
2 CONC  the western bank of the Dnieper including Kiev and Novgorod.  While Iaroslav was absent in Novgorod, the Pechenegs laid siege to Kiev, but were defeated after Iaroslav returned with a formidable army to relieve the siege[141]. He
2 CONC  succeeded in the territories of his half-brother Mstislav on the latter's death in 1036, becoming sole ruler or "Autocrat of the land of the Rus"[142].
2 CONT
2 CONT His greatest project was the conquest of Constantinople but his armies were defeated in decisive battles in 1043[143].
2 CONT
2 CONT He supervised the rapid expansion of the city of Kiev, modelled on Constantinople.  His law code Rus'ka Pravda also contributed to the development and consolidation of his administration especially in the area around Kiev, althoug
2 CONC h it appears to have had little impact in the north[144].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of Iaroslav 19 Feb 1054 at Vyshgorod aged 76 and his burial place[145].  He left a testament dividing his territories between his sons, the substance of which is recorded in the Primary Chro
2 CONC nicle[146] although no original text has survived.
2 CONT
2 CONT [m firstly ---.  The fact of Iaroslav´s first marriage is indicated by the chronology of his oldest son, although as noted below no primary source has yet been identified which refers to this oldest son.  Nevertheless, Iaroslav´s 
2 CONC birth date, if accurate as shown above, does suggest that an earlier marriage is likely.]
2 CONT
2 CONT m [secondly] (1019) INGIGERD Olafsdottir of Sweden, daughter of OLOF "Skotkonung" King of Sweden & his wife Estrid of the Obotriten ([1000/03]-10 Feb 1050).
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam of Bremen names "filius Iacobus et filia Ingrad" as the children of "Olaph rex Sueonum" & his wife Estred, specifying that Ingrad married "rex sanctus Gerzlef de Ruzzia"[147].  Her birth date range is estimated based on the b
2 CONC irth of her oldest child in 1020, and her youngest known child in [1036].  Morkinskinna names “Queen Ingigerđr the daughter of King Óláfr the Swede” as wife of “King Yaroslav [of] Russia”[148].  Snorre records the betrothal of "In
2 CONC gegerd the king's daughter" and "King Jarisleif…from Russia"[149].
2 CONT
2 CONT Her birth date range is estimated based on the birth of her oldest child in 1020, and her youngest known child in [1036].  The Historia Norwegie records the marriage of "sororem Olaui Sueonensis…Margaretam" and "rex Iarezlafus de 
2 CONC Ruscia" at her brother's instigation, after her betrothal to Olav of Norway was terminated[150].  It is more probable that she was the daughter rather than sister of King Olof if it is correct that she had ten children by her husb
2 CONC and.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is referred to as IRINA in Russian sources[151].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of "the Princess wife of Yaroslav" 10 Feb [1048/50][152].
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Iaroslav & his first wife had one child:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  [ILIYA Iaroslavich (-1020).
2 CONT
2 CONT Baumgarten names him but cites only one secondary source in support[153].  He is not named in the Primary Chronicle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Novgorod 1015.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Iaroslav & his second wife had ten children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  VLADIMIR Iaroslavich (1020-Novgorod 1052, bur Novgorod Church of St Sophia).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Vladimir son of Iaroslav in 1020[154].  Snorre names "Valdemar, Vissivald and Holte the Bold" as the children of "King Jarisleif" & his wife[155].
2 CONT
2 CONT His father installed him in 1043 as Prince of Novgorod.  He subjugated the Finnic tribe of Yam to secure Novgorod's control over the Gulf of Finland[156].
2 CONT
2 CONT Psellos records that Vladimir led an attack on Constantinople in [1045], apparently in revenge for the death of some Scythian noble in a brawl, but was defeated and forced to retreat[157].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of Vladimir "Yaroslav's eldest son" at Novgorod in 1052 and his place of burial[158].  As he predeceased his father, his descendants were ignored in the latter's testament and were subsequen
2 CONC tly considered ineligible to succeed as Grand Princes of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT m --- ([1022]-1066).  The name of Vladimir´s wife is not known.  Baumgarten identifies the wife of Vladimir as Oda, daughter of Luitpold Graf von Stade & his wife Ida von Elstorf [Brunswick][159].  This is based partially on the A
2 CONC nnales Stadenses which name "Odam sanctimonialem de Rinthelen" as daughter of "Lippoldo filio domiine Glismodis" and "Ida [de Elsthorpe]", specifying that she married "regi Ruzie" by whom she had a son "Warteslaw", returned to Sax
2 CONC ony with her son after her husband died[160].
2 CONT
2 CONT Vladimir & his wife had one child, Rostislav, Prince of Rostov, Novgorod, and Volynia (1056-1064), Prince of Tmutorokan (1064-1065).
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  ANASTASIA Iaroslavna ([1023]-[1074/1096], bur Admont Abbey).
2 CONT
2 CONT Baumgarten names the second wife of King András and gives her origin but only cites one secondary source in support[163].  The primary source which confirms her parentage and marriage has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband's death in 1060, she and her sons took refuge at the court of Heinrich IV King of Germany but, leaving her elder son there, she and her younger son went to Austria[164].  The Annals of Lambert record that "regina
2 CONC  Ungariorum, mater Salomonis regis" presented the sword of "rex Hunnorum Attila" to "duci Baioriorum Ottoni" after her son was restored as king of Hungary[165].
2 CONT
2 CONT She became a nun at Admont in 1074 as AGMUNDA.
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([1039]) as his second wife, ANDRÁS of Hungary, son of VÁSZOLY [Vazúl] Prince of Hungary Duke between March and Gran & --- of the Bulgarians ([1014]-Zirc autumn 1060, bur Tihany, Abbey of St Anian).  He succeeded in 1047 as ANDR
2 CONC ÁS I "the Catholic" King of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. ''' IZIASLAV Iaroslavich (1024-killed in battle Nezhatinaia Niva 3 Oct 1078 bur Kiev).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Izyaslav "a second son" to Vladimir in 1024[166].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded in 1054 as IZIASLAV I Grand Prince of Kiev.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  IELIZAVETA Iaroslavna (-after 25 Sep 1066).
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre records the marriage of King Harald and Elisabeth "called by the Northmen Ellisif" daughter of "King Jarisleif", specifying in a later passage that "the Swedish king Olaf…was brother of [her] mother"[167].  Morkinskinna rec
2 CONC ords the marriage of “Haraldr Sigurdarson” and “King Yaroslav and Queen Ingigerdr…daughter…Elisabeth, the Norsemen call her Ellisif”[168].  Snorre records that "Queen Ellisif came from the West…with her stepson Olaf and her daught
2 CONC er Ingegerd" after her husband was killed[169].
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1044) HARALD Sigurdson, son of SIGURD Syr King of Ringeringe & his wife Asta Gudbrandsdatter (1015-killed in battle Stamfordbridge 25 Sep 1066).  He succeeded in 1047 as HARALD III "Hardråde" King of Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.   SVIATOSLAV Iaroslavich (1027-27 Dec 1076, bur Chernigov, Church of the Saviour).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Svyatoslav "a third son" to Vladimir in 1027[170].  He succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Chernigov, and in 1073 as SVIATOSLAV II Grand Prince of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.  VSEVOLOD Iaroslavich (1030-13 Apr 1093, bur Kiev St Sofia).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Vsevolod "a fourth son" to Vladimir in 1030[171].  Snorre names "Valdemar, Vissivald and Holte the Bold" as the children of "King Jarisleif" & his wife[172].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Pereyaslavl, and in 1076 as VSEVOLOD I Grand Prince of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.  VIACHESLAV Iaroslavich ([1033/36]-Smolensk [1055/57]).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Vyacheslav son of Vladimir in [1034/36][173].  In accordance with the terms of his father's testament, he succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Smolensk.  The Primary Chronicle records the death
2 CONC  of Vyacheslav son of Yaroslav at Smolensk in [1056/57][174].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---.  The name of Viacheslav´s wife is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Viacheslav & his wife had one child, Boris, Prince of Chernigov (1077) and Prince of Tmutorokan (1077-1078)
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.   ANNA Iaroslavna (1036-5 Sep ([1075/78], bur Abbaye Villiers near La-Ferté-Alais).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Liber Modernorum Regum Francorum records the marriage of "filiam regis Russorum Annam" with King Henri[176].  The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Anna filia Georgii regis Sclavonum" as wife of King Henri[177].
2 CONT
2 CONT She was consecrated Queen Consort at Reims on her wedding day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Anna caused a scandal in France by her second marriage and was forced to leave the court, although she returned after his death in 1074[178].  The Liber Modernorum Regum Francorum records the marriage of "Anna, Henrici relic
2 CONC ta" and "Rodulfo comitis"[179].  "Rodulphus…Ambianensis comes" donated property to Sainte-Marie d'Amiens, with the consent of "Simon filius meus et Gualterus, Gualteri Tyrelli natus", by charter dated 1069, subscribed by "Anna uxo
2 CONC r eius"[180].
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (Reims 19 May 1051) as his second wife, HENRI I King of France, son of ROBERT II "le Pieux" King of France & his third wife Constance d'Arles [Provence] ([end 1009/May 1010]-Palais de Vitry-aux-Loges, forêt d’Orléans, Lo
2 CONC iret 4 Aug 1060, bur église de l'Abbaye royale de Saint-Denis).
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([1061]) as his third wife, RAOUL III “le Grand” Comte de Valois, son of RAOUL II Comte de Valois & his wife Aliénor de Champagne (-Péronne 23 Feb or 8 Sep 1074, bur Montdidier, later transferred to Crépy-en-Valois, égl
2 CONC ise collégiale Saint-Arnoul).
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.   IGOR Iaroslavich ([1036]-1060).
2 CONT
2 CONT In accordance with the terms of his father's testament, he succeeded in 1054 as Prince of Volynia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his brother Viacheslav in 1057 as Prince of Smolensk.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the death of Igor son of Yaroslav in 1060[181].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---.  The name of Igor´s wife is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Igor & his wife had two children: David, Prince of Volynia (1085-1086, 1087-1113), Prince of Dorogobuzh (1100-1113).
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Foundation of Medieval Genealogy page on Russia Rurikid (covering his family by marriage):
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#IziaslavIaroslavichdied1078B
2 CONT
2 CONT C.  GRAND PRINCES of KIEV, descendants of IZIASLAV I Grand Prince of Kiev (third son of IAROSLAV I)
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Iziaslav was his father's oldest surviving son, and succeeded as Grand Prince of Kiev in 1054 in accordance with his father's testament, only one of his sons, Sviatopolk II, succeeding as Grand Prince and none of his gran
2 CONC dsons.  In accordance with the family tradition, all later descendants were thereby effectively excluded from the succession.
2 CONT
2 CONT IZIASLAV I 1054-1078
2 CONT
2 CONT IZIASLAV Iaroslavich, son of IAROSLAV I "Mudriy/the Wise" Grand Prince of Kiev & his [second] wife Ingigerd Olafsdottir of Sweden (1024-killed in battle Nezhatinaia Niva 3 Oct 1078, bur Kiev Church of the Holy Virgin).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle records the birth of Izyaslav "a second son" to Vladimir in 1024[192].
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Turov.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded on the death of his older brother in 1052 as Prince of Novgorod.  In accordance with the terms of his father's testament, he succeeded in 1054 as IZIASLAV I Grand Prince of Kiev.  It is also likely that his territorie
2 CONC s included Novgorod and Pskov[193].
2 CONT
2 CONT He was deposed in Kiev by a mob which, dissatisfied with his refusal to attack the Kuman [Cumans], released Vseslav Prince of Polotsk from prison and proclaimed him Grand Prince of Kiev in Sep 1068, forcing Iziaslav to flee to Pol
2 CONC and[194].
2 CONT
2 CONT Iziaslav I was restored in 1069 with the help of Bolesław II King of Poland[195].
2 CONT
2 CONT He was deposed again in Kiev in 1073 by his younger brother Sviatoslav, restored again in 1077 after Sviatoslav died.
2 CONT
2 CONT He answered the call from his brother Prince Vsevolod for military support against their nephew Oleg Sviatoslavich, whom they defeated 3 Oct 1078 at Nezhatin Meadow near Chernigov.  The Primary Chronicle records the death of Izyas
2 CONC lav son of Iaroslav in battle on the meadow of Nezhata 3 Oct 1078 and his place of burial[196].
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([1043]) GERTRUDA of Poland, daughter of MIESZKO II LAMBERT King of Poland & his wife Richeza [Ezzonen] ([1020]-4 Jan 1107).
2 CONT
2 CONT Baumgarten names the wife of Grand Prince Iziaslav and gives her origin, citing primary sources in support[197].  The Primary Chronicle records that "the Princess, Svyatopolk's mother" died 4 Jan 1107[198].
2 CONT
2 CONT Grand Prince Iziaslav I & his wife had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  MSTISLAV Iziaslavich (-1069).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Mstislav son of Izyaslav, recording that his father established him as Prince of Polotsk in 1069 but that "he soon died"[199].
2 CONT
2 CONT He was installed as Prince of Novgorod by his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was expelled from Novgorod by Vseslav Prince of Polotsk in [1066/67].  He was not restored in Novgorod when his father returned from exile in Poland, but was replaced by Gleb Sviatoslavich.
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---.  The name of Mstislav´s wife is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mstislav & his wife had one child, Rostislav.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  IAROPOLK PIOTR Iziaslavich (-murdered 22 Nov 1086).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Iaropolk, son of Izyaslav, recording that he and his father attacked Chernigov together in 1078[201].
2 CONT
2 CONT He was installed as Prince of Volynia and, maybe, Turov by his uncle Vsevolod Grand Prince of Kiev in 1078, but was expelled in 1085 by his cousin Vladimir "Monomakh" and found refuge in Poland.  He was restored the following year
2 CONC  but murdered soon after.
2 CONT
2 CONT m ([1073]) as her first husband, KUNIGUNDE von Weimar, daughter of OTTO Graf von Weimar Markgraf of Meissen & his wife Adela de Louvain (-8 Jun 1140).  The Annalista Saxo names (in order) "Odam, Cunigundam, Adelheidam" as the thre
2 CONC e daughters of Markgraf Otto & his wife, specifying that Kunigunde married firstly "regi Ruzorum", secondly "Cononi comiti de Bichlingge, filio ducis Ottonis de Northeim", and thirdly "Wipertus senior"[202].  The primary source wh
2 CONC ich identifies her first husband more precisely has not yet been identified.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baumgarten records her first marriage but cites only one secondary source in support[203].  She married secondly Kuno von Northeim Graf von Beichlingen and thirdly (1110) as his second wife, Wiprecht [II] von Groitsch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Iaropolk & his wife had four children: Anastasia, wife of Gleb, Prince of Minsk; Yaroslav (died imprisoned); Unknown Daughter, wife of Gunther I, Graf von Schwarzburg; and Vyacheslav.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  SVIATOPOLK MIKHAIL Iziaslavich (1050-16 Apr 1113).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Primary Chronicle names Svyatopolk son of Iziaslav, recording that his father established him as Prince of Polotsk in 1069 after the death of his brother Mstislav[208].
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his uncle in 1093 as SVIATOPOLK II Grand Prince of Kiev.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  [EUDOXIA] Iziaslavna (-1089).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Annales Capituli Cracoviensis record that in 1088 "Mesko uxorem duxit" without naming his wife[209].  Baumgarten records the parentage of the wife of Mieszko and suggests that her name was Eudoxia, citing primary sources in su
2 CONC pport[210].
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1088) MIESZKO Prince of Poland, son of BOLESŁAW II "Szczodry/the Liberal" King of Poland & his wife Vizeslava Sviatoslavna of Kiev (1069-1089).
2 CONT
