2 CONT From the Kievan Rus database maintained by Bonnie Smyre:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.unc.edu/~smyre/rus/Monastery_Monastery_of_the_Caves.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Monastery: Monastery of the Caves
2 CONT
2 CONT Relations with princes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Regular intercourse was established between the Monastery and the prince's court.
2 CONT
2 CONT From the moment of its foundation the monastery supported Yaroslav's efforts to secure the independence of the Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Izyaslav Yaroslavich was wont to come to the monastery with his men-at-arms to ask Anthony for his prayers and blessing before taking any steps of political importance.
2 CONT
2 CONT The monastery and probably Anthony himself participated in the uprising of of the people of Kiev against Izyaslav Yaroslavich in 1068.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Izyaslav, with the help of Polish feudal lords, regained the Kiev throne, Anthony, fearing the prince's wrath, was compelled to leave the city by night. He was taken away by Sviatoslav Yaroslavich.
2 CONT
2 CONT Feodosij likewise did not remain passive, although his attitude towards Izyaslav Yaroslavich was different. He recognized Izyaslav, but he was uneasy about Izyaslav's attraction to Catholicism and Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Feodosij was a very staunch defender of Izyaslav's rights to the Kiev throne when it was occupied by his brother Sviatoslav. Relations between Feodosij and the court were almost severed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sviatoslav was on the point of imprisoning Feodosij. Feodosij was saved by the intervention of the notables and the brethren. Feodosij made his peace with Sviatoslav and allowed him to be mentioned in the church services, but only
2 CONC  after Izyaslav.
2 CONT
2 CONT 12th Century: The death of Gleb Vseslavich's widow was marked, the chronicle reveals, by an enormous contribution of land and money to Pechera Monastery. Her father, Yaropolk Izyaslavich, who died in 1087, gave the monastery the v
2 CONC olosts of Nebl, Derevskaya and Lutsk, and those "near Kiev."
2 CONT
