2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://diasporiana.org.ua/wp-content/uploads/books/28368/file.pdf Harald III Sigurdsson Hardrade]
2 CONT
2 CONT Harald III Sigurdsson Hardrade-ruled from 1047 to 1066. Harald Hardrade was one of Norway's most colorful rulers, and Snori's sagas made the most of his adventuresome exploits. Since there are so many King Haralds in Norwegian his
2 CONC tory, it is a good thing that posterity has accorded them surnames. Harald I was called the Fairhaired; Harald II was called Graycloak, and Harald III is known as Härdräde, which means Hard Ruler. Since his father was Sigurd Syr, 
2 CONC he was also called Harald Sigurdsson. Harald III grew up with his half brother St. Olof. Both had the same mother-Asta Gudbrandsdotter-and both were descended from Harald I the Fairhaired through their fathers. Olofs father Harald
2 CONC  Gränske was a great-grandson of Harald I through Björn, and Harald IlI's father Sigurd was also a great-grandson of Harald I, through Sigurd Rese. (The interested reader can check this out on Chart 1.) The half brothers were devo
2 CONC ted to each other, and Harald fought loyally beside Olof in the Battle of Stiklestad in 1030. When Olof was killed, Harald, still in his teens, fled abroad. During the next fourteen years Harald traveled widely. His first stop was
2 CONC  in Sweden. He gathered some of Olof's men who had escaped from the battle, and together they traveled to the court of Yaroslav the Wise. Harald was made captain of the Russian royal guard and distinguished himself in the Polish c
2 CONC ampaign of 1031. After three years, Harald and 500 of his warriors sailed down the Dnieper to Constantinople. There they enlisted in the service of the Empress Zoe, who ruled from 1028 to 1052. Zoe is a fascinating subject in her 
2 CONC own right for the student of medieval history. Harald got such high marks in the service of the Empress that he was soon made leader of the whole Varangian guard (the Scandinavian mercenaries of Byzantium). Harald is credited with
2 CONC  leading the Varangians to many victories throughout the Mediterranean. If we are to believe Snori, Harald conquered eighty Saracen cities in North Africa and also vanquished Sicily and Jerusalem. He bathed in the Jordan, made gre
2 CONC at gifts to the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, and rid Palestine of robbers.
2 CONT
2 CONT When he returned to Byzantium, it was rumored that he wished to marry the Empress. Her husband at the time (she had a succession of husbands) was Constantine Monamach. This man, we are told, became insanely jealous and had Harald 
2 CONC put into a dungeon. The Norwegian managed to escape, sailed north across the Black Sea, and returned up. the Dnieper to Kiev, where he once again was welcomed at Yaroslav's court. That winter (1045) Yaroslav gave his daughter Eliz
2 CONC abeth in marriage to Harald. Elizabeth's mother-a daughter of King Olof, Skötkonung-was Swedish, so the young couple shared a common Scandinavian heritage.nRetracing his steps, Harald came back to Sweden. From there, he made raids
2 CONC  upon Denmark and added more gold to the treasures he had heaped up in North Africa and elsewhere in the Mediterranean. By 1046, Harald was ready to return home to Norway. He had left as a fifteen-year-old youth, but he was return
2 CONC ing as a seasoned warrior, already past thirty.
2 CONT Harald's nephew, Magnus the Good, offered him half the kingdom in return for half the treasure he had amassed while abroad. With the blowing of trumpets, a great banquet, and much pomp and ceremony, Harald was given his royal titl
2 CONC e. In the presence of a great assembly, he then poured out his gold upon a large ox hide, weighed it carefully, and divided it with Magnus.mA year later, as we have seen, while Magnus and Harald were both in Denmark, Magnus fell f
2 CONC rom his horse and died. Harald was then made king over all Norway.mmHarald and his wife, Elizabeth of Kiev, had two daughters: Mariamand Ingegerd.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1048 Harald took a second wife. According to Snori, he did this without repudiating Elizabeth. Some modern historians doubt that the clergy of Norway would have allowed their king to live in open bigamy, so they assume that Sno
2 CONC ri was wrong. They surmise that Elizabeth must have died in Russia before Harald left there.mOn the other hand, it seems to us that Snorn could easily be right.mNorway had been "Christian" for only a few decades, and it is possibl
2 CONC e that the people and even their clergy would not have frowned upon a king's having more than one wife. Furthermore, most Scandinavian kings of Harald's time had their share of concubines or frillas, and these enjoyed many wifely 
2 CONC privileges, including the right of succession for their sons.mHarald's new wife was Tora, the daughter of Torberg Arnasson.mThey had two sons-Magnus and Olof-and we may assume that Tora patiently played second fiddle to Elizabeth 
2 CONC who continued to bear the title of queen.mHarald's long reign (nearly twenty vears) was marked by endless and seemingly pointless strife against the Danish king Sven Estridsson,
2 CONT
2 CONT
