2 CONC curately or not. The simplest account is that he was killed by his own men in Fettercairn, through the treachery of Finnguala (also called Fimberhele), daughter of Cuncar, Mormaer of Angus, in revenge for the killing of her only s
2 CONC on.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Prophecy of Berchán adds little to our knowledge, except that it names Kenneth "the kinslayer", and states he died in Strathmore.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kenneth's son Malcolm II (Máel Coluim mac Cináeda) was later king of Alba. Kenneth may have had a second son, named either Dúngal or Gille Coemgáin. Sources differ as to whether Boite mac Cináeda should be counted a son of Kenneth
2 CONC  II or of Kenneth III (Cináed mac Duib).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.rpi.edu/~holmes/Hobbies/Genealogy/ps05/ps05_447.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT He began his reign by ravaging the Britons, probably as an act of vengeance, but his name is also included among a group of northern and western kings said to have made submission to the Anglo-Saxon king Edgar in 973, perhaps at C
2 CONC hester; and the chronicler Roger of Wendover (Flores Historiarum, under the year 975) states that shortly afterward Kenneth received from Edgar all the land called Lothian (i.e., between the Tweed and the Forth rivers). This is th
2 CONC e first mention of the River Tweed as the recognized border between England and Scotland. Kenneth was slain, apparently by his own subjects, at Fettercairn in the Mearns. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Kenneth II reigned 971-995; he was murdered by his own men. He is said to have married a princess of Leinster.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
