2 CONC Duncan, pp.24–25. 7.Jump up ^ Duncan, pp. 21–22; ESSH, p. 484. 8.Jump up ^ See ESSH, pp. 483–484 & 495–502. 9.Jump up ^ The name of Cuncar's daughter is given as Fenella, Finele or Sibill in later sources. John of Fordun credits C
2 CONC onstantine III (Causantín mac Cuilén) and Kenneth III (Cináed mac Duib) with the planning, claiming that Kenneth II planned to change the laws of succession. See ESSH, pp. 512–515. 10.Jump up ^ ESSH, p. 516. 11.Jump up ^ Annals of
2 CONC  the Four Masters, s.a. 998: "Dúngal Cináed's son, was killed by Gille Coemgáin, Cináed's son." It is not clear if the Cináeds (Kenneths) referred to are Cináed mac Maíl Coluim (Kenneth II) or his nephew and namesake Cináed mac Du
2 CONC ib (Kenneth III). Smyth, pp. 221–222, makes Dúngal following ESSH p. 580. 12.Jump up ^ Compare Duncan, p.345 and Lynch (ed), Genealogies, at about p. 680. See also ESSH, p. 580.
