1 NOTE Kenneth III King of Scotland
2 CONT Buried Iona Argyllshire Scotland
2 CONT Reigned 971-995
1 NOTE Kenneth II (died 995), king of Scotland (971-95), the son of Malcolm I 
2 CONT MacDonald (reigned 943-54). From the beginning of his reign, Kenneth waged 
2 CONT war against the Saxons, particularly the earls of Northumbria, who 
2 CONT occupied southern Scotland, and the Scandinavian Vikings, who controlled 
2 CONT Scotland north of the Spey River. Although his wars were not successful, 
2 CONT Kenneth consolidated central Scotland into a strong kingdom. He was 
2 CONT murdered.
1 NOTE [FAVthomas.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT    Kenneth became King of Scots in 971, when his predecessor Cuilen waskilled by the Strathclyde Britons. There are some indications thatKenneth had a joint kingship with Cuilen's brother Olaf until 977, whenOlaf was killed. Kenne
2 CONC th led successful raids against Strathclyde in both971 and 972.
2 CONT    In 973, Kenneth attended a conference of kings at Chester, in England,which was probably organized by Edgar, King of England. Kenneth appearsto have had a quiet but successful reign and was killed 'by treachery' in995, and succ
2 CONC eeded by Constantine son of Cuilen, a distant cousin. It ispossible that Kenneth married an Irish woman from Leinster, as atwelfth-century Scottish poem calls his son Malcolm 'son of a woman ofthe Leinstermen'.King of Alba, said t
2 CONC o have married a princess ofLeinster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reigned from 971-995.REFN: 1320
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. Burke's Peerage 99th ed (GS #942 D22bup prefix p. 285-86)
2 CONT     2. Dict of Nat'l Biog (GS #920.042 D561n vol 30 p. 439-40)
2 CONT     3. A Vindication of Macbeth (GS #929.2706 M288c p. 7, 14)
2 CONT     4. Encyclopedia Britannica 1964 Ed (GS #032 En19b vol 13 p. 342)
2 CONT     5. Also searched without positive results: Scots Peerage, The Complete Peerage, Royal Dau of Engl, and Queens of Sctl and English Princes
1 NOTE From THE RUFUS PARKS PEDIGREE by Brian J.L. Berry,  chart pg 45.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 44:
2 CONT
2 CONT 18.     Kenneth II MacMalcolm (Cinaed), treacherously slain by his own subjects in the Mearns, Kincardineshire 995; second son; succeeded 971; received Lothian from King Edgar the Peaceful of England as vassal and suzerain, after 
2 CONC ravaging the land.  His chiefs were continuously engaged in a contest with Sigurd, Earl of Orkney, for the territory at the northern tip of Scotland.  The central districts, however, were consolidated during his reign.  His death 
2 CONC was brought about through an intrigue of Finella, dau. of Gunchar, a mormaor (provincial ruler) of the Earl of Angus.  He was bur. on Iona.  He was succeeded by two cousins in turn.
2 CONT
2 CONT !Availability: The libraries of Ken, Karen, Kristen, Kevin, Brian, Amie, Adam and FAL
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: "Ancestral Roots of Sixty Colonists" by Frederick Lewis Weis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 145 line (170-17):
2 CONT
2 CONT 17.   Cinaed (Kenneth), King of Scots, 971, killed by his own men.  (Berchan's Prophecy indicates that his wife was a Leinster woman.
1 NOTE Acceded 971-995. Killed by Penella, whose son he had put todeath.[large-G675.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT [Brøderbund WFT Vol. 2, Ed. 1, Tree #1990, Date of Import: 18 Jul 1996]
