1 OCCU Göternas jarl, Lagman
1 TITL Jarl
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Jarl Karl Sunesson Gautland av Västergötland=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Sune Ivarsson and Astrid Ogmundsdottir
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://theutenberg.org/vastergotlands-lagman-algotssoner-och-folkungar/Lagman Karl(e) of Edsvära is considered identical to the Earl of Västgötland Karl Sunesson]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The earl name Karl – mentioned both in the Icelandic sagas and in Saxo – is repeated in the lawman Karl(e) of Edsvära , who was mentioned above. He has been identified with some certainty as the West Goth Earl Karl Suneson , who i
2 CONC ntervened in the Norwegian throne struggles in 1137 after the death of King Harald Gill in 1136, and who in a border treaty with the same Harald Gill, dated to 1135, is even called “king” (“Karlle kononge” ). According to the avai
2 CONC lable biographical information about Earl Karl Suneson, he was married to the illegitimate daughter of the Norwegian King Harald Gill, Birgitta (Brigida) Haraldsdotter , who after her husband’s death first remarried the Swede who,
2 CONC  after the murder of Eric the Holy in Östra Aros in 1159 or 1160, elected King Magnus Henriksson and then to Earl Birger Brosa ( lily in his knightly seal ). The Norwegian king's daughter's children with Earl Karl Suneson/lawman K
2 CONC arl of Edsvära were thus half-siblings with her children with Earl Birger Brosa. Based on the term “king”, it can be noted that Karl Suneson's father Sune Ivarsson was supposed to have been the son and grandson of the great men Iv
2 CONC ar Sigtryggsson and “Sigtrygg in Närke” mentioned in Heimskringla's Olav the Holy saga . It is said that Norway's King Olaf Haraldsson “the fat one” (later the holy one) together with his consort Queen Astrid (Olof Skötkonung's da
2 CONC ughter) and his daughter Ulvhild, sometime at the beginning of the 11th century visited this great man Sigtrygg in Närke on his way to Gårdarike. Since he is mentioned by Snorri as a recipient of the Norwegian royal family, he mus
2 CONC t have had an elevated position in Sweden at the beginning of the 11th century.9  
2 CONT
2 CONT It thus seems as if Västergötland in ancient times was ruled by earls or kings. The term jarl may possibly have its roots in the early Norwegian office of earl , where primarily the so-called Lade-earls are to be mentioned. As ear
2 CONC ly as the 7th century, the word earl appears in Anglo-Saxon society , which has the same root as the word jarl . An earl or jarl was a ruler over one or more counties and held the highest social position under the king himself. Th
2 CONC e term jarl was used in a similar way in ancient Norwegian society, where the most famous earls were, as mentioned, the Lade-earls. The term earl/jarl would thus belong to the early Germanic system of government and designate the 
2 CONC ruler over a country or a province just below the "king level". Perhaps we can speak of a viceroy? In Latin, the word is rendered with dux, i.e. primarily duke. In my studies in the research project Between the Lily and the Seawee
2 CONC d, I have come to the conclusion that one can imagine a transition from the Herulian male name EirilaR, probably a high-ranking Herulian (military?) personality, to the designation jarl (earl). The Heruls were a tribe that during 
2 CONC the Migration Period was allowed to settle next to the Goths ( the Gautarna/Geaterna ). Perhaps this neighboring tribe's royal form of government with an EirilaR as military commander influenced the Gothic social structure – perha
2 CONC ps via the Norwegian jarls?10 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is noteworthy that Earl Karl Sunesson has been considered to be identifiable with the West Götaland lawman Karl(e) of Edsvära who was present at the same time , and that the same man is even called “king” in a border treaty wit
2 CONC h the Norwegian king. Karl Sunesson is stated to have been the son of Ivar Sunesson and Astrid Ogmundsdotter. While he was descended from the 11th century “Sigtrygg in Närke” on his father’s side, he had Norwegian roots on his mot
2 CONC her’s side. His mother Astrid Ogmundsdotter was the daughter of the Norwegian Ogmund Ormsson , who was the son of the Norwegian Earl Orm Eilivsson of the Norwegian Lade-jarlarnas family and his wife Sigrid Finnsdotter , a daughter
2 CONC  of the Norwegian Earl Finn Arnesson . The name Lade-jarlarna derives from the family’s estate Lade near Trondheim. According to Evert Melefors in his article Namnet Hâkon from 1991, among the oldest Norwegian bearers of the royal
2 CONC  name Håkon (Hâkon; Hanha-kuniR ) are both the Norwegian king Håkon Ædelsteinfostre or Håkon the Good , who was the son of the Norwegian founder Harald Hårfager (c. 890 – 940), and the first Lade-jarl Hákon Grjótgarðsson . The nam
2 CONC e Ædelsteinfostre comes from the fact that King Håkon was, according to tradition, raised by King Æthelstan of England, who reigned 924 – 939 and who is the first of the Germanic Anglo-Saxon royal dynasties to call himself rex tot
2 CONC ius Britanniæ , as king of all Britain. King Æthelstan was the grandson of King Alfred the Great of Wessex or 'West Saxon'. The Norwegian king Håkon Ædelsteinforstre's relationship to King Æthelstan points to the close family cont
2 CONC acts that existed between the Scandinavian and Anglo-Saxon kingdoms during the Viking Age. For the sake of order, it should be noted that there is a " thegn runestone " in Väne county, namely the runestone that stands at the Velan
2 CONC da farm in Väne-Åsaka parish. On the stone you can read the inscription " Tyrvi erected the stone after Ogmund, his husband, a very good thegn; Tor wige ". The name Ogmund was a name used especially in Norway during the Viking Age
2 CONC . The stone also reflects the Thor worship that was common in these areas.11 
2 CONT
2 CONT Above, the Götajarlar were mentioned, Svarin and Karl , the latter of whom is also mentioned in the Icelandic manuscript Morkinskinna . The Icelandic sagas mention no less than a dozen Götajarlar, namely:
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Ingemund , 8th century ( Landnámabók and Vatsdœla saga ),'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sigfastr Jarl , 8th century ( Landnámabók),'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Lodvir Jarl, 8th century (Gull-þori's saga),'''
2 CONT
2 CONT *''' Herrauðr Jarl, 8th century (Ragnar saga Lodbrókar),'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Ottar Jarl, 9th century (Fagrskinna),'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Harald Jarl, 9th century (Hardar saga ók Hólmverja),'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Sigurd Jarl, 9th-11th century (Gunnlaug saga ormstungu),'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Ragnvald Ulfsson Jarl, 11th century (Flateyarbók I and Heimskringla), King Stenkil's father,'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Valgaut Jarl, 11th century (Olafs saga hins Helga I and Flateyarbók I),'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Tufi Jarl, 11th century (Olaf's saga of Helga I, possibly Adam of Bremen 'Tuph ducis'),'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Karl Jarl, 11th century (Morkinskinna and Saxo). 12 ''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Although none of these Götajarlar, with the possible exception of Ragnvald, Tufi and Karl, can be proven as historically existing persons, it may, however, in view of what has been said here about the Herulian name EirilaR , be we
2 CONC ll worth noting the high frequency with which the word jarl occurs in both the Icelandic poets and in Saxo, as well as the fact that we see in the series of names two names Si-g-fast and Si-g-urd that would fit the image with the 
2 CONC si- rhyming udd sound that would be found in the names of the rulers of Götaland. The name Ingemund is one of the “parade names” of the early Middle Ages, when Ingemund , Hallsten and Ingevald are mentioned by tradition as the anc
2 CONC estors of the lily/lion- bearing Folkunga family , which was present in both Västergötland and Östergötland . A medieval genealogy about the ancestral roots of the Folkunga family tells the following in Olaus Petri’s version: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Hæc ex collectanijs M: Olaui Petri, quondam pastoris Stockholm: Folko Fylbyter fuit paganus, et genuit tres filios christianos , Ingemundum, Halstanum et Ingevaldum, qui genuit Folkonem Grossum patrem Benedicti Sniuils, qui Bened
2 CONC ictus genuit Birgerum ducem et Carolum ducem et Magnum dictum Minneskiold. Birgerus dux genuit Philippum ducem. Carolum dux genuit Ulphonem, Magnus Minneskiold genuit Hic Birgerum ducem. Waldemarum Regem et Ericum ducem et Benedic
2 CONC tum ducem Finlandiæ et Episcopum Lincopensem. Magnus Rex genuit Birgerum Regem, Ericum et Waldemarum duces. Ericus dux genuit Magnum Regem Suetiæ, Noruegiæ".
2 CONT
2 CONT Translated: "Folke Filbyter was a pagan and fathered three Christian sons Ingemund, Hallsten and Ingevald, who fathered Folke the Fat, who was the father of Bengt Sniuils. This Bengt fathered Duke Birger and Duke Karl and Magnus c
2 CONC alled Minneskiold. Duke Birger fathered Duke Philip. Duke Karl fathered Ulf and Magnus Minneskiöld fathered Duke Birger, who fathered King Valdemar, King Magnus, Duke Erik and Duke Bengt of Finland, also bishop of Linköping. King 
2 CONC Magnus fathered King Birger, Duke Erik and Duke Valdemar. Duke Erik fathered King Magnus of Sweden, Norway and Scania."
2 CONT
2 CONT However, nowhere can it be directly proven that the Göta Kingdom or Västergötland at this early time would have had an earl's office at all. Or, as I said, were the earliest lawmen of Västergötland equivalent to the earls? Bo Gräs
2 CONC lund says in his article Snorri, Ragnvald Ulfsson and Brunnsbo Storäng that the mention of Västergötland earls in the saga literature could very well reflect an unreflected use of Norwegian social terminology without actual citize
2 CONC nship in Västergötland. However, one could also see these circumstances as another factor that speaks for a stronger original community between Goths, Norwegians and Danes, a constantly current theme in my research on the oldest h
2 CONC istory of Västergötland.13
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id= 6000000225644189866&size=large}} 
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id= 6000000225644270870&size=large}} 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Image 2.''' From manuscript B 59 at the Royal Library. The two pages known as the Västergötlands lagmanslängd are depicted here , consisting of the notes added by the Vidhemsprästen to the Older Västgötalagen. The Vidhemspräste
2 CONC n begins with the pagan lagman Lum(ber) and ends with the lagman Folke who existed sometime between 1225 and around 1240. The Vidhemsprästen's text on the lagmans has been reproduced above in a more modern reading. Photo: KB photo
2 CONC  detail. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://theutenberg.org/vastergotlands-lagman-algotssoner-och-folkungar/Västergötland's Lawmen – Algotssöner and Folkungar]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Earl of West Göta, Karl Sunesson, was the great-grandson of the great man "Sigtrygg in Närke".'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''As can be seen from the table of the lawmen of Västergötland, the name of the 15th lawman was Sigtrygg Algotsson . He was the son of the 12th lawman Algot Karl(e)sson and the grandson of the 11th lawman Karl(e) of Edsvära or Ka
2 CONC rl Sunesson''' . As mentioned above, his father was Sune Ivarsson , who was the son and grandson of the great men Ivar Sigtryggsson and “Sigtrygg in Närke”, i.e. the “lord Sigtrygg” who at the beginning of the 11th century receive
2 CONC d the Norwegian king Olaf the fat (the holy) and his family on the journey to Gårdarike. It should be mentioned that the name Sigge is a short form or so-called hypocorism for names that begin with Sig-, for example Sigmund , Sigv
2 CONC ard , Sigsten and Sigtrygg. A grandfather or great-grandfather with, for example, the name Sigtrygg may well have been reflected in a grandson or granddaughter's name Sigge, which later name is therefore a short form of the name S
2 CONC igtrygg. When naming children, it was the "principle of invocation" that was the most prominent principle during this time. The meaning is that the names of older ancestors were "inherited" in the family and therefore recur in lin
2 CONC e after line within the family or family group. The "inherited" names can be derived on both the agnatic and cognatic side. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding the name Sigtrygg and its etymology, we can descend into the very oldest mythology, for Saxo tells in the Chronicle of the Kingdom of Denmark – in the first book of 'The Tale of Gram' – that the Swedish king Sigtrygg had
2 CONC  a daughter named Gro and that the earl of the Goths Svarin aspired to King Sigtrygg's Swedish kingdom. In the Poetic Edda – in the ' Second poem about Helge Hundingsbane' –  there is a story about a 'Svarin's mound' , where a chi
2 CONC eftain Svarin was supposed to lie buried. It is in connection with the name Sigryn and the place Arasten that 'Svarin's mound' is mentioned. Starkodder, who according to the Hervarar saga fought a battle at the falls in Stora Ed o
2 CONC r Trollhättan , was, according to the Poetic Edda, at home at 'Svarin's mound' , where he apparently lived. Snorri's Ynglingasaga also tells of Vendsyssel's king Sigtrygg. Vendsyssel was the northernmost part of Jutland, which, th
2 CONC rough archaeological finds, including the so-called king's mound in Hullsjö in Gärdhem parish, can be proven to have had early connections with the area around Hunneberg in Väne county. Just next to Hullsjö, the aforementioned gol
2 CONC d finds in Vittene were made a few years ago. In the family of the Norwegian founder of the kingdom, Harald Hårfager we find his daughter Álof (Olof) and his son Sigtrygg. Obviously the name Sigtrygg is a name that finds its roots
2 CONC  not only in the old Danish and Norwegian royal families but far down in the purely mythological contexts. Wasn't the name Sigtrygg a transliteration of Allfather himself, i.e. Odin? Just as the name Gaut (Al-gaut) was also a tran
2 CONC sliteration of Allfather himself.19
2 CONT
2 CONT In Harald Hårfager's saga in Heimskringla it is told how Harald Hårfager's sons, among them his son Sigtrygg , received Vingulmark, Raumarike, Vestfold and Telemark. It is also interesting to note that already in a society founded
2 CONC  by Norwegian Vikings in Ireland during the period 989 – 1029 there is found King Sigtrygg Silkskegg , who was the son of the king of Ireland 940 – 980 Olof Kurran and the woman Kormland. In an attack on Ireland, Njal's saga tells
2 CONC  us , King Sigtrygg and his mother Kormland had to ask for help from Earl Sigurd in the Orkney Islands. Since the Swedish savior Erengisle Suneson (Sune Jonsson's family; boat) , who is known from 1334 – 1392, calls himself Earl o
2 CONC f the Orkney Islands, it may be of interest to mention this.20  
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Sigtrygg is also found in the Danish regency list. A Danish king Sigtrygg was contemporary with the ruler of East Franconia, i.e. Germany, who goes by the name Louis the Fat (899 – 911). Louis the Fat was the son of Louis
2 CONC  the German and thus the great-grandson of Emperor Charlemagne . Among the last Carolingians one encounters names such as Charles the Bald , Charles the Fat , Charles the Fool , Louis the Blind, etc. It should be noted that empero
2 CONC rs, kings, rulers and other great men at this time were given epithets such as 'the bald', 'the fat', 'the blind', 'the pious', 'the foolish', etc. The same type of epithet is also connected with the ancestor of the Folkunga famil
2 CONC y, Folke the Fat , who for chronological reasons must be placed at the end of the 11th century and the first part of the 12th century. According to the genealogy given above, Folke the Fat (Folke Grossus) was the son of Ingevald a
2 CONC nd grandson of Folke Filbyter . When it comes to the question of “Folke Filbyter and his three Christian sons Ingemund, Halsten and Ingevald” – according to the genealogy given above – the question is whether we are still in the r
2 CONC eal historical layers or in the area of ​​mythology. However, the names Ingemund , Halsten and Ingevald are highly historical realities, as these names were borne by the very oldest saved. For example, there is the interesting lil
2 CONC y- bearing knight Halsten Petersson , mentioned in Njudung and in Flundre härad by the Göta älv 1329 – 1357, King Magnus Eriksson’s confidant.21 
2 CONT
2 CONT If one assumes that the genealogy of the Folkunga family presented above – in Olaus Petri's version – reflects a historical reality, then chronologically the three sons Ingemund , Hallsten and Ingevald would be in the time span of
2 CONC  the middle of the 11th century and onwards. The pagan Folke Filbyter could then, hypothetically, be thought to have lived during the beginning of the 11th century. The chronology is important in this context. King Knut Svensson t
2 CONC he Holy was killed in 1086, whereupon his presumably young daughters Ingegerd and Cecilia were taken to safety to Sweden, where they both then married, Ingegerd with Folke the Fat and Cecilia with Earl Erik of Göta. Based on this,
2 CONC  Ingegerd Knutsdotter and Folke the Fat should be placed in the time span 1075 –1150 and their son Bengt Snivel to around 1110 – 1170. If these time spans are reasonably accurate, Folke the Fat's father Ingevald should be placed i
2 CONC n the time span 1030 – 1080 and his supposed father Folke Filbyter in the time span late 9th – early 11th century. In and of itself, this is chronologically consistent with the 1029 mentioned great man 'Sigtrygg i Närke', who was 
2 CONC the great-grandfather of Earl Karl Sunesson/lawman Karl(e) of Edsvära, and he is probably related to both Sigtrygg Bengtsson's lily- bearing ancestors and the ancestors of the Algotssöns. 
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id= 6000000225645530833&size=large}} 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Image 7.''' According to the Vidhemsprästen, Folke den tjocke and Ingegärd Knutsdotter's grandson Magnus Minnesköld lived at Bjälbo in Göstrings district in Östergötland. If you read Snorri's Heimskringla, you might conclude th
2 CONC at his grandson, Earl Birger Brosa , Magnus Minnesköld's older brother, also lived at Bjälbo. The farm is assumed to have been an old chieftain's farm with a castle hall and defensive tower. The stone church in Bjälbo dates from t
2 CONC he second half of the 12th century, i.e. from Birgers Brosa's time. The tower has a slightly later date. However, there is no evidence anywhere that the brothers' grandfather Folke den tjocke and his wife Ingegärd Knutsdotter (dau
2 CONC ghter of King Knut the Holy) can be linked to Bjälbo. Photo: the author. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The designations “Folkungar” and “Folkungaätten” are ambiguous. The Folkungarna were a “political interest group” or a “political party”, usually in opposition to the monarchy. But the designations can also refer to a family, a fa
2 CONC mily clan or a dynasty, where it is the ancestor Folke who forms the root of the designation; thus “Folke’s descendants” ! Whether the mythical Folke Filbyter should be included in this context is uncertain, but Folke den tjöke ca
2 CONC n definitely be counted as the ancestor of the family or clan. As mentioned above, he lived during the first half of the 12th century.22 
2 CONT
2 CONT But did Folke and his wife Ingegärd Knutsdotter live in Bjälbo in Östergötland? Nothing is known about this. No sources tell us where Folke and Ingegärd lived. We do know that their grandson Magnus Minnesköld owned Bjälbo. If you 
2 CONC read Snorri Sturluson's Heimskringla , you might conclude that it may have been the family estate of Bjälbo that was also inhabited by his grandson Earl Birger Brosa – Magnus Minnesköld's older brother – during the second half of 
2 CONC the 12th century. Bjälbo can be assumed to have been an old chieftain's estate with a castle hall and defensive structures. The stone church in Bjälbo dates from the second half of the 12th century, i.e. from the time of Earl Birg
2 CONC er Brosa. The tower has a slightly later date. It is a strongly built tower with good defensive capabilities and with many rooms for living during the battles that must have raged around Bjälbo. The designation Bjälboätten is some
2 CONC times used synonymously with the designation Folkungaätten. The term Bjälboätten can very well be used if one refers to Magnus Minnesköld and his descendants. However, the term should hardly be used for the generations before Magn
2 CONC us Minnesköld, this is because it is not known where the earliest “folkunga generations” were settled and owned. As noted, an old and substantial landholding can be attested to the older ranks of the Folkungas in Västergötland, an
2 CONC d there are also indications of abundant landholding around Nydala Monastery by Lake Rusken in Njudung. The traditional terms “Folkungar” and “Folkungaätten” can still very well be used as designations for the descendants of Folke
2 CONC  and Ingegärd.23 
2 CONT
