2 CONT Helena of Rascia From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia For other uses, see Helena of Serbia and Ilona of Hungary (disambiguation). Helena of Rascia Chronicon Pictum P113 Az aradi véres gyűlés.JPG Helena, as depicted in Chronicon P
2 CONC ictum Queen consort of Hungary Tenure	1131–1141 Spouse	Béla II Issue Elisabeth, Duchess of Poland Géza II Ladislaus II Stephen IV Sophia House	Vukanović Father	Uroš I of Rascia Mother	Anna Diogene Born	after 1109 Died	after 1146 R
2 CONC eligion	Eastern Orthodoxy↓ Roman Catholicism Helena of Rascia (Serbian: Јелена/Jelena, Hungarian: Ilona királyné; b. after 1109 – after 1146) was Queen consort of Hungary through her marriage with Béla II, who ruled 1131–1141. Lif
2 CONC e[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena was the daughter of Duke Uroš I of Rascia and his wife, Anna Diogene-Vukanović. Around 1129, King Stephen II of Hungary arranged her marriage with his cousin Béla, who had been blinded on the order of the king's father, Kin
2 CONC g Coloman of Hungary. The king granted estates near Tolna to the couple. Following the childless king's death, her husband was crowned King of Hungary on 28 April 1131. Helena exerted material influence over her blind husband duri
2 CONC ng his reign. It was she who persuaded her husband's partisans, with her two sons in her arms, to massacre, at an assembly in Arad, 68 aristocrats they suspected of having suggested King Coloman blind her husband. When her husband
2 CONC  died on 13 February 1141, their eldest son Géza II ascended the throne while still a child. Therefore, Helena and her brother Beloš Vukanović, whom she had invited to the court, governed the Kingdom of Hungary till September 1146
2 CONC  when he came of age. Marriage and children[edit]
