2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: Although they were members of the Angelos family, they often bore other surnames. Rulers of Byzantium INDEX PAGE Last updated 8th December 2003
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Alexios III Angelos (c. 1153 – 1211) was Byzantine emperor from 1195 to 1203.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexios III Angelos was the second son of Andronicos Angelos and Euphrosyne Castamonitissa. Andronicus was himself a son of Theodora Comnene, the youngest daughter of Emperor Alexios I Komnenos and Irene Ducaena. Thus Alexius Ange
2 CONC lus was a member of the extended imperial family. Together with his father and brothers, Alexios had conspired against Emperor Andronikos I Komnenos (c. 1183), and thus he spent several years in exile in Muslim courts, including t
2 CONC hat of Saladin.
2 CONT
2 CONT His younger brother Isaac II Angelos, was threatened with execution under orders of their first cousin once removed Andronicos I Comnenos on September 11, 1185. Isaac made a desperate attack on the imperial agents and killed their
2 CONC  leader Stephanus Hagiochristophorites. He then took refuge in the church of Hagia Sophia and from there appealed to the populace. His actions provoked a riot, which resulted in the deposition of Andronicus I, and the proclamation
2 CONC  of Isaac II Angelus as emperor. Alexius was now closer to the imperial throne than ever before.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage to Euphrosyne Doucaena Camaterina Alexios had three daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eirene Angelina, who married (1) Andronicus Contostephanus, and (2) Alexius Palaeologus, by whom she was the grandmother of Emperor Michael VIII Palaeologus.  Anna Angelina, who married (1) the sebastokrator Isaac Komnenos, great-
2 CONC nephew of emperor Manuel I Comnenus, and (2) Theodore Lascaris, emperor of Nicaea.  Eudocia Angelina, who married (1) King Stefan I Prvovenčani of Serbia, then (2) Emperor Alexius V, and (3) Leo Sgouros, ruler of Corinth.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexios_III_Angelos
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://hu.wikipedia.org/wiki/III._Alexiosz_biz%C3%A1nci_cs%C3%A1sz%C3%A1r
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Written by: The Editors of Encyclopædia Britannica http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/14568/Alexius-III-Angelus
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexius III Angelus, also spelled Alexios III Angelos   (died 1211, Nicaea, Nicaean empire [now İznik, Turkey]), Byzantine emperor from 1195 to 1203. He was the second son of Andronicus Angelus, grandson of Alexius I. In 1195 he w
2 CONC as proclaimed emperor by the troops; he captured his brother, the emperor Isaac II, at Stagira in Macedonia and had him blinded and imprisoned. Crowned in April 1195, Alexius III was a weak and greedy emperor, and his coup d’état 
2 CONC had disastrous results. Byzantine prestige declined in the Balkans, where his failure to aid his son-in-law Stephen Prvovenčani (Stephen the First-Crowned) caused the latter to turn to the Bulgars for help. Campaigns against the B
2 CONC ulgars ended in defeat (1195 and 1196), and intrigues and diplomacy were equally unsuccessful because the new Bulgarian ruler, Kalojan, acknowledged the pope’s supremacy instead of that of Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1203 the Fourth Crusade restored Isaac II and his son (crowned Alexius IV). Alexius III fled the capital with what treasure he could collect and escaped to Thrace. After an unsuccessful attempt to recover the throne, he wandere
2 CONC d about Greece and surrendered to Boniface of Montferrat, then master of a great part of the Balkan Peninsula, but left his protection and sought shelter with Michael I, despot of Epirus. Finally, he went to Asia Minor, where his 
2 CONC son-in-law Theodore Lascaris was holding his own against the Latins. Alexius, joined by the sultan of Iconium (modern Konya, Turkey), demanded Theodore’s crown and, when it was refused, marched against him. Taken prisoner by Theod
2 CONC ore in 1211, Alexius was sent to a monastery at Nicaea, where he died.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''Note:''' See profile of: [https://www.geni.com/people/Alessio-Span-lord-of-Drivasto-Polog/6000000041839032060 '''Alessio Span'''], lord of Drivasto & Polog, who was [https://www.geni.com/people/Alexios-III-byzantine-emperor/600
2 CONC 0000000515214098 '''Alexios III''']'s descendant.
