2 CONT GODWIN, son of WULFNOTH & his wife --- ([993]-Winchester 15 Apr 1053, bur Winchester Old Minster[398]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Florence of Worcester, Earl Godwin was son of Wulfnoth[399].   Ætheling Æthelstan, under his will dated [1014], made a bequest to "Godwine, Wulfnoth's son, the estate at Compton which his father possessed", listed sec
2 CONC ond among the bequests to non-members of the royal family[400].  Installed as Earl in 1018 by King Canute, and as bajulus[401] over almost the whole kingdom[402].  He heads the lists of witnesses to all the king's lay charters aft
2 CONC er 1023[403], indicating an unrivalled position of power.  By the end of King Canute's reign he was referred to as Ealdorman of Wessex[404].  He supported Queen Emma in opposing the accession of King Harold I in 1036[405].  He app
2 CONC ears to have played a major role in the accession and acceptance in England of King Edward "the Confessor" in 1042, and in 1045 married his daughter to the king.  However, his relations with the king became tense following a dispu
2 CONC te over the appointment of a new archbishop of Canterbury in 1050.  In 1051, Earl Godwin refused the king's order to punish an affray at Canterbury, in which one of the men of Eustache Comte de Boulogne was killed.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The dispute escalated, and 1 Sep 1051 Godwin and his two older sons made a show of force to the king near Tetbury.  Earls Leofric and Siward rallied to the king's support, and battle was avoided.  Godwin and his family were given 
2 CONC five days' safe conduct to leave the country by the King's Council held on 8 Sep 1051[406].  
2 CONT
2 CONT He fled with his wife and sons Svein, Tostig and Gyrth to Bruges.  Godwin returned with an army in Sep 1052, joined forces with his son Harold who returned from Ireland, and sailed into London.  The family gathered popular support
2 CONC  and was fully reinstated[407].  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that Earl Godwin died soon after, three days after suffering a stroke[408].  Florence of Worcester records his death and burial at Winchester[409]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m (1019) GYTHA, daughter of THORGIL Sprakalegg & his wife --- (-St Omer after 1069).  She is named by Florence of Worcester[410].  Her origin is deduced from Ulf Jarl of Denmark being described as the uncle of her son Svein by Flo
2 CONC rence of Worcester[411].  Morkinskinna names “Gytha, the daughter of Thorgils sprakaleggr and the sister of Jarl Úlfr” as the wife of “Godwin”[412].  Adam of Bremen records that "Wolf sororem" married "duci Gudvino", and was mothe
2 CONC r of "ducis Suein, Tostin et Haroldum [genuit parricidas]"[413], although this last reference is unexplained.  She was living in Exeter when King William I attacked it in 1068.  She took refuge at Flat Holme, an island in the Bris
2 CONC tol Channel[414].  Florence of Worcester records that "Gytha…comitissa…mater Haroldi regis Anglorum ac soror Suani regis Danorum" fled to Flanders, in a passage dealing with events in early 1068[415].  She became a nun at Saint-Om
2 CONC er.  According to Orderic Vitaliis, she "secretly gathered together a great store of treasure and … fled to France never to return"[416]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin & his wife had ten children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         EADGYTH ([1020/22]-Winchester 18 Dec 1075, bur Westminster Abbey).  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that in 1045 "king Edward took to wife Edith the daughter of Earl Godwin, ten days before Candlemas"[417].  She was e
2 CONC ducated in Wilton nunnery.  Florence of Worcester records that her husband repudiated her in 1051 when he sent the rest of her family into exile and sent her "in disgrace with only a single handmaid" to Wherwell Abbey[418].  She w
2 CONC as brought back to court when her father was restored the following year[419].  She commissioned the Vita Ædwardi Regis from a foreign clerk, probably from Saint-Omer, setting out the history of her family.  She continued to live 
2 CONC around Winchester after the Norman conquest, and appears to have been treated well by King William I[420].  Florence of Worcester records the death "XIV Kal Jan" in [1074] of "Edgitha regis Haroldi germana quondam Anglorum regina"
2 CONC  at Winchester and her burial at Westminster[421].  m (23 Jan 1045) EDWARD "the Confessor" King of England, son of ÆTHELRED II King of England & his second wife Emma of Normandy ([1005]-Palace of Westminster 5 Jan 1066, bur Westmi
2 CONC nster Abbey). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         SVEIN ([1021/23]-Constantinople[422] 29 Sep 1052).  Florence of Worcester specifies that Svein was the eldest son of "earl Godwin and Githa"[423].  He was created Earl in Berkshire, Gloucestershire, Herefordshire, Oxfor
2 CONC dshire and Somerset in 1043 by King Edward "the Confessor".  He led an expedition into South Wales in 1046, allied with Gruffydd ap Llywellyn King of Gwynedd in North Wales.  The Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records that he seduced Eadgi
2 CONC fu Abbess of Leominster on his way back[424], for which he was outlawed by King Edward, who divided his earldom between his brother Harold and his cousin Bjørn Ulfsen.  The Chronicle records that Svein fled to Bruges in 1047, then
2 CONC  to Denmark the year after[425].  He returned to England in 1049, murdered his cousin Bjørn Ulfsen, and was exiled again in 1049 by an assembly of the whole army at Sandwich, this time taking refuge with Baudouin IV Count of Fland
2 CONC ers[426].  After his father secured his recall in [1050], he was pardoned and partially reinstated[427].  After joining his father's threatened armed rebellion against the king in 1051, he was outlawed yet again and fled with his 
2 CONC parents to Bruges.  According to the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle, he died while returning from a pilgrimage to Jerusalem[428], before the family's restoration.  According to Florence of Worcester[429], Svein died "in Lycia from illness 
2 CONC brought on by the severity of the cold".  Svein is not mentioned in the Vita Ædwardi, commissioned by his sister Queen Eadgyth, presumably because his dissolute life was considered best forgotten.  Svein had one illegitimate child
2 CONC , maybe by EADGIFU Abbess of Leominster, whom he was refused permission to marry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         HAAKON ([1046/47]-after 1065).  "Hacun filius Suani filii sui [Godwini]" was sent as a hostage to Normandy in 1051, possibly after Duke Guillaume II's visit to England[430].  Eadmer of Canterbury records that he was fre
2 CONC ed and returned to England with his uncle Harold Godwinsson in [1064/65][431]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.         HAROLD ([1022/25]-killed in battle Hastings 14 Oct 1066, bur [Waltham Abbey]).  His parentage is confirmed in several places in the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle[432].  He succeeded as HAROLD II King of England and was crowned 
2 CONC 6 Jan 1066. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -        KINGS of ENGLAND. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.         TOSTIG ([1025/30]-killed in battle Stamford Bridge 25 Sep 1066).  After joining his father's threatened armed rebellion against the king in 1051, Tostig fled to Bruges with his parents, although he returned the followin
2 CONC g year.  He was created Earl of Northumbria in 1055 to succeed Earl Siward[433].  Northumbria rebelled against him in Oct 1065, after several years of Scottish raids which Tostig had failed to halt.  In his place, the thegns of Yo
2 CONC rkshire elected Morcar, younger brother of Edwin Earl of Mercia, as Earl of Northumbria.  King Edward attempted unsuccessfully to crush the rebellion on Tostig's behalf, but Tostig was forced to flee to Flanders with his wife in D
2 CONC ec 1065[434].  Baudouin V Count of Flanders installed him as castellan of Saint-Omer[435].  Returning to England in search of revenge, he was repulsed from landing at Sandwich in Kent in May 1066, ravaged Norfolk and then sailed t
2 CONC o the Humber where he was defeated by Edwin Earl of Mercia.  Tostig sailed to Scotland, where he joined forces with Harald III "Hardråde" King of Norway who had recently arrived to invade England.  Morkinskinna records that Tostig
2 CONC  first sailed to Denmark to seek report from King Svend II, and then to Norway to King Harald III, sending “Gunnhildr´s son Guthormr” to offer Northumbria to the king in return for his help[436].  After defeating the Northumbrians
2 CONC  at Gate Fulford near York 20 Sep 1066, their combined army was defeated by King Harold's forces at Stamford Bridge 25 Sep 1066, where both he and the Norwegian king were killed[437].  m (before Sep 1051[438]) as her first husband
2 CONC , JUDITH de Flandre, daughter of BAUDOUIN IV Count of Flanders & his second wife [Eléonore] de Normandie ([1033]-5 Mar 1094, bur St Martin Monastery).  The Annalista Saxo names "Iudhita…amita Rodberti comitis de Flandria ex cognat
2 CONC ione beati Ethmundi regis" as husband of "Haroldi" (in error for Tostig) but correctly names her second husband "Welphus filius Azzonis marchionis Italorum"[439].  The Genealogia Welforum names "filiam comitis Flandrie, reginam An
2 CONC glie, Iuditam nomine" as wife of Welf[440].  Florence of Worcester says that Judith was "daughter of Baldwin Count of Flanders" but does not specify which Count Baldwin nor is this clear from the context[441].  According to the Vi
2 CONC ta Ædwardi Regis, she was the sister of Count Baudouin V[442].  Alberic de Trois Fontaines asserts that Judith was one of the children of Baudouin V Count of Flanders and his wife Adela de France[443], but there are other clear er
2 CONC rors in his listing of this couple's children so the statement should be viewed with caution.  Judith is also listed as the daughter of Count Baudouin V (after Mathilde) in a manuscript whose attribution to Orderic Vitalis is disp
2 CONC uted, which also shows her first marriage[444].  Judith moved to Denmark after her first husband was killed.  The Chronicon of Bernold records the death "1094 IV Non Mar" of "Iuditha uxor ducis Welfonis Baioariæ" and her burial "a
2 CONC pud monasterium…Sancti Martini" built by her husband[445].  The necrology of Raitenbuch records the death "III Non Mar" of "Iudinta regina Anglie, filia marchionis de Este uxor Welfonis nostri fundatoris"[446], exaggerating her st
2 CONC atus resulting from her first marriage and confusing her paternity.  The necrology of Weingarten records the death "III Non Mar" of "Judita dux regina Anglie"[447], also exaggerating her status resulting from her first marriage.  
2 CONC Tostig & his wife had [--- children]:
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         infant children .  According to the Vita Ædwardi Regis, the children of Tostig and Judith were "unweaned" when their father died[448]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tostig had [three] [probably illegitimate] children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         SKULI "kongsfostre" .  Snorre names "Skule, a son of Earl Toste, who since has been called the king's foster-son, and his brother Ketil Krok…of high family in England" when recording that they accompanied Olav King of N
2 CONC orway[449], the context suggesting that he and his brother were at least young adults at the time.  If this is correct, they must have been their father's illegitimate sons and were not the "unweaned" children referred to above.  
2 CONC Morkinskinna records that “Skúli, the son of Jarl Tostig Godwinson, and Ketill krókr from Hálogaland came to Norway” with Olaf III King of Norway after the failed invasion of England in 1066[450].  Morkinskinna records that “not l
2 CONC ong after King Haraldr´s fall Skúli went west to England to ask for the return of King Haraldr´s body” which was “readily granted to him”[451]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT -        NORWEGIAN NOBILITY. 
2 CONT
2 CONT c)          [KETIL "krókr" .  Snorre names "Skule, a son of Earl Toste, who since has been called the king's foster-son, and his brother Ketil Krok…of high family in England" when recording that they accompanied Olav King of Norwa
2 CONC y[452], the context suggesting that he and his brother were at least young adults at the time.  If this is correct, they must have been their father's illegitimate sons and were not the "unweaned" children referred to above.  Mork
2 CONC inskinna records that “Skúli, the son of Jarl Tostig Godwinson, and Ketill krókr from Hálogaland came to Norway” with Olav III King of Norway after the failed invasion of England in 1066, without specifying any relationship betwee
2 CONC n Skuli and Ketil[453].  One possibility is that Ketil was the uterine brother of Skuli and not the son of Tostig.  Morkinskinna records that King Olav III “arranged a good marriage for Ketil and appointed him a district chieftain
2 CONC  in the north”, adding that “many distinguished men are descended from Ketil”[454].  m ---.  Morkinskinna records that Olav III King of Norway “arranged a good marriage for Ketil”[455].  The name of Ketil´s wife is not known.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT d)          [OLAV (-1066 or after).  Simeon of Durham names "Tosti's son Olave and earl from the isle of Orkney named Paul" in 1066[456].  The reference to this son named Olav has not been corroborated in other sources so far cons
2 CONC ulted.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.         GYRTH ([1032]-killed in battle Hastings 14 Oct 1066[457]).  Florence of Worcester records that he fled with his parents to Bruges in 1051, after the family's disgrace[458].  He was appointed Earl of the East Angles in 1
2 CONC 057, in succession to Earl Ælfgar who had succeeded his father as Earl of Mercia.  After Gyrth's death, his lands were given to Ralph "the Staller" by William I King of England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.         [ÆLFGAR .  Orderic Vitalis names Ælfgar after Gyrth and before Leofwine in his list of the children of Earl Godwin[459].  He is not named among Earl Godwin's sons by Florence of Worcester[460] and no other reference to 
2 CONC him has so far been found.]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.         LEOFWINE (-killed in battle Hastings 14 Oct 1066457).  Florence of Worcester records that he fled to Ireland with his brother Harold in 1051, after the family's disgrace[461].  He was appointed Earl of Kent and Essex in
2 CONC  1057.  After his death, the county of Kent was awarded to Odo, Bishop of Bayeux, half-brother of William I King of England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.         WULFNOTH ([1036/46][462]-Feb [1094][463]).  Eadmer records that "Wulfnothus itaque filius Godwini" was sent as a hostage to Normandy with his nephew Haakon in 1051[464], possibly after Duke Guillaume II's visit to Engla
2 CONC nd but presumably before Sep of that year as he is not named among the sons of Godwin who fled England on the family's disgrace.  His brother Harold unsuccessfully negotiated for his release in [1064/65] during his visit to Duke G
2 CONC uillaume's court[465].  Florence of Worcester records that "comites Morkarum et Rogerum, Siwardum cognomento Barn, et Wlnothum regis Haroldi germanum" were released by King William on his deathbed in 1087[466].  He was taken to En
2 CONC gland by King William II but placed in confinement again in Winchester. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.         GUNHILD (-Bruges 24 Aug 1087).  Having taken a vow of chastity, she went to Saint-Omer in Flanders after 1066, then Bruges before moving to Denmark.  She returned to Bruges before she died[467]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 10.      ÆLFGIFU (-before Jan 1066[468]).  During the period of captivity of her brother Harold Godwinsson at the court of Guillaume II Duke of Normandy, Harold promised one of his sisters in marriage to one of Duke Guillaume's no
2 CONC bles[469], most probably Ælfgifu.  She is mentioned by name in Domesday Book.   
2 CONT
