2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin (sometimes Godwine) (c.1001 - April 15, 1053), was one of the most powerful lords in England under the Danish king Canute the Great and his successors. Canute made him the first Earl of Wessex. Godwin was the father of Haro
2 CONC ld II and of Edith of Wessex, wife of Edward the Confessor.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin was a seventh generation descendant of King Ethelred of Wessex (c. 837-871). Ethelred was succeeded by his younger brother Alfred the Great because his own son Aethelhelm of Wessex (c. 870-927) was still a minor at the time
2 CONC  of his death. Aethelhelm was later granted lands by his uncle but was never able to claim the throne. His daughter Elfleda of Wessex (c.890-918) however became Queen consort to King Edward the Elder (c.871-924).
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brother Aethelfrith of Wessex (c.900-927) lived the life of a common landlord. His son Eadric of Wessex was granted possession of Washington in the South Downs of West Sussex. His own son Aethelwerd of Wessex (c.930-998) is kn
2 CONC own as "the Historian". Aethelmar Cild (c.960-1015), son of the later, was a benefactor of Eynsham Abbey. His son was Wulfnoth Cild (c.983-1015) who was Thegn of Sussex but is otherwise obscure. Godwin was son to Wulfnoth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1014, Godwin was first married to Thyra Sveinsdóttir, daughter of Sweyn I , King of Denmark, Norway and England. Thyra however died in 1018. Her death apparently did not prevent her brother Canute from creating Godwin Earl 
2 CONC of Wessex during the same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1019, Godwin was married again to Gytha Thorkelsdóttir, granddaughter of the legendary Viking Styrbjörn Starke and great-granddaughter to Harold Bluetooth. The marriage resulted in the birth of at least twelve children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Driella of Wessex (c. 1019)
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith of Wessex, (c. 1020 - December 19, 1075), queen consort of Edward the Confessor
2 CONT
2 CONT Sweyn Godwinson, Earl of Mercia (c. 1021 - 1052). At some point he declared himself an illegitimate son of Canute the Great but this is considered to be a false claim.
2 CONT
2 CONT Harold II of England (c. 1022 - October 14, 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT Gungilda of Wessex, a nun (c. 1024 - 1080)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ylgiva of Wessex (c. 1025)
2 CONT
2 CONT Tostig Godwinson, Earl of Northumbria (c. 1026 - September 25, 1066).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ydgiva of Wessex (c. 1028)
2 CONT
2 CONT Gyrth Godwinson (c. 1032 - October 14, 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT Leofwine Godwinson , Earl of Kent (c. 1035 - October 14, 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT Wulfnoth Godwinson (c. 1036 - 1087)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ylfgar of Wessex (c. 1038)
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 12, 1035, Canute died. His kingdoms were divided among three rival rulers. Harold Harefoot, illegitimate son by Aelgifu of Northampton, usurped the throne of England. Harthacanute, legitimate son by Emma of Normandy, r
2 CONC eigned in Denmark. Norway rebelled under Magnus the Noble. On 1037, the throne of England was reportedly claimed by Alfred of Wessex, son of Emma of Normandy and Ethelred the Unready and half-brother of Harthacanute. Godwin is rep
2 CONC orted to have either captured Alfred himself or to have deceived him by pretending to be his ally and then surrendering him to the forces of Harold Harefoot. Either way Alfred was blinded and soon died.
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 17, 1040, Harold Harefoot died and Godwin supported the accession of Harthacanute to the throne of England. When Harthacanute himself died (June 8, 1042), Godwin supported the claim of his half-brother Edward the Confesso
2 CONC r to the throne. Edward was another son of Emma and Ethelred, having spent most of the previous thirtyyears in Normandy. His reign restored the native royal house of Wessex to the throne of England. Despite his alleged responsibil
2 CONC ity for the death of Edward's brother Alfred, Godwin secured the marriage of his daughter Edith (Eadgyth) to Edward in 1045. Godwin soon became the leader of opposition to growing Norman influence as Edward drew advisors, nobles a
2 CONC nd priests from his former place of refuge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Exiled from the kingdom in September 1051 for refusing to punish the people of Dover for a violent clash with the visiting Eustace II, Count of Boulogne, Godwin returned the following year with an armed force, compelling Edward to
2 CONC  restore his earldom.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 15, 1053, Godwin died. His son Harold succeeded him as Earl of Wessex, an area then covering roughly the southernmost third of England. Harold later succeeded Edward the Confessor and became King of England in his own rig
2 CONC ht.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin, earl av Wessex
2 CONT
2 CONT Från Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin, född ca 1001, död 5 april 1053, var en av de mäktigaste adelsmännen i England under Knut den store och hans efterträdare. Knut gjorde honom till den förste earlen av Wessex. Godwin var far till Harald II, Tostig Godwinson 
2 CONC och Edith av Wessex, Edvard Bekännarens drottning.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin var den sjunde generationens ättling till kung Ethelred av Wessex, Alfred den stores äldre bror. Hans ättlingar förbisågs i den kungliga successionsordningen, men blev framstående adelsmän i kungariket. Godwins far var Wulf
2 CONC noth Cild (ca 983-1015) thegn av Sussex. Wulfnoth ledde en del av den kungliga flottan till sjöröveri, vilket fick konsekvensen att hans landområden förverkades och att han landsförvisades. Det ankom därefter på hans unge son, God
2 CONC win, att förbättra familjens förmögenhet efter faderns död 1014.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin stödde Edmund Järnsida, kung Ethelred den rådvilles son. Då Edmund revolterade mot fadern, invaderade Knut den store England. Edmund dödades tillsammans med många av sina anhängare, men Godwin undkom och svor trohet till Kn
2 CONC ut den store. Han blev en av Knuts rådgivare och följde honom till Danmark för att kväsa ett uppror där. 1022 gifte han sig med Knuts syster, Thyra Sveinsdóttir. Hon dog strax därefter, men Godwin fortsatte att vinna anseende och 
2 CONC 1023 var han den mäktigaste earlen i England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin gifte sig med en annan dansk adelsdam, Gytha Thorkelsdóttir, barnbarn till den legendariske vikingen Styrbjörn Starke och barnbarnsbarn till Harald Blåtand. Äktenskapet ledde till många barn:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Sweyn Godwinson, earl av Mercia (ca. 1025-1052). Han hävdade att han var Knut den stores illegitime son, men detta anses vara falskt.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Harald II av England (ca. 1022-14 oktober 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.Tostig Godwinson, earl av Northumbria (ca. 1026-25 september 1066).
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.Edith av Wessex, (ca. 1030- 19 december 1075), Edvard Bekännarens drottning
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.Gyrth Godwinson (ca. 1030-14 oktober 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.Gunhilda av Wessex, (ca. 1035-1080), nunna
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.Ælfgifu av Wessex (ca.1035)
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.Leofwine Godwinson, earl av Kent (ca. 1035-14 oktober 1066)
2 CONT
2 CONT 9.Wulfnoth Godwinson (ca.1040)
2 CONT
2 CONT 12 november 1035 dog Knut den store. Hans kungarike delades upp mellan tre rivaliserande härskare. Harald Harfot, en illegitim son med Aelgifu av Northampton, tillskanskade sig Englands tron medan Hardeknut, en legitim son med Emm
2 CONC a av Normandie regerade i Danmark. Norge gjorde uppror genom Magnus I av Norge. 1037 krävde Emmas och Ethelreds son, Hardeknuts halvbror, Alfred av Wessex tronen. Godwin ska antingen själv ha gripit Alfred eller lurat honom att va
2 CONC ra en allierad och överlämnat honom till Harald Harfots trupper. I alla händelser gjordes Alfred blind och han dog inom kort.
2 CONT
2 CONT 17 mars 1040 dog Harald Harfot, varefter Godwin stödde Hardeknut som tronföljare. Då Hardeknut i sin tur dog 8 juni 1042 stödde Godwin hans halvbrors, Edvard Bekännaren tronanspråk. Edvard var ytterligare en son till Ethelred och 
2 CONC Emma som hade tillbringat större delen av de gångna trettio åren i Normandie. Hans regering återställde den kungliga familjen Wessex på den engelska tronen. Trots Godwins ansvar för Edvards bror Alfreds död säkrade makten genom gi
2 CONC ftermålet mellan sin dotter Eadgyth (Edith av Wessex) 1045. Godwin blev ledare för oppositionen mot det ökande normandiska inflytandet, då Edvard anlitade rådgivare, adelsmän och präster från sin tidigare tillflyktsort.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin landsförvisades från kungariket i september 1051 för att han vägrat att straffa folket i Dover för en våldsam sammandrabbning med den besökande Eustace II, greve av Bologna. Godwin återvände dock följande år och tvingade me
2 CONC d militära styrkor Edvard att återställa hans samhällsposition.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin dog 15 april 1053. Hans son Harald efterträdde honom som earl av Wessex, ett område som på den tiden täckte den södra tredjedelen av England. Harald efterträdde senare Edvard Bekännaren och blev kung av England.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin, Earl of Wessex
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT  This article needs additional citations for verification.
2 CONT
2 CONT Please help improve this article by adding reliable references. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (June 2007) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin (sometimes Godwine, Goodwin, Godwyn, Goodwyn and sometimes known as Godwin of Wessex) (c. 1001–April 15, 1053), was one of the most powerful lords in England under the Danish king Canute the Great and his successors. Canute
2 CONC  made him the first Earl of Wessex. Godwin was the father of Harold II and of Edith of Wessex, wife of Edward the Confessor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Rise: support of Edmund, then Canute 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Height of power: support of Harold 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Later conflicts, decline, and death 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Progeny 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.1 Family Trees 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Sources 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 External links 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Rise: support of Edmund, then Canute
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin's father was probably Wulfnoth Cild, Cild being a nickname (c. 983-1015) who was Thegn of Sussex, although later documents describe his father as a churl. Wulfnoth was supposedly a sixth generation descendant of King Ethelr
2 CONC ed of Wessex (also caled Aethelred I), the elder brother of Alfred the Great; Ethelred's descendants were passed over in the royal succession, but became prominent nobles in the kingdom. The patrilineal descent of Godwin from a Ki
2 CONC ng of Wessex is still being researched, however, and should not be taken as certain. Wulfnoth led a section of the royal fleet into piracy and as a consequence had his lands forfeited, and was exiled. It was left to his young son,
2 CONC  Godwin, to improve the family fortunes after his father's death in 1014. In his day, Earl Godwin was seen as very much of a new man, who had been "made" by two advantageous marriages to Danish noblewomen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin was a major supporter of Edmund Ironside, the son of King Aethelred the Unready. While Edmund was in rebellion against his father, Canute and his army invaded England. Edmund was killed, along with many of his supporters, b
2 CONC ut Godwin survived and pledged his loyalty to Canute. He befriended Canute's brother-in-law, Earl Ulf, and became one of Canute's advisors, accompanying him to Denmark to suppress a rebellion there. By 1018 he was an earl, becomin
2 CONC g Earl of the West Saxons in about 1019. In 1022 he married Thyra Sveinsdóttir (also called Thyra Swensdottir), Canute's sister. She died soon afterwards without issue, but Godwin continued to gain prestige and by 1023 he was the 
2 CONC most powerful earl in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Height of power: support of Harold
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 12, 1035, Canute died. His kingdoms were divided among three rival rulers. Harold Harefoot, Canute's illegitimate son by Aelgifu of Northampton, seized the throne of England. Harthacanute, Canute's legitimate son by Em
2 CONC ma of Normandy, reigned in Denmark. Norway rebelled under Magnus the Noble. In 1037, the throne of England was reportedly claimed by Alfred of Wessex, younger son of Emma of Normandy and Ethelred the Unready and half-brother of Ha
2 CONC rthacanute. Godwin is reported to have either captured Alfred himself or to have deceived him by pretending to be his ally and then surrendering him to the forces of Harold Harefoot. Either way Alfred was blinded and soon died at 
2 CONC Ely.
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 17, 1040, Harold Harefoot died and Godwin supported the accession of his half-brother Harthacanute to the throne of England. When Harthacanute himself died (June 8, 1042), Godwin finally supported the claim of his half-br
2 CONC other Edward the Confessor to the throne. Edward was another son of Emma and Ethelred, having spent most of the previous thirty years in Normandy. His reign restored the native royal house of Wessex to the throne of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Later conflicts, decline, and death
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his alleged responsibility for the death of Edward's brother Alfred, Godwin secured the marriage of his daughter Edith (Eadgyth) to Edward in 1045. As Edward drew advisors, nobles and priests from his former place of refug
2 CONC e in a bid to develop his own power base, Godwin soon became the leader of opposition to growing Norman influence. After a violent clash between the people of Dover and the visiting Eustace II, Count of Boulogne, Edward's new brot
2 CONC her-in-law, Godwin was ordered to punish the people of Dover (as he and Earl Leofric had done in Worcester, in Leofric's own earldom). This time, however, Godwin refused, choosing to champion his own countrymen against a (visiting
2 CONC ) foreign ruler and his own king. Edward rightly saw this as a test of power, and managed to enlist the support of the other earls Earl Siward from Northumbria and Earl Leofric from Mercia. Godwin and his sons were exiled from the
2 CONC  kingdom in September 1051. However, they returned the following year with an armed force, which gained the support of the navy, burghers, and peasants, so compelling Edward to restore his earldom. This however set a precedent to 
2 CONC be followed by a rival earl some years later, and then by Godwin's own son in 1066.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 15, 1053, Godwin died at Winchester, supposedly while denying that he had any role in the death of the King's brother Alfred in 1036. His son Harold succeeded him as Earl of Wessex, an area then covering roughly the south
2 CONC ernmost third of England. With the death of Earl Siward (1055) and later Earl Algar (1062), the children of Godwin were poised to assume sole control. Tostig was helped into the earldom of Northumbria, thus controlling the north. 
2 CONC The Mercian earl was sidelined, especially after Harold and Tostig broke the Welsh-Mercian alliance in 1063. Harold later succeeded Edward the Confessor and became King of England in his own right. At this point, both Harold's rem
2 CONC aining brothers in England were earls in their own right, Harold was himself king and in control of Wessex, and he had married the sister of Earl Edwin of Mercia and Earl Morcar of Northumbria (who had succeeded his brother Tostig
2 CONC ). Godwin's family looked set to inaugurate a new royal dynasty, much as the Capetians had replaced the Carolingians in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Progeny
2 CONT
2 CONT Godwin married a second time to a Danish noblewoman, Gytha Thorkelsdóttir (also known as Githa), said to be the granddaughter of the legendary Viking Styrbjörn Starke and great-granddaughter to Harold Bluetooth, king of Denmark an
2 CONC d thus also ancestor to King Canute. The marriage resulted in the birth of many children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sweyn Godwinson, Earl of Herefordshire (c. 1023-1052). At some point he declared himself an illegitimate son of Canute the Great but this is considered to be a false claim. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Harold II of England (c. 1022-October 14, 1066) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tostig Godwinson, Earl of Northumbria (c. 1026-September 25, 1066) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith of Wessex, (c. 1030-December 19, 1075), queen consort of Edward the Confessor 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gyrth Godwinson (c. 1030-October 14, 1066) 
2 CONT
