0 @N6000000003220307877@ NOTE Regj. 1202-1204.
0 @N6000000002187982774@ NOTE Haakon III of Norway
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Håkon III (1170s–1 January 1204), (Norwegian Håkon Sverresson, Old Norse Hákon Sverrisson) was king of Norway from 1202–1204.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born as the second illegitimate son of the future king Sverre, then a Faroese adventurer, by his concubine Astrid Roesdatter, at some point in the 1170s. This was during the Norwegian civil wars.
1 CONT
1 CONT The civil wars period of Norwegian history lasted from 1130 to 1240. During this period there were several interlocked conflicts of varying scale and intensity. The background for these conflicts were the unclear Norwegian success
1 CONC ion laws, social conditions and the struggle between different aristocratic parties and between Church and King. There were then two main parties, firstly known by varying names or no names at all, but finally condensed into the p
1 CONC arties of Bagler and Birkebeiner. The rallying point regularly was a royal son, who was set up as the leader of the party in question, to oppose the rule of king from the contesting party. Sverre and Håkon were leaders of the birk
1 CONC ebeiner party.
1 CONT
1 CONT Håkon is first mentioned as one of the leaders of his father's armies in a battle against the bagler in Oslo in 1197. Subsequently he is mentioned several times as taking part in his father's wars against the bagler. On his deathb
1 CONC ed his father, who died on 9 March 1202, declared that he had no other son alive than Håkon. He also wrote a letter to Håkon advising him to settle the longstanding dispute with the church. When the news of Sverre's death reached 
1 CONC Håkon and the birkebeiner assembled in Nidaros, Håkon was first taken as chieftain by the birkebeiner. The same spring he was taken as king at the thing in Nidaros.
1 CONT
1 CONT The same spring the Norwegian bishops, who had been in exile in Sweden and Denmark and had supported the bagler, returned to Norway and made a settlement with Håkon. It seems likely that he gave in to most of their demands. Norway
1 CONC  was released from the interdict it had been placed under during the reign of Sverre. Håkon is said to have been on friendly terms with the farmers and the common people, and the bagler party soon lost much of its support. In the 
1 CONC autumn of 1202, the bagler king Inge Magnusson was killed by the local farmers of Oppland and the bagler party in Norway was dissolved. A new bagler pretender, Erling Steinvegg, soon appeared in Denmark, but declined to renew the 
1 CONC fighting, as he saw little chance of succeeding against Håkon. Håkon was thus the undisputed ruler of the country.
1 CONT
1 CONT Håkon appears to have had a troubled relationship with his father's queen, Margareta Eriksdotter. After Sverre's death, Margareta attempted to return to her native Sweden with her daughter by Sverre, Kristina. Håkon's men forcibly
1 CONC  separated her from her daughter, as he wanted to keep her at his court. Subsequently, Margareta seemingly settled with Håkon and went to his court. During christmas in 1203, Håkon fell ill after a bloodletting, and on 1 January 1
1 CONC 204 he died. His death was suspected as poisoning and his step-mother Margareta was suspected of the crime. In the end she had one of her men undergo a trial by ordeal on her behalf to prove her innocence, but the man was badly bu
1 CONC rned. This was taken as proof of her guilt, and she had to flee back to Sweden.
1 CONT
1 CONT Håkon Sverresson was not married, and at his death no heirs were known. He was therefore succeeded by his 4-year-old nephew Guttorm Sigurdsson. But after his death a woman, Inga of Varteig, whom Håkon had taken as a concubine for 
1 CONC a time in 1203, appeared at the birkebeiner court with an infant son who she claimed was Håkon's son. The child had been born in present-day Østfold after the death of the putative father. The boy, named Håkon after his father, la
1 CONC ter became king Håkon IV. In the summer of 1218, Inga underwent a successful trial by ordeal (bore iron) in Bergen to show the paternity of her son.
1 CONT
1 CONT During Håkon's brief reign, he managed to release Norway from the church's interdict, and end the civil wars, at least for a time. Whether the peace would have lasted if he had been allowed to live is impossible to say. As it turn
1 CONC ed out, his early death sparked a renewal of the fighting, as the bagler pretender Erling Steinvegg in a matter of months gathered an army and went to Norway to claim the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Håkon was buried in the old cathedral in Bergen. The cathedral was demolished in 1531, and the site is today marked by a memorial.
1 CONT
1 CONT Our main sources to Håkon's life are Sverris saga and the Bagler sagas, both written shortly after the events, in the 13th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sverre Sigurdsson King of Norway
1 CONT 1202-1204 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Guttorm Sigurdsson
0 @N6000000000945026345@ NOTE Inga av Varteig. Født anslått 1185. Død omkring 1234.  Ble mor til Håkon IV (Håkonsson) Konge av Norge. (født 1204 på Falkenborg, Eidsberg. Død 15.12.1263 på Orknøyene.) Inga skal ha vært meget vakker. Hun gjorde Håkon Sverressons
1 CONC  bekjentskap i Sarpsborg høsten 1203 under et opphold hos sin frende Audun, en av de gjeveste menn i Borg. Mens hun var fruktsommelig ble hun sveket av sine frender, måtte vandre hjemmefra og ble mottatt av Erland på Huseby iEidsb
1 CONC erg. Hun bodde på nabogården Falkenborg da hun fødte Håkon. I 1205 måtte hun dra med gutten til Hamar på julaften, men av frykt for sin frende, biskop Ivar, reiste hun straks videre til Lillehammer og gjemte seg der i julen. I jan
1 CONC uar 1206 dro hun nordover gjennom Østerdalen, ble forfulgt av biskop Nikulas og baglerne, men kom lykkelig frem til Nidaros.
1 CONT Hun hørte åpenbart til en god bondeslekt i Østfold og ble, etter rimelige konstruksjoner av moderne historikere, kort etter kongesønnens fødsel i 1204 gift med birkebeinerhøvdingen Vegard fra Verdal. I dette ekteskapet hadde hun v
1 CONC isstnok en sønn, Olav. På den annen side er dette ikke til hinder for at hun i 1217-18, etter en del vanskeligheter, ?bærer jern? for sannheten av at kong Håkon er Håkon Sverressons sønn. Etter Vegards død i 1221 nevnes hun en og 
1 CONC annen gang i Håkon Håkonssons saga sammen med andre av hoffets damer. Inga er antagelig begravet i St. Halvards kirke.
1 CONT Kilder:
1 CONT Norsk Biografisk Leksikon, Bind V (1931), side 163, 192, Bind VI (1934), side 504.
1 CONT C.M. Munthe: Norske slegtsmerker, NST Bind I (1928), side 183-185.
1 CONT Mogens Bugge: Våre forfedre, nr. 805 (NST 2 s. 239).
1 CONT Bent og Vidar Billing Hansen: Rosensverdslektens forfedre, side 52.
0 @N6000000000945049144@ NOTE Inga skal ha vært meget vakker. Hun gjorde Håkon Sverressons bekjentskap i 
1 CONT Sarpsborg høsten 1203 under et opphold hos sin frende Audun, en av de gjeveste menn i
1 CONT Borg.
1 CONT Mens hun var fruktsommelig ble hun sveket av sine frender, måtte vandre hjemmefra
1 CONT og ble mottatt av Erland på Huseby i Eidsberg. Hun bodde på nabogården Falkenborg da hun
1 CONT fødte Håkon. I 1205 måtte hun dra med gutten til Hamar på julaften, men av frykt for sin
1 CONT frende, biskop Ivar, reiste hun straks videre til Lillehammer og gjemte seg der i julen. I januar
1 CONT 1206 dro hun nordover gjennom Østerdalen, ble forfulgt av biskop Nikulas og baglerne, men
1 CONT kom lykkelig frem til Nidaros.
1 CONT Hun hørte åpenbart til en god bondeslekt i Østfold og ble, etter rimelige konstruksjoner
1 CONT av moderne historikere, kort etter kongesønnens fødsel i 1204 gift med birkebeinerhøvdingen
1 CONT Vegard fra Verdal. I dette ekteskapet hadde hun visstnok en sønn, Olav. På den annen side
1 CONT er dette ikke til hinder for at hun i 1217-18, etter en del vanskeligheter, ?bærer jern? for
1 CONT sannheten av at kong Håkon er Håkon Sverressons sønn. Etter Vegards død i 1221 nevnes
1 CONT hun en og annen gang i Håkon Håkonssons saga sammen med andre av hoffets damer.
1 CONT Inga er antagelig begravet i St. Halvards kirke.
