0 @N6000000002187639750@ NOTE Boleslaus III of Poland
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus III the Wrymouth (Polish: Boleslaw III Krzywousty; 1085–1138) was duke of Poland from 1102. He was a son of Duke Wladislaus Herman and Judith of Bohemia, daughter of emperor Henry III. Boleslaus III was thus the brother-
1 CONC in-law of emperor Henry IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT He defeated the Pomeranians at the Battle of Naklo in 1109, and took control of Pomerania between 1119 and 1123, once again regaining Polish access to the Baltic Sea. The government of the local Pomeranians was left in place. He a
1 CONC lso defeated Henry V, Holy Roman Emperor in 1109, in the Battle of Glogów and Battle of Psie Pole. However, in 1135 he became a vassal of Henry's son Lothair II, Holy Roman Emperor, with Pomerania and Rügen as the fief.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus also campaigned in Hungary from 1132 to 1135, but to little effect.
1 CONT
1 CONT First he married Zbyslava, daughter of Sviatopolk II, grand duke of Kiev. They had one son:
1 CONT
1 CONT Wladislaus II the Exile (b. 1105), King of Poland 
1 CONT Second, he married Salome von Berg-Schelklingen, by whom he had 14 children (6 sons + 8 daughters), of which we know 4 sons and 5 daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus IV the Curly (b. 1125) 
1 CONT Mieszko III the Old (b. 1126) 
1 CONT Henry of Sandomierz (b. 1127) 
1 CONT Casimir the Just (b. 1138) 
1 CONT Rycheza of Poland (b. 12 April 1116), married to Grand Duke Volodar 
1 CONT Dobronega of Poland (b. 1128), married Markis Dietrich of Niederlausitz 
1 CONT Gertruda of Poland 
1 CONT Judith of Poland (b. 1132), married Otto I of Brandenburg 
1 CONT Agnes of Poland (b. 1137), married Mstislav II of Kiev 
1 CONT Before his death in 1138 he announced his testament (Boleslaus the Wrymouth's testament) dividing his land between four of his sons. The "senioral principle" established in the testament stated that at all times the oldest member 
1 CONC of the dynasty was to have supreme power over the rest and also control an indivisible "senioral part": a vast strip of land running N-S through the middle of Poland, with Kraków as the main city. The Senior's prerogatives also in
1 CONC cluded control over Pomerania, which was a fief of the Empire. The principle was quickly broken, at which there began a period of almost 200 years of feudal dissolution in Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT    This royalty-related article is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
