0 @N6000000003949868425@ NOTE ENCYCLOPÆDIA BRITANNICA, Vol. 18, p. 807  (1086 - 1138), called the Wry-Mouthed, prince of Poland, the son of Wladyslaw I Herman and Judith of Bavaria, succeeded his father in 1102. His early years were troubled continually by the
1 CONC  rivalry of his natural half-brother Zhigniew, who, till he was imprisoned and blinded, involved Boleslaw in contests with Bohemia and the Emperor Henry V. The first of the German wars began in 1109, when Henry, materially assiste
1 CONC d by the Czechs, invaded Silesia. Th Poles avoided an encounter in the open field, but harried the Germans so successfully and defended Glogau so stoutly that the emperor withdrew leaving the plain covered with corpses. The chief 
1 CONC political result of this great disaster was the complete independence of Poland for the next quarter of a century. It was during this respite that Boleslaw devoted himself to the main business of his life - the subjugation of Pome
1 CONC rania with the view of gaining access to the sea. The struggle began in 1109, when Boleslaw inflicted a terrible defeat on the Slavonic but still pagan Pomeranians at Naklo which compelled their temporary submission. In 1120-24 th
1 CONC e rebellion of the vassal Prince Warcislaw of Szczecin again brought Boleslaw into the country, but the resistance was as stout as ever, and only after 18,000 of his followers had fallen and 8,000 more had been expatriated did War
1 CONC cislaw submit to his conqueror. The obstinacy of the resistance convinced Boleslaw that Pomerania must be Christianized before it could be completely subdued; and this important work was partially accomplished by St. Otto, bishop 
1 CONC of Bamberg, an old friend of Boleslaw' father, who knew the Slavonic languages. In his later years Boleslaw waged an unsuccessful war with Hungary and Bohemia. He died in 1138, leaving four sons, between whom the kingdom was divid
1 CONC ed, the eldest son having overlordship of the whole.
0 @N6000000004533651116@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus III on a painting by Jan MatejkoBoleslaus III the Wrymouth(Boleslaw III Krzywousty), (1086-1138) was duke of Poland from 1102.He was a son of Ladislaus Herman of Poland and Judith of Bohemia.
1 CONT
1 CONT He defeated the Pomeranians at the battle of Naklo in 1109, and tookcontrol of Pomerania between 1119 and 1123, regaining Polish access tothe sea. He also defeated Henry V, Holy Roman Emperor in 1109, in theBattle of Hundsfeld.
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus also campaigned in Hungary from 1132 to 1135, but to littleeffect.
1 CONT
1 CONT First he married Zbyslava of Kiev, daughter of Sviatopolk II of Kievgrand duke of Kiev. They had one son:
1 CONT
1 CONT Ladislaus (b. 1105), King of Poland
1 CONT Second he married Salome von Berg-Schelklingen, with whom he had 14children (6 sons + 8 daughters) from whom we know of 4 sons and 5daughters:
1 CONT
1 CONT Boleslaus IV the Curly (b. 1125)
1 CONT Mieszko III the Old (b. 1126)
1 CONT Henry of Sandomierz (b. 1127)
1 CONT Casimir the Just (b. 1138)
1 CONT Rycheza of Poland (b. 12 April 1116), married to grandduke Volodar
1 CONT Dobronega of Poland (b. 1128), married Markis Dietrich ofNiederlausitz
1 CONT Gertruda of Poland
1 CONT Judith of Poland (b. 1132), married Otto I of Brandenburg
1 CONT Agnes of Poland (b. 1137), married Mstislav II of Kiev
1 CONT Before his death in 1138 he announced the testament (Boleslaw theWrymouth's testament) dividing his land between four of his sons. The"senioral principle" established in the testament stated, that atevery time the oldest member of
1 CONC  the dynasty was to have a supremepower over the rest and also control an indivisible "senioral part" -a vast stripe of land running N-S through the middle of Poland, withKrakow as the main city. Senior's prerogatives included als
1 CONC o controlover Pomerania, which was a fief of the Empire. The principle wasquickly broken, which began an almost 200 years period of feudaldissolution in Poland.
