1 CONC aptism. He made an alliance with King Olaf Tryggvesson, in virtue of which the payment of tribute to Norway on the udal lands in Orkney was discontinued; and after his son Hundi-Hiodve’s death, while a hostage in Norway, he paid n
1 CONC o allegiance to the King. He made alliance with Sihtric Silkbeard, King of Dublin (who came to Hrossey before Yule 1013), against Brian, High King of Ireland (1002-1014), who had married Sihtric’s mother, Gormflaeth (da. of Morugh
1 CONC  MacFinn, King of Leinster (?) 965-972). By this alliance, which was instigated by Gormflaeth, who was "bitter against King Brian," King Sihtric agreed that if they killed King Brian, Sigurd should marry Gormflaeth and be High Kin
1 CONC g of Ireland. Sigurd landed at Dublin on Palm Wednesday (21 Apr. 1014] and was killed at the battle of Clontarf on Good Friday [23 Apr. 1014]. He had three sons by an unnamed mother or mothers: Somerled, Brusi and Einar Rangmund (
1 CONC Wrymouth). By Donada, dau. of Malcolm MacKenneth, King of Scots, whom he m. probably in or after 1005, he had a 4th son, Thorfinn, who was b. circa 1009. Sigurd, who had left his three elder sons to rule Orkney, when he started fo
1 CONC r Dublin, was suc. in Orkney by all three, who divided the Earldom among themselves; but Caithness and Sutherland were seized by King Malcolm MacKenneth for (their half-brother) his grandson Thorfinn whom he created Earl of Caithn
1 CONC ess in 1014, and he "set men to govern the dominion with him."  Burial Mound, Hofry, Caithness, Scotland  [Complete Peerage, X:Appendix A:8-9]
