1 CONT And so, to procure the remission of the tax, she did her lord's imperious
1 CONT bidding; and
1 CONT "Then she rode forth, clothed on with chastity."
1 CONT It was the rapacity of the great earls that, among many other evils of misrule,
1 CONT made the country weak and demoralized. But Tostig went beyond the enormities
1 CONT of most of the grasping and licentious nobles and preyed upon the common
1 CONT people. The people of Northumbria rose against his power, drove him from York,
1 CONT and chose Morcar, one of the sons of Leofric, as their chief. [Knight's Popular
1 CONT History of England, Vol. 1, p. 202]
1 CONT
1 CONT !1st husband of Judith/Fausta of Flanders; younger son of Godwin, Earl of
1 CONT Wessex; brother of Earl Harold. [Ped. of Charlemagne, Vol. I, p. 271]
1 CONT
1 CONT !Northumbria, 1055. Tostig, the brother of Earl Harold, is the first
1 CONT southerner to become Earl of Northumbria.
1 CONT !Wales, July 1063. Campaigns by Earl Tostig and his brother Earl Harold put
1 CONT Gruffudd ap Llywelyn to flight. [Chronicle of the Royal Family, p. 26]
1 CONT
1 CONT Prior to the Conquest, he owned Preston, Lancashire, Lonsdale, Kendal, Carmel,
1 CONT Furness and other places in Cumberland. (Cumberland was formerly Westmoreland
1 CONT named Cumbria in 1974.) [Early Kendall Ancestry, p. 6]
1 CONT
1 CONT Earl of Northumbria (1055-66), third son of Earl Godwine of Wessex, his
1 CONT appointment to the earldom of Northumbria in 1055, in contravention of the
1 CONT claims of the family of Earl Siward, seemed to cement the dominance of
1 CONT Godwine's sons over the English kingdom. Tostig, however, was unpopular and
1 CONT provoked a rebellion within his earldom in 1065; when his brother Harold did
1 CONT not support him militarily, he went into exile and joined Harold's enemies.
1 CONT Harold became king soon after; Tostig was killed alongside Harald Hardraada,
1 CONT king of Norway, at the battle of Stamfordbridge. [The Columbia Companion to
1 CONT British History, p. 748
0 @N6000000002187757627@ NOTE Tostig Godwinson
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Tostig Godwinson (1026? – September 25, 1066) was an Anglo-Saxon earl of Northumbria and brother of King Harold II of England, the last crowned Anglo-Saxon King of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Early life 
1 CONT 2 Earldom 
1 CONT 3 Death 
1 CONT 4 Trivia 
1 CONT 5 Notes 
1 CONT 6 Tostig in Non-Fiction Books 
1 CONT 7 Tostig in Fiction 
1 CONT 8 Family Trees 
1 CONT 9 See also 
1 CONT 10 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early life
1 CONT Tostig was born the third child of Godwin, Earl of Wessex and Kent, and Gytha Thorkelsdóttir. In 1051, he married Judith, the daughter of Count Baldwin IV, half-sister of Baldwin V of Flanders, and aunt of Matilda who married Will
1 CONC iam the Conqueror. This made him William's uncle-in-law.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Earldom
1 CONT That same year, 1051, Tostig and his father were banished from England to which they forcefully returned in 1052. Three years later in 1055, Tostig became the Earl of Northumbria upon the death of Earl Siward.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tostig appears to have governed in Northumbria with some difficulty. He was never popular with the Northumbrian ruling class, a mix of Danish invaders,and Anglo Saxon survivors of the last Norse invasion. The reasons for this are 
1 CONC not clear. Tostig was known to have taken a heavy hand against those who resisted his rule, including the murder of several scions of Northumbrian families. The reasons for this resistance include frequent absences at the court of
1 CONC  King Edward in the south, and possibly a lack of leadership against the Scots, voracious raiders, whose king was a personal friend of Tostig. This was a Catch-22 situation, however; Tostig's unpopularity made it difficult to rais
1 CONC e local levies to combat the Scots. He resorted to using a strong force of Danish mercenaries (housecarles) as his main force, an expensive and resented policy (the housecarle's leaders were later slaughtered by rebels). Local bia
1 CONC ses probably also played a part. Tostig was of the south of England, a distinctly different culture from the north, which had not bent its head to a southern earl in many lifetimes. In 1063, still immersed in the confused local po
1 CONC litics of Northumbria, his popularity apparently plummeted to a new and dangerous level. Many of the inhabitants of Northumbria were Danes, who had enjoyed lesser taxation than in other parts of England. Yet the wars in Wales, of 
1 CONC which Tostig's constituents were principle beneficiaries, needed paying for. Tostig had been a major commander in these wars attacking in the North whilst his brother Harold marched up from the South.
1 CONT
1 CONT In late 1063 or early 1064 Tostig had Gamal, son of Orm, and Ulf, son of Dolfin, assassinated when they visited him under safe conduct.[1] Also, the Vita Edwardi, otherwise sympathetic to Tostig, states that he had 'repressed [the
1 CONC  Northumbrians] with the heavy yoke of his rule'. This may refer to any or all of the situations discussed above.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 3 October 1065 the thegns of Yorkshire and the rest of Yorkshire descended on York and occupied the city. They killed Tostig's officials and supporters, then declared Tostig outlawed for his unlawful action and sent for Morcar,
1 CONC  younger brother of Edwin, Earl of Mercia. The Northern rebels marched south to press their case with King Edward. They were joined at Northampton by Earl Edwin and his forces. There they were met by Earl Harold, who came to negot
1 CONC iate and did not bring his forces. He had been sent by King Edward to open negotiations with the rebels. After Harold had spoken with the rebels at Northampton, he realised that Tostig would not be able to retain Northumbria. When
1 CONC  he returned to Oxford where the royal council was to meet on 28 October, he had probably already made up his mind. Harold persuaded the King to agree to the demands of the rebels. Tostig was outlawed a short time later, possibly 
1 CONC early in November, because he refused to accept his deposition as commanded by Edward. This led to the fatal confrontation and enmity between the Godwinsons. (there is an interesting side story to this: at a meeting of the King an
1 CONC d his council, Tostig publicly accused Harold of fomenting the rebellion, truly an outrageous claim to make, unless there was a grain of truth. Harold certainly rid himself of a troublesome and ambitious brother, one who had the e
1 CONC ar of the King, and who may have been angling for the throne. Also, Harold was keen to unify England in the face of the grave threat from William of Normandy, who openly declared his intention to take the English throne. Perhaps H
1 CONC arold sold out his unpopular brother to ensure peace and support. Tostig certainly thought so.
1 CONT
1 CONT Tostig then took ship with his family and some loyal thegns and took refuge with his father-in-law, Count Baldwin IV. He even attempted to form an alliance with William who himself claimed the throne of England. Baldwin provided h
1 CONC im with a fleet and he landed in the Isle of Wight in May 1066 where he collected money and provisions, and he raided the coast as far as Sandwich. King Harold called out land and naval forces and Tostig retreated. He moved north 
1 CONC and after an unsuccessful attempt to get his brother Gyrth to join him he raided Norfolk and Lincolnshire. The earls Edwin and Morcar defeated him decisively, and deserted by his men, he fled to his sworn brother, King Malcolm III
1 CONC  of Scotland. Tostig spent the summer of 1066 in Scotland. He made contact in some way with King Harald III Hardrada of Norway and persuaded him to invade England. One of the sagas claims that he sailed for Norway, and greatly imp
1 CONC ressed the Norwegian King and his court, managing to sway a decidedly unenthusiastic Harald, who had just concluded a long and inconclusive war with Denmark,into raising a levy to take the throne of England. With Hardrada's aid, T
1 CONC ostig sailed up the Humber and defeated Earls Morcar and Edwin at Gate Fulford.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hardrada's army invaded York, taking hostages after a peaceful surrender, and likely agreed with the local inhabitants to gather commandeered supplies at Stamford Bridge, near York, a conveniently central spot, well fed by streams
1 CONC  and roads. It was here that Harold Godwinson, now King of England, racing northward with an English army from London, found Harald and about 6,000 of his men, basking in the sun and awaiting supplies. The Norwegians, and the Flem
1 CONC ish mercenaries hired by Tostig, were largely without armor and carried only personal weapons. The day was very hot and no resistance was expected. The remainder of the 11,000 man force remained guarding the Norse ships, beached m
1 CONC iles away at Riccall. After a brief meeting of the two kings, where Harald refused to surrender, and Tostig to abandon him, a long battle ensued. Despite making a brave stand, and reinforced late in the day by a desperate, sweatin
1 CONC g column from Riccall, the Norwegians suffered a complete and utter defeat. Fewer than twenty of the three hundred Norwegian ships returned home. King Harald of Norway died there, as did Tostig Godwinson.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT On September 25, 1066, King Harold II of England (Tostig's brother) marched his army from the south of England where they were awaiting the Normans up to York and halted the Norwegian invasion at the Battle of Stamford Bridge, in 
1 CONC which Tostig and Harald III were both killed. After the death of Tostig, his two sons took refuge in Norway, while his wife Judith married Duke Welf of Bavaria. It is believed that after Stamford Bridge his body was taken to York 
1 CONC and buried at York Minster.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Trivia
1 CONT In Julian Rathbone's historical novel, "The Last English King", Tostig is depicted as Edward the Confessor's catamite.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Harold: The Last Anglo-Saxon King, by Ian W. Walker, 1997. 
1 CONT ^ Accurate Description of the Cathedral and Metropolical Church of St. Peter, by Francis Drake 1786 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Tostig in Non-Fiction Books
1 CONT Popular (as opposed to scholarly) non-fiction books that cover Tostig's life and role in history include:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1066: The Year of the Conquest (1977) by David Howarth (ISBN 0-88029-014-5) 
1 CONT The Making of the King 1066 (1966) by Alan Lloyd (ISBN 0-88029-473-6) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Tostig in Fiction
1 CONT The Last English King (2000) by Julian Rathbone (ISBN 0-349-11385-8) 
1 CONT Harold, The Last of the Saxon Kings, by Lord Bulworth-Lytton 
1 CONT The King's Shadow, by Elizabeth Alder 
1 CONT
