0 @N6000000003051245489@ NOTE ["The British Monarchy"]
1 CONT Kenneth was the brother of Dubh. In 973 he acknowledged King Edgar of England as his lord in return for recognition that the Scots now held Lothian, which they had seized from the Angles. In about 994, however, he broke his promis
1 CONC e to keep the peace and invaded England. He was defeated, and lost Lothian again. He killed Culen's brother in 977 and was himself killed in 995 in a blood feud at Fettercairn, Kincardineshire by Culen's son, Constantine.
1 CONT
1 CONT [Weis 146] Berchan's "Prophecy" indicates that his wife was a Leinster woman.
1 CONT
1 CONT ["Kenneth II of Scotland." Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia. 3 Jun 2006, 08:19 UTC. Wikimedia Foundation, Inc. 6 Jun 2006 <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Kenneth_II_of_Scotland&oldid=56634830>.]
1 CONT Kenneth II (Cináed mac Maíl Choluim), son of Malcolm I, King of Scots, succeeded Culen, son of Indulf, who had been slain by the Brythons of Strathclyde in 971 in Lothian. He did not established himself as sole King until he kille
1 CONC d Culen's brother Amlaíb in 977, after which he ruled all of Scotland. His strength, like that of the other Kings of his branch of the house of Kenneth MacAlpin lay chiefly north of the Tay.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kenneth II began his reign by ravaging the British kingdom, but he lost a large part of his force on the river Cornag. Soon afterwards he attacked Eadulf, earl of the northern half of Northumbria, and ravaged the whole of his terr
1 CONC itory. He fortified the fords of the Forth as a defence against the Britons and again invaded Northumbria, carrying off the earl's son. About this time he gave the city of Brechin to the church.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the English chroniclers, Kenneth II paid homage to King Edgar for the cession of Lothian, but these statements are probably due to the controversy as to the position of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT The mormaers, or chiefs, of Kenneth II were engaged throughout his reign in a contest with Sigurd the Norwegian, earl of Orkney, for the possession of Caithness and the northern district of Scotland as far south as the Spey. In th
1 CONC is struggle the Scots attained no permanent success.
1 CONT
1 CONT At some point in his life, Kenneth II married, but the details are scanty. His wife was said to have been a Princess of Leinster by the Prophecy of Berchán. Her name, parentage, and fate are unknown. The marriage produced Malcolm 
1 CONC II.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 995 Kenneth II died at Fettercairn in the Mearns. According to some accounts, he was slain treacherously by his own subjects, through the intrigue of Finnguala, daughter of Cuncar, Mormaer of Angus. He was buried on the Isle of
1 CONC  Iona.
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT Anderson, Alan Orr, Early Sources of Scottish History, 1922.
1 CONT Chronicles of the Picts and Scots, ed. W. F. Skene (Edinburgh, 1867)
1 CONT W. F. Skene, Celtic Scotland (Edinburgh, 1876)
0 @N6000000003232714972@ NOTE Alias:<ALIA> Kenneth /of Alba/
1 CONT
1 CONT [Sargent.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT King of Scots 971 -995
1 CONT
1 CONT In an attempt to regain the Straclyde region, and to avenge the defeat of Culen, Kenneth II too was defeated and returned to his own lands with some thinking to do. 20 years went by before Kenneth II even thought about trying the 
1 CONC warfare angle of being king again, and he ruled his kingdom with diplomacy and calm. But this isn't the reason why his reign was somewhat longer than most -around 873 he murdered Culen's brother Olaf before he even had a chance to
1 CONC  try it first.
1 CONT
1 CONT So the peace wasn't what it seemed, and eventually in 994 Kenneth was tempted to hit out at the south again. Unfortunately for him this crusade was a failure and he too had to cut his vacation short in order to come home and clear
1 CONC  up a revolt which was underway in his own land while he was away.
1 CONT
1 CONT Kenneth II's death was somewhat different from the others and more gruesome than usual too. When trying to keep the peace in one of his regions, he slew the son of Finella the wife of that region's controller. Finella, bittered an
1 CONC d saddened by her loss, set a trap for him. She built a room within a tower and lined it with beautiful tapestries, but behind the tapestries she had hidden loaded crossbows which all pointed to a statue placed in the center of th
1 CONC e room. In the hand of the statue was a golden apple, and it was set up so that when the apple was picked -the bows would unleash their bolts. One night whilst entertaining her king at a feast, she led him to this room and offered
1 CONC  the apple as a symbol of their lasting friendship. The unsuspecting King took the apple and was instantly killed in a hail of bolts.
