0 @N6000000002187825152@ NOTE Otto, Count of Ballenstedt
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto, Count of Ballenstedt, called Otto the Rich (died 9 February 1123), was the first Ascanian prince to call himself count of Anhalt, and was also briefly named duke of Saxony. He was the father of Albert the Bear, who later con
1 CONC quered Brandenburg from the Slavs and called himself its first margrave.
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto was the eldest son of Adalbert II, Count of Ballenstedt and Adelheid, daughter of Otto I, Margrave of Meissen. After the death of his father-in-law, Magnus, Duke of Saxony, in 1106, Otto inherited a significant part of Magnus
1 CONC ' properties, and hoped to succeed him as duke. However, Lothar of Supplinburg was named duke in his stead. In 1112, after Lothar had been banned, Otto was appointed duke of Saxony by Emperor Henry V; but in the same year, he came
1 CONC  into a dispute with the emperor and was stripped of his ducal title. He now allied himself with Lothar, and helped Lothar defeat Hoyer I, Count of Mansfeld, who had been named duke of Saxony by the Emperor, in 1115.
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto conquered the areas around Zerbst and Salzwedel from Slavs, and maintained Lothar's support once Lothar became king in 1125. He also claimed the County of Weimar-Orlamünde, of which his mother was the heir.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT Otto married Eilica, daughter of Magnus, Duke of Saxony, before 1095. They had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert the Bear (1100-1170) 
1 CONT Adelheid (died 1139), married Henry IV, Count of Stade, and in 1139, Werner, Count of Osterburg 
0 @N6000000002187825163@ NOTE Eilika of Saxony
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Eilika of Saxony (ca. 1080 – 16 January 1142), younger daughter of Magnus, Duke of Saxony and Sophia (married 1071), daughter of Béla I of Hungary. After the death of her father, she became the heiress of Werben and Palatinate of 
1 CONC Saxony. She was married Count Otto of Ballenstedt and was mother of Albrecht der Bär.
