0 @N6000000001540772168@ NOTE Adela of France, Countess of Flanders
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Adela Capet, Adèle of France or Adela of Flanders[1], known also as Adela the Holy or Adela of Messines; (1009 ? 8 January 1079, Messines) was the second daughter of Robert II (the Pious), and Constance of Arles. As dowry to her f
1 CONC uture husband, she received from his father the title of Countess of Corbie.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Her family
1 CONT 2 Political influence
1 CONT 2.1 Battle of Cassel
1 CONT 3 Church influence
1 CONT 4 Note
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Her family
1 CONT She was a member of the House of Capet, the rulers of France. As the wife of Baldwin V, she was Countess of Flanders from 1036 to 1067.
1 CONT
1 CONT She married first 1027 Richard III Duke of Normandy (997 ? 1027). They never had children. As a widow, she remarried in 1028 in Paris to Baldwin V of Flanders (1012 ? 1067). Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Baldwin VI of Flanders, (1030 ? 1070)
1 CONT Matilda of Flanders (1032 ? 1083). In 1053 she married William Duke of Normandy, the future king of England
1 CONT Robert I of Flanders, (1033-1093)
1 CONT Henry of Flanders (c. 1035)
1 CONT Sir Richard of Flanders (c. 1050-1105)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Political influence
1 CONT Adèle?s influence lay mainly in her family connections. On the death of her brother, Henry I of France, the guardianship of his seven-year-old son Philip I fell jointly on his widow, Ann of Kiev, and on his brother-in-law, Adela's
1 CONC  husband, so that from 1060 to 1067, they were Regents of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Battle of Cassel
1 CONT When Adela's third son, Robert the Frisian, was to invade Flanders in1071 to become the new count (at that time the count was Adela's grandson, Arnulf III), she asked Phillip I to stop him. Phillip sent troops in order to to aid A
1 CONC rnulf, being among the forces sent by the kinga contingent of ten Norman knights led by William FitzOsborn. Robert's forces attacked Arnulf's numerically superior army at Cassel beforeit could organize, and Arnulf himself was kill
1 CONC ed along with William FitzOsborn. The overwhelming triumph of Robert made Phillip invest him with Flanders, making the peace. A year later, Phillip married Robert's step-daughter, Bertha of Holland, and in 1074, Phillip restored t
1 CONC heseigneurie of Corbie to the crown.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Church influence
1 CONT Adèle had an especially great interest in Baldwin V?s church-reform politics and was behind her husband?s founding of several collegiate churches. Directly or indirectly, she was responsible for establishing the Colleges of Aire (
1 CONC 1049), Lille (1050) and Harelbeke (1064) as well as the abbeys of Messines (1057) and Ename (1063). After Baldwin?s death in 1067, she went to Rome, took the nun?s veil from the hands of Pope Alexander II and retreated to the Bene
1 CONC dictine convent of Messines, near Ypres. There she died, being buried at the same monastery. Her commemoration day is 8 September.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Note
1 CONT ^ Other forms of her name are Adela, Adélaïde, Adelheid, Aelis and Alix.
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adela_of_France%2C_Countess_of_Flanders"
