1 CONC g the nunnery at Schweinfurt, he cut the bar of the convent door with his sword and carried off the beautiful Judith of Schweinfurt and made her his bride.    Source: Leo van de Pas
0 @N6000000000437226996@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> Duke Name Suffix:<NSFX> Of Bohemia
0 @N6000000001353965892@ NOTE Bretislav I, byname BRETISLAV THE RESTORER, Czech BRETISLAV OBNOVITEL(b. 1005?--d. Jan. 10, 1055), prince of Bohemia from 1034 to 1055, who permanently attached the province of Moravia to Bohemia.
1 CONT Bretislav succeeded his father, Oldrich, to the Bohemian throne aftera period of dynastic struggles. He proceeded to win back lands that earlier had been lost to Poland, regaining in two great campaigns not only all of Moravia but
1 CONC  also Silesia and Kraków. He also captured Poznan and Gniezno, but his ambitions for a greater West Slav nation troubled the Holy Roman emperor Henry III, who mounted an armed invasion and compelled Bretislav to evacuate all of Po
1 CONC land except Silesia (whichnevertheless was returned to Poland some years later). Bretislav's efforts to regain Slovakia from Hungary were unsuccessful, it did not return to Czech control until 1918).
1 CONT To obviate the incessant struggles that had endangered Bohemia at every vacancy of the throne, Bretislav, with the consent of the nobles, decreed that the oldest member of the house of Premysl should be the ruler of Bohemia. Breti
1 CONC slav was therefore succeeded first by his eldest son, Spytihnev II (1031-61), and then by his second son, Vratislav II
