0 @N6000000002187639856@ NOTE Mieszko II Lambert
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko II Lambert 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign 1025–1034. 
1 CONT Coronation December 25, 1025, in Gniezno Cathedral, Poland. 
1 CONT Royal House Piast. 
1 CONT Coat of Arms The Piast Eagle. 
1 CONT Parents Boleslaw I the Brave,
1 CONT Enmilda. 
1 CONT Consorts Richensa. 
1 CONT Children With Richensa:
1 CONT Boleslaw the Forgotten,
1 CONT Casimir I,
1 CONT Gertruda,
1 CONT ,Adelaide/Rixa of Poland
1 CONT
1 CONT Date of Birth 990. 
1 CONT Place of Birth  ? 
1 CONT Date of Death May 10, 1034. 
1 CONT Place of Death  ? 
1 CONT Place of Burial Cathedral Basilica of St. Peter and St. Paul, Poznan, Poland. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko II Lambert (990-1034), also spelled Miezko II, Mieszislav II was the duke and short-term king of Poland. He was the son of Boleslaw I the Brave and Enmilda, daughter of Dobromir, Duke of Lusatia. Mieszko II was married to 
1 CONC Richensa of Lotharingia (Rixa), the granddaughter of Otto II, Holy Roman Emperor. Their children were Casimir I of Poland, Adelaide/Rixa of Poland, and Gertrude of Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko II was very well educated for the period. He was able to read and write, and knew both Greek and Latin. He is unjustly known as Mieszko Gnusny (the "Lazy," "Stagnant" or "Slothful"). He received that epithet due to the unf
1 CONC ortunate way his reign ended; but at the beginning he acted as a skillful and talented ruler. Before he became king in 1025, he probably served as his father's governor in Cracow, most likely from 1013, and reputedly built many ch
1 CONC urches.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Beginning in 1028, he successfully waged war against Germany: he was able to repel the German army, and later even invaded Saxony. He allied Poland with Hungary, resulting in a temporary Hungarian occupation of Vienna. This war wa
1 CONC s probably prompted by family connections of Mieszko's in Germany who opposed Emperor Conrad II.
1 CONT
1 CONT An understanding of what happened later requires an understanding of Mieszko's family. His older brother Bezprym was the son of an unknown Hungarian wife of Boleslaw's and was later expelled by Mieszko. He also had a younger broth
1 CONC er, Otton. By Slavic custom, a father should divide his legacy among all his sons. However, since a kingdom cannot be divided, Mieszko's brothers received nothing from their father's legacy.
1 CONT
1 CONT As Bezprym was the oldest son, many probably felt that he should have succeeded his father as king. Bezprym had, however, always been disliked by his father, as indicated by his name (the Piasts tended to give names such as Bolesl
1 CONC aw, Mieszko and later Kazimierz, Wladyslaw and emperors' names such as Otto, Conrad and Heinrich: Bezprym was a commoner's name, which implied that Boleslaw did not wish Bezprym to succeed him). He was packed off to a monastery.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mieszko's two brothers escaped abroad: Otton to Germany, Bezprym to Kievan Rus. Soon after, the German emperor and the grand duke of Kiev, Yaroslav I the Wise, made alliance and simultaneously invaded Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Facing two enemies, Germany on the west and Rus on the east, Mieszko escaped to Bohemia.Bezprym began his reign by sending his crown and regalia to Germany. Mieszko soon returned, but was forced to pledge fealty to the German Empe
1 CONC ror, and Poland was divided among him, his brothers Otton and Bezprym, and a certain Thiedric (probably a nephew or cousin). Otton was killed by one of his own men, and Mieszko was able to reunite Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT What happened next is a mystery. Historians now think that Mieszko was killed (1034) in a plot hatched by the aristocracy.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Mieszko's death, Poland's peasants revolted in a "pagan reaction." The exact reasons and date are unknown. Mieszko's son, Casimir I, was either expelled by this insurrection, or the insurrection was caused by the aristocracy
1 CONC 's expulsion of him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Some modern historians argue that the insurrection was caused more by economic than by religious issues, such as new taxes for the Church and the militarization of the early Polish polity. Priests, monks and knights were killed; c
1 CONC ities, churches and monasteries were burned.
1 CONT
1 CONT The chaos became still greater when unexpectedly the Czechs invaded from the south. The land became divided among local rulers, one of whom is known by name: Maslaw, ruler of Masovia. Greater Poland was so devastated that it cease
1 CONC d to be the core of the Polish kingdom. The capital was moved to Cracow in Lesser Poland.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] See also
1 CONT Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700 by Frederick Lewis Weis, Lines: 147-22, 243-6. 
1 CONT History of Poland (966-1385) 
0 @N6000000002322323199@ NOTE Somewhat deranged (he died mad), Mieszko was dominated by his wife, the
1 CONT German Ryxa, and let the achievements of his father, Boleslaw I, crumble.
1 CONT Much territory was lost to Bohemia and to the Holy Roman Empire. At his
1 CONT death, Poland fell into anarchy. The Piast princes lost their title of king,
1 CONT although Boleslaw II held it briefly from 1076-1079, and allowed the
1 CONT authority of the central government to diminish in favor of the power of the
1 CONT regional nobility, engaging in many struggles resulting in a territorial loss.
